#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:162
msgid "Using Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:167
msgid ""
"@dfn{Texinfo} is a documentation system that uses a single source file to "
"produce both online information and printed output.  This means that instead "
"of writing several different documents, one for each output format, you need "
"only write one document."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:174
msgid ""
"Using Texinfo, you can create a printed document (via the @TeX{} typesetting "
"system) in PDF format, including chapters, sections, cross-references, and "
"indices.  From the same Texinfo source file, you can create HTML output "
"suitable for use with a web browser, you can create an Info file for use in "
"GNU Emacs or other Info-reading programs, and also create DocBook, EPUB 3, "
"or @LaTeX{} files."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:175
#, no-wrap
msgid "Source file format"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:176
#, no-wrap
msgid "Semantic markup"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:182
msgid ""
"A Texinfo source file is a plain text file containing text interspersed with "
"@dfn{@@-commands} (words preceded by an @samp{@@}) that tell the Texinfo "
"processors what to do.  Texinfo's markup commands are almost entirely "
"@dfn{semantic}; that is, they specify the intended meaning of text in the "
"document, rather than physical formatting instructions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:189
msgid ""
"Output to most output formats is handled by the @command{texi2any} program.  "
"@xref{Generic Translator @command{texi2any}}.  The exception is when Texinfo "
"code is processed by @TeX{} to DVI or PDF (@pxref{Printed Books}, below).  "
"Emacs also offers commands for converting Texinfo code to Info "
"(@pxref{@code{texinfo-format} commands})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:192
msgid ""
"GNU Emacs has a special mode, called Texinfo mode, that provides various "
"Texinfo-related features. (@xref{Texinfo Mode}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:193
#, no-wrap
msgid "Limited scope of Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:202
msgid ""
"Texinfo was devised specifically for the purpose of writing software "
"documentation and manuals.  If you want to write a good manual for your "
"program, Texinfo has many features which we hope will make your job easier.  "
"However, Texinfo is not intended to be a general-purpose formatting "
"program.  It provides almost no commands for controlling the final "
"formatting, so may be inappropriate for your needs if you want to lay out a "
"newspaper, devise a glossy magazine ad, or follow the exact formatting "
"requirements of a publishing house."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:203
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spelling of Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:204
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pronunciation of Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:212
msgid ""
"Spell ``Texinfo'' with a capital ``T'' and the other letters in lowercase.  "
"The first syllable of ``Texinfo'' is pronounced like ``speck'', not "
"``hex''.  This odd pronunciation is derived from the pronunciation of "
"@TeX{}.  Pronounce @TeX{} as if the @samp{X} were the last sound in the name "
"`Bach'.  In the word @TeX{}, the @samp{X} is, rather than the English letter "
"``ex'', actually the Greek letter ``chi''."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:216
msgid ""
"Texinfo is the official documentation format of the GNU project.  More "
"information, including manuals for GNU packages, is available at the "
"@uref{https://www.gnu.org/doc/, GNU documentation web page}."
msgstr ""

