#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:268
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:269 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:801
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:277
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2any}.)  Info format is mostly a plain text "
"transliteration of the Texinfo source.  It adds a few control characters to "
"provide navigational information for cross-references, indices, and so on.  "
"The Emacs Info subsystem (@pxref{Top,,, info, Info}), and the standalone "
"@command{info} program (@pxref{Top,,, info-stnd, GNU Info}), among others, "
"can read these files.  @xref{Info Files}, and @ref{Creating and Installing "
"Info Files}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:278
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plain text output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:279
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plain text"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:283
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2any --plaintext}.)  This is almost the same as "
"Info output with the navigational control characters omitted."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:284
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML output @subentry overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:285 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:807
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:300
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2any --html}.)  HTML, standing for Hyper Text "
"Markup Language, is the language for writing documents on the World Wide "
"Web.  Web browsers can render this language online.  There are many versions "
"of HTML, both different standards and browser-specific variations.  "
"@command{texi2any} uses a subset of the language that can be interpreted by "
"any common browser, intentionally not using many newer or less widely-"
"supported tags.  Although the native output is thus rather plain, it can be "
"customized at various levels, if desired.  @xref{Generating HTML}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:301
#, no-wrap
msgid "EPUB 3 output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:302
#, no-wrap
msgid "EPUB 3"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:309
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2any --epub3}.)  EPUB is a format designed for "
"reading electronic books on portable devices.  It is a derivative of HTML@.  "
"The format was developed by the International Digital Publishing Forum "
"(IDPF), which is now part of the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C)@.  The "
"latest major revision, EPUB 3, dates from 2011."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:311
#, no-wrap
msgid "DVI output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:312
#, no-wrap
msgid "dvips @r{(program)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:313
#, no-wrap
msgid "xdvi"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:314
#, no-wrap
msgid "DVI"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:325
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2dvi}.)  The DeVIce Independent binary format is "
"output by the @TeX{} typesetting program (@uref{http://tug.org}).  This is "
"then read by a DVI `driver', which knows the actual device-specific commands "
"that can be viewed or printed, notably Dvips for translation to PostScript "
"(@pxref{Top,,, dvips, Dvips}) and Xdvi for viewing on an X display "
"(@uref{http://sourceforge.net/projects/xdvi/}).  @xref{Hardcopy with "
"@TeX{}}.  (Be aware that the Texinfo language is very different from "
"@TeX{}'s usual languages: plain @TeX{}, @LaTeX{}, Con@TeX{}t, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:326
#, no-wrap
msgid "PostScript output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:327
#, no-wrap
msgid "PostScript"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:334
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2dvi --ps}.)  PostScript is a page description "
"language that became widely used around 1985 and is still used today.  "
"@uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PostScript} gives a basic description "
"and more preferences.  By default, Texinfo uses the @command{dvips} program "
"to convert @TeX{}'s DVI output to PostScript.  @xref{Top,,, dvips, Dvips}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:335
#, no-wrap
msgid "PDF output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:336
#, no-wrap
msgid "Beebe, Nelson"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:337
#, no-wrap
msgid "PDF"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:349
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2dvi --pdf} or @command{texi2pdf}.)  This format "
"was developed by Adobe Systems for portable document interchange, based on "
"their previous PostScript language.  It can represent the exact appearance "
"of a document, including fonts and graphics, and supporting arbitrary "
"scaling.  It is intended to be platform-independent and easily viewable, "
"among other design goals; @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Portable_Document_Format} and @uref{http://tug.org/TUGboat/tb22-3/tb72beebe-"
"pdf.pdf} have some background.  By default, Texinfo uses the "
"@command{pdftex} program, an extension of @TeX{}, to output PDF; see "
"@uref{http://tug.org/applications/pdftex}.  @xref{PDF Output}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:350
#, no-wrap
msgid "LaTeX output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:351
#, no-wrap
msgid "@LaTeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:359
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2any --latex}.)  This is a typesetting system "
"built on top of @TeX{}.  It was originally released by Leslie Lamport in "
"1984.  @LaTeX{} adds more definitions to those of @TeX{} and has a wide "
"range of packages built on it. @LaTeX{} is ubiquitous in academic "
"literature. The current version of @LaTeX{} is under active development; "
"more information is available online at @uref{https://www.latex-"
"project.org/}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:364
msgid ""
"The @LaTeX{} output can be further processed into DVI, PostScript, or PDF@.  "
"In theory, the @LaTeX{} output should allow for much more customizability of "
"the output than would be possible with the plain @TeX{} implementation of "
"Texinfo."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:366
#, no-wrap
msgid "DocBook output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:367
#, no-wrap
msgid "XML DocBook output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:368 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:816
#, no-wrap
msgid "DocBook"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:375
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2any --docbook}.)  This is an XML-based format, "
"primarily for technical documentation.  It therefore bears some resemblance, "
"in broad outline, to Texinfo.  See @uref{http://www.docbook.org}.  Various "
"converters from DocBook @emph{to} Texinfo have also been developed; see the "
"Texinfo web pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:376
#, no-wrap
msgid "XML Texinfo output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:377
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo XML output, overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:378
#, no-wrap
msgid "DTD, for Texinfo XML"
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:379
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo.dtd"
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:380
#, no-wrap
msgid "txixml2texi"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:381 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:812
#, no-wrap
msgid "XML"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:387
msgid ""
"(Generated via @command{texi2any --xml}.)  The @command{texi2any} XML "
"output, unlike all the other output formats, is a transliteration of the "
"Texinfo source, rather than finished output.  Texinfo XML files cannot be "
"viewed in web browsers or other programs."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:394
msgid ""
"XML is a generic syntax specification usable for any sort of content.  (A "
"reference is at @uref{http://www.w3.org/XML}.)  The purpose of the Texinfo "
"XML output is to allow further processing by XML tools.  The output syntax "
"is defined in an XML DTD, which is contained in a file @file{texinfo.dtd} "
"included in the Texinfo source distribution."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:399
msgid ""
"The Texinfo source distribution includes a utility script @file{txixml2texi} "
"to do a backward transformation to recreate the original Texinfo content "
"(except for Texinfo macros and conditionals)."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:403 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:405
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:405
msgid "@nodedescription What is an Info file?"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:406
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:412
msgid ""
"As mentioned above, Info format is mostly a plain text transliteration of "
"the Texinfo source, with the addition of a few control characters to "
"separate nodes and provide navigational information, so that Info-reading "
"programs can operate on it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:417
msgid ""
"Info files are nearly always created by processing a Texinfo source "
"document.  @command{texi2any}, also known as @command{makeinfo}, is the "
"principal command that converts a Texinfo file into an Info file; "
"@pxref{Generic Translator @command{texi2any}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:427
msgid ""
"Generally, you enter an Info file through a node that by convention is named "
"`Top'.  This node normally contains just a brief summary of the file's "
"purpose, and a large menu through which the rest of the file is reached.  "
"From this node, you can either traverse the file systematically by going "
"from node to node, or you can go to a specific node listed in the main menu, "
"or you can search the index menus and then go directly to the node that has "
"the information you want.  Alternatively, with the standalone Info program, "
"you can specify specific menu items on the command line (@pxref{Top,,, info, "
"Info})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:432
msgid ""
"If you want to read through an Info file in sequence, as if it were a "
"printed manual, you can hit @key{SPC} repeatedly, or you get the whole file "
"with the advanced Info command @kbd{g *}.  (@xref{Advanced,, Advanced Info "
"commands, info, Info}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:436
msgid ""
"The @file{dir} file in the @file{info} directory serves as the departure "
"point for the whole Info system.  From it, you can reach the `Top' nodes of "
"each of the documents in a complete Info system."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:437
#, no-wrap
msgid "URI syntax for Info"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:441
msgid ""
"If you wish to refer to an Info file via a URI, you can use the (unofficial) "
"syntax exemplified by the following.  This works with Emacs/W3, for example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:445
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"info:emacs#Dissociated%20Press\n"
"info:///usr/info/emacs#Dissociated%20Press\n"
"info://localhost/usr/info/emacs#Dissociated%20Press\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:448
msgid "The @command{info} program itself does not follow URIs of any kind."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:450 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:452
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printed Books"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:452
msgid "@nodedescription Characteristics of a printed book or manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:453
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printed book and manual characteristics"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:454
#, no-wrap
msgid "Manual characteristics, printed"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:455
#, no-wrap
msgid "Book characteristics, printed"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:456
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo printed book characteristics"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:457
#, no-wrap
msgid "Characteristics, printed books or manuals"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:459
#, no-wrap
msgid "Knuth, Donald"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:464
msgid ""
"A Texinfo file can be formatted and typeset as a printed book or manual.  To "
"do this, you need @TeX{}, a sophisticated typesetting program written by "
"Donald Knuth of Stanford University.  It is not part of the Texinfo "
"distribution."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:469
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides a file @file{texinfo.tex} that contains the definitions "
"that @TeX{} uses when it typesets a Texinfo file.  You can get the latest "
"version of @file{texinfo.tex} from the Texinfo home page, @uref{https://"
"www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:474
msgid ""
"A Texinfo-based book is similar to any other typeset, printed work: it can "
"have a title page, copyright page, table of contents, and preface, as well "
"as chapters, numbered or unnumbered sections and subsections, page headers, "
"cross-references, footnotes, and indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:479
msgid ""
"@TeX{} is very powerful and has a great many features.  However, because a "
"Texinfo file must be able to present information both on a character-only "
"terminal in Info form and in a typeset book, the formatting commands that "
"Texinfo supports are necessarily limited."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:482
msgid ""
"@xref{Hardcopy with @TeX{}}, for more information on processing a manual "
"with @TeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:484 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:486
#, no-wrap
msgid "History"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:486
msgid "@nodedescription Acknowledgements, contributors and genesis."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:488
#, no-wrap
msgid "Stallman, Richard M."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:489
#, no-wrap
msgid "Chassell, Robert J."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:490
#, no-wrap
msgid "Fox, Brian"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:491
#, no-wrap
msgid "Berry, Karl"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:492
#, no-wrap
msgid "Smith, Gavin"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:500
msgid ""
"Richard M. Stallman invented the Texinfo format, wrote the initial "
"processors, and created Edition 1.0 of this manual.  Robert@tie{}J.  "
"Chassell greatly revised and extended the manual, starting with Edition "
"1.1.  Brian Fox was responsible for the standalone Texinfo distribution "
"until version 3.8.  Karl Berry continued maintenance from Texinfo 3.8 "
"(manual edition 2.22), and Gavin Smith has continued maintenance since "
"Texinfo 6.0."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:501
#, no-wrap
msgid "History of Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:502
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo history"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:503
#, no-wrap
msgid "Beginnings"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:505
#, no-wrap
msgid "NLS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:510
msgid ""
"Richard Stallman included an on-line, hypertext help system called Info in "
"the original implementation of Emacs (in 1975/6).  Stallman had been "
"inspired after seeing a presentation a couple of years earlier on the "
"``NLS'' hypertext system of Douglas Engelbart."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:534
#, no-wrap
msgid "Scribe"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:535
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reid, Brian"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:541
msgid ""
"In another development, in the 1970's at CMU, Brian Reid developed a program "
"and format named Scribe to mark up documents for printing.  It used the "
"@code{@@} character to introduce commands, as Texinfo does.  Much more "
"consequentially, it strove to describe document contents rather than "
"formatting, an idea wholeheartedly adopted by Texinfo."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:542
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bolio"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:543
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bo@TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:548
msgid ""
"Meanwhile, people at MIT developed another format called Bolio.  Richard "
"Stallman (RMS) worked on converting Bolio to use @TeX{} as its typesetting "
"language, resulting in Bo@TeX{}.  The earliest Bo@TeX{} version seems to "
"have been 0.02 on October 31, 1984."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:553
msgid ""
"Bo@TeX{} could only be used as a markup language for documents to be "
"printed, not for online documents.  RMS combined Bo@TeX{} and Info to create "
"Texinfo, a mark-up language for text that is intended to be read both online "
"and as printed hard copy."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:559
msgid ""
"The original translator to create Info was written (primarily by RMS and Bob "
"Chassell) in Emacs Lisp, namely the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} and other "
"functions.  In the early 1990s, Brian Fox reimplemented the conversion "
"program in C, now called @command{makeinfo}, as well as the standalone "
"@command{info} program."
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:560
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reimplementing in Perl"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:562 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:16698
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cons, Lionel"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:563 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:17964
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dumas, Patrice"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:571
msgid ""
"In 2012, the C @command{makeinfo} was itself replaced by a Perl "
"implementation generically called @command{texi2any}.  This version was "
"initially based on @command{texi2html}, an independent program originally "
"written by Lionel Cons, later with substantial work by many others.  The "
"features needed to make @command{texi2html} a replacement for "
"@command{makeinfo} were implemented by Patrice Dumas.  That program, "
"however, was never released."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:572
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tree representation of documents"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:573
#, no-wrap
msgid "Syntax tree representation of documents"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:574
#, no-wrap
msgid "Abstract syntax tree representation of documents"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:581
msgid ""
"The current implementation (also written by Patrice Dumas) inherited the "
"design of customization and other features from @command{texi2html} (for "
"more on @command{texi2html} compatibility, @pxref{@command{texi2html}}).  "
"However, @command{texi2any} was a full reimplementation: unlike "
"@command{texi2html}, it constructs a tree-based representation of the input "
"document for all back-ends to work from."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:584
msgid ""
"The new Perl program was intended to have the following advantages over the "
"old C program:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:586
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML customization.  Many GNU and other free software packages"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:593
msgid ""
"had been happily using the HTML customization features in "
"@command{texi2html} for years.  Thus, in effect two independent "
"implementations of the Texinfo language had developed, and keeping them in "
"sync was not simple.  Adding the HTML customization possible in "
"@command{texi2html} to a C program would have been an enormous effort at "
"that time."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:594
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unicode, and multilingual support generally, especially of east"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:597
msgid ""
"Asian languages.  At that time, doing it in C would have been tantamount to "
"rewriting the entire program."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:598
#, no-wrap
msgid "Additional back-ends.  The @command{makeinfo} code had become"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:605
msgid ""
"convoluted to the point where adding a new back-end was quite complex, "
"requiring complex interactions with existing back-ends.  In contrast, the "
"new implementation provides a clean tree-based representation for all back-"
"ends to work from.  People had requested numerous different back-ends "
"(@LaTeX{}, the latest (X)HTML, @dots{}); this change made them much more "
"feasible to implement."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:606
#, no-wrap
msgid "@command{texi2any}, as reference implementation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:607
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reference implementation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:608
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implementation, @command{texi2any} as reference"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:610
msgid "Reference Implementation"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:610
#, no-wrap
msgid "@command{texi2any} was intended to be a reference implementation"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:614
msgid ""
"that defines parts of the language not fully specified by the manual, and to "
"provide a reference for command-line options for any other program that "
"might process Texinfo in the future."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:617
#, no-wrap
msgid "Future of Texinfo implementations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:626
msgid ""
"At the time, the new Perl program was much slower than the old C program.  "
"Since then, the parser, part of the Info converter and the HTML converter as "
"a whole have been translated to C, leading to important gains in speed "
"compared to the pure Perl implementation of @command{texi2any}.  The main "
"program has also been translated to C.  The @command{texi2any} C "
"implementation embeds a Perl interpreter for the parts that require Perl."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:628 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:630
#, no-wrap
msgid "Adding Output Formats"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:630
msgid "@nodedescription Man pages and implementing new formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:631
#, no-wrap
msgid "Additional output formats"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:635
msgid ""
"The output formats in the previous sections handle a wide variety of usage, "
"but of course there is always room for more."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:636
#, no-wrap
msgid "Output formats, supporting more"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:648
msgid ""
"If you are a programmer and would like to contribute to the GNU project by "
"implementing additional output formats for Texinfo, that would be "
"excellent.  The way to do this that would be most useful is to write a new "
"back-end for @command{texi2any}, our reference implementation of a Texinfo "
"parser; it creates a tree representation of the Texinfo input that you can "
"use for the conversion.  The documentation in the source file @file{tta/perl/"
"Texinfo/Convert/Converter.pm} is a good place to start if you are willing to "
"develop in Perl "
"(@pxref{Texinfo::Convert::Converter,,,texi2any_internals,Texinfo modules "
"documentation}).  @xref{Generic Translator @command{texi2any}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:649
#, no-wrap
msgid "Output formats, with Texinfo XML as input"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:650
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo XML output, as input"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:655
msgid ""
"Another viable approach is use the Texinfo XML output from "
"@command{texi2any} as your input.  This XML is an essentially complete "
"representation of the input, but without the Texinfo syntax and option "
"peculiarities, as described above."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:656
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo parsers, discouraging more"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:662
msgid ""
"If you still cannot resist the temptation of writing a new program that "
"reads Texinfo source directly, let us give some more caveats: please do not "
"underestimate the amount of work required.  Texinfo is by no means a simple "
"language to parse correctly, and remains under development, so you would be "
"committing to an ongoing task."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:663
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo language tests"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:664
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tests, of Texinfo language"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:669
msgid ""
"Extensive tests of the language and processor are developed with "
"@command{texi2any}; we encourage anyone thinking of writing a program to "
"process Texinfo to use these test cases and verify that they give correct "
"results with your new program."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:670
#, no-wrap
msgid "Man page output, not supported"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:680
msgid ""
"From time to time, proposals are made to generate traditional Unix man pages "
"from Texinfo source.  However, because man pages have a strict conventional "
"format, creating a good man page requires a completely different source from "
"that needed for the typical Texinfo applications of writing a good user "
"tutorial and/or a good reference manual.  This makes generating man pages "
"incompatible with the Texinfo design goal of not having to document the same "
"information in different ways for different output formats.  You might as "
"well write the man page directly."
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:681
#, no-wrap
msgid "help2man"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:682
#, no-wrap
msgid "O'Dea, Brendan"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:689
msgid ""
"As an alternative way to support man pages, you may find the program "
"@command{help2man} to be useful.  It generates a traditional man page from "
"the @samp{--help} output of a program.  In fact, the man pages for the "
"programs in the Texinfo distribution are generated with this.  It is GNU "
"software written by Brendan O'Dea, available from @uref{https://www.gnu.org/"
"software/help2man}."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:691 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:694
#, no-wrap
msgid "Writing a Texinfo File"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:694
msgid "Beginning and Ending a File"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:694
msgid "@nodedescription Format of a Texinfo source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:698
msgid ""
"This chapter describes Texinfo syntax and what is required in a Texinfo "
"file, and gives a short sample file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:700
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conventions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:702
msgid "@nodedescription General rules for writing a Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:702
#, no-wrap
msgid "General Syntactic Conventions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:703
#, no-wrap
msgid "General syntactic conventions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:704
#, no-wrap
msgid "Syntactic conventions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:705
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conventions, syntactic"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:706
#, no-wrap
msgid "Characters, basic input"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:708
msgid "Formatting Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:710
msgid ""
"This section describes the general conventions used in all Texinfo documents."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:713
#, no-wrap
msgid "Source files, characters used"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:721
msgid ""
"All printable ASCII characters except @samp{@@}, @samp{@{} and @samp{@}} can "
"appear in a Texinfo file and stand for themselves.  @samp{@@} is the escape "
"character which introduces commands, while @samp{@{} and @samp{@}} are used "
"to surround arguments to certain commands.  To put one of these special "
"characters into the document, put an @samp{@@} character in front of it, "
"like this: @samp{@@@@}, @samp{@@@{}, and @samp{@@@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:723
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{at} @@-commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:724
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:729
msgid ""
"In a Texinfo file, the commands you write to describe the contents of the "
"manual are preceded by an @samp{@@} character; they are called @dfn{@@-"
"commands}.  (The @samp{@@} in Texinfo has the same meaning that @samp{\\} "
"has in plain @TeX{}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:730
#, no-wrap
msgid "Braces, when to use"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:737
msgid ""
"Depending on what they do or what arguments they take, you need to write @@-"
"commands on lines of their own, or as part of sentences.  As a general rule, "
"a command requires braces if it mingles among other text; but it does not "
"need braces if it is on a line of its own.  For more details of Texinfo "
"command syntax, see @ref{Command Syntax}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:743
msgid ""
"Whitespace following an @@-command name is optional and (usually)  ignored "
"if present.  The exceptions are contexts when whitespace is significant, "
"e.g., an @code{@@example} environment."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:748
msgid ""
"Texinfo supports the usual quotation marks used in English and in other "
"languages; see @ref{Inserting Quotation Marks}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:750
#, no-wrap
msgid "Multiple dashes in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:751
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dashes in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:752
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hyphens in source, two or three in a row"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:753
#, no-wrap
msgid "Em dash, producing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:754
#, no-wrap
msgid "En dash, producing"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:764
msgid ""
"Use three hyphens in a row, @samp{---}, to produce a long dash---like this "
"(called an @dfn{em dash}), used for punctuation in sentences.  Use two "
"hyphens, @samp{--}, to produce a medium dash (called an @dfn{en dash}), used "
"primarily for numeric ranges, as in ``June 25--26''.  Use a single hyphen, "
"@samp{-}, to produce a standard hyphen used in compound words.  For display "
"on the screen, Info reduces three hyphens to two and two hyphens to one (not "
"transitively!).  Of course, any number of hyphens in the source remain as "
"they are in literal contexts, such as @code{@@code} and @code{@@example}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:772
msgid ""
"@emph{Whitespace}.  Texinfo files are plain text files composed of lines "
"terminated by the usual newline character (line feed).  Texinfo processors "
"read input one line at a time.  Paragraphs are terminated by an empty line "
"or a line containing only spaces. A sequence of several spaces in text is "
"usually treated the same as a single space (except in verbatim modes)."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:773
#, no-wrap
msgid "Form feed character"
msgstr ""

#. type: kbd{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:774
#, no-wrap
msgid "CTRL-l"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:783
msgid ""
"Form feed (@kbd{CTRL-l}) characters terminate any open paragraph.  Other "
"form feeds, as well other ASCII whitespace (tab, carriage return)  are "
"treated the same as space characters (although the results may differ "
"depending on the output format).  Hence, there is not much point in using "
"these whitespace characters in documents.  Non-ASCII spaces, such as Unicode "
"``em space'', are not recognized as whitespace at all, and will be treated "
"as regular, non-whitespace characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:786
msgid ""
"However, in verbatim modes, for example in code samples, tab characters may "
"produce the correct formatting in the output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:792
#, no-wrap
msgid "PDF/DVI"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:794
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Ignored between paragraphs.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:795 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9688
#, no-wrap
msgid "LaTeX"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:800
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Ignored in contexts where spaces are ignored except after specific\n"
"commands closing braces (@code{@@caption} for example).  Left as is\n"
"in the output otherwise.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:806
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Ignored in contexts where spaces are ignored except after specific\n"
"commands closing braces (@code{@@caption} for example).  In other\n"
"contexts, they may be treated as regular spaces (and thus consolidated\n"
"with surrounding whitespace).\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:811
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Written as a numeric entity except contexts where spaces are ignored;\n"
"for example, in @samp{@@footnote@{ ^L foo@}}, the form feed is\n"
"ignored.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:815
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Keep them everywhere; in attributes, escaped as @samp{\\f}; also,\n"
"@samp{\\} is escaped as @samp{\\\\} and newline as @samp{\\n}.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:818
#, no-wrap
msgid "Completely removed, as they are not allowed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:827 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:829
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:831
#, no-wrap
msgid "Comments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:829
msgid "@nodedescription Writing comments and ignored text in general."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:832
#, no-wrap
msgid "comment"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:833
#, no-wrap
msgid "c"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:843
msgid ""
"You can write comments in a Texinfo file by using the @code{@@comment} "
"command, which may be abbreviated to @code{@@c}.  Such comments are for a "
"person looking at the Texinfo source file.  All the text on a line that "
"follows either @code{@@comment} or @code{@@c} is a comment; the rest of the "
"line does not appear in the visible output.  (To be precise, the character "
"after the @code{@@c} or @code{@@comment} must be something other than a dash "
"or alphanumeric, or it will be taken as part of the command.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:849
msgid ""
"Often, you can write the @code{@@comment} or @code{@@c} in the middle of a "
"line, and only the text that follows after the @code{@@comment} or "
"@code{@@c} command does not appear; but some commands, such as "
"@code{@@settitle}, work on a whole line.  You cannot use @code{@@comment} or "
"@code{@@c} within a line beginning with such a command."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:850
#, no-wrap
msgid "DEL @r{(comment character)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:851
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catcode for comments in @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:859
msgid ""
"In cases of nested command invocations, complicated macro definitions, etc., "
"@code{@@c} and @code{@@comment} may provoke an error when processing with "
"@TeX{}.  Therefore, you can also use the @kbd{DEL} character (ASCII 127 "
"decimal, 0x7f hex, 0177 octal) as a true @TeX{} comment character (catcode "
"14, in @TeX{} internals).  Everything on the line after the @kbd{DEL} will "
"be ignored and the next line will be merged."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:860
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ignored text"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:861
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unprocessed text"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:862
#, no-wrap
msgid "ignore"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:870
msgid ""
"You can also have long stretches of text ignored by the Texinfo processors "
"with the @code{@@ignore} and @code{@@end ignore} commands.  Write each of "
"these commands on a line of its own, starting each command at the beginning "
"of the line.  Text between these two commands does not appear in the "
"processed output.  You can use @code{@@ignore} and @code{@@end ignore} for "
"writing comments.  (For some caveats regarding nesting of such commands, "
"@pxref{Conditional Nesting}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:872
#, no-wrap
msgid "Minimum"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:874
msgid "@nodedescription What a Texinfo file must have."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:874
#, no-wrap
msgid "What a Texinfo File Must Have"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:875
#, no-wrap
msgid "Minimal Texinfo file (requirements)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:876
#, no-wrap
msgid "Required in Texinfo file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:877
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo file minimum"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:885
msgid ""
"By convention, the name of a Texinfo file ends with one of the extensions "
"@file{.texi}, @file{.texinfo}, @file{.txi}, or @file{.tex}.  Using "
"@file{.tex} is discouraged as this extension is already used by @TeX{} and "
"@LaTeX{} input files.  The most common and recommended extension is "
"@file{.texi}.  The name of a Texinfo file should only contain ASCII "
"characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:895
msgid ""
"The output name is based on the input file name, in the default case.  "
"First, any of the extensions @file{.texi}, @file{.tex}, @file{.txi}, or "
"@file{.texinfo} is removed from the input file name; then, the output format "
"specific extension is added---@file{.html} when generating HTML, "
"@file{.info} when generating Info, etc.  The output name should only contain "
"ASCII characters@footnote{@command{texi2any} can handle non ASCII characters "
"in input file names, but non ASCII characters in output name will create "
"problems for some output formats, especially for cross-references.}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:896
#, no-wrap
msgid "Requirements for formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:897
#, no-wrap
msgid "Minimal requirements for formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:898
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:901
msgid ""
"In order to be made into a printed manual, a Texinfo file must begin with a "
"line like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:911
msgid ""
"The contents of the file follow this beginning, and then you must end the "
"Texinfo source with a line like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:914 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1005
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2240
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@bye\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:916
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\input @r{(raw @TeX{} startup)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:922
msgid ""
"The @code{@@bye} line at the end of the file on a line of its own tells "
"@TeX{} that the file is ended and to stop formatting.  If you leave this "
"out, you'll be dumped at @TeX{}'s prompt at the end of the run."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:928
msgid ""
"Furthermore, you will usually provide a Texinfo file with a title, a title "
"page, indices, and the like, all of which are explained in this manual.  But "
"the minimum, which can be useful for short documents, is just the one line "
"at the beginning and the one line at the end."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:932
msgid ""
"Without additional information, the input and output encodings are assumed "
"to be UTF-8, an universal codeset compatible with 7-bit ASCII."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:934
#, no-wrap
msgid "Short Sample Texinfo File"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:938
msgid "@nodedescription A short sample Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:938
#, no-wrap
msgid "Short Sample"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:938
msgid "Sample Beginning"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:939
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sample Texinfo file, no comments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:942
msgid "Here is a short sample Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:946
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@settitle Sample Manual 1.0\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:949
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@copying\n"
"This is a short example of a complete Texinfo file.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:956
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Copyright @@copyright@{@} 2026 Free Software Foundation, Inc.\n"
"@@end copying\n"
"@@documentinfo\n"
"@@title Sample Title\n"
"@@end documentinfo\n"
"@@maketitle\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:958
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@contents\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:961
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Top\n"
"@@top GNU Sample\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:964
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This manual is for GNU Sample\n"
"(version @@value@{VERSION@}, @@value@{UPDATED@}).\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:968
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node First Chapter\n"
"@@nodedescription The first chapter is the only chapter in this sample.\n"
"@@chapter First Chapter\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:972 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2159
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@cindex chapter, first\n"
"This is the first chapter.\n"
"@@cindex index entry, another\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:975 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2161
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here is a numbered list.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:979 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2165
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@enumerate\n"
"@@item\n"
"This is the first item.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:983
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@item\n"
"This is the second item.\n"
"@@end enumerate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:988 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2173
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node First Section\n"
"@@section First Section\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:990 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2175
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First section of first chapter.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:994 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2179
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Second Section\n"
"@@section Second Section\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:996
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Second section of first chapter.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1001
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Index\n"
"@@nodedescription Complete Index\n"
"@@unnumbered Index\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1003 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2238
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@printindex cp\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1008 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1010
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo File Header"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1010
msgid "@nodedescription The first lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1011
#, no-wrap
msgid "Header for Texinfo files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1012
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo file header"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1015
msgid "Texinfo files start with the line:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1024
msgid ""
"The @samp{\\input texinfo} line tells @TeX{} to use the @file{texinfo.tex} "
"file, which tells @TeX{} how to translate the Texinfo @@-commands into "
"@TeX{} typesetting commands.  (Note the use of the backslash, @samp{\\}; "
"this is correct for @TeX{}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1028
msgid ""
"It makes sense to include any command that affects document formatting as a "
"whole in the header.  The @code{@@settitle} line is usually present at the "
"beginning of the header:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1031
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@settitle Sample Manual 1.0\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1038
msgid ""
"The @code{@@settitle} line specifies a title for the page headers (or "
"footers) of the printed manual, and the default title and document "
"description for the @samp{<head>} in HTML@.  @code{@@synindex} "
"(@pxref{@code{@@synindex}}), for instance, is another command often included "
"in the header."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1047
msgid ""
"The start of the Texinfo file up to the first content that is output as part "
"of the main body of the document is the @dfn{preamble}.  It includes the "
"header, @ref{Document Permissions} and @ref{Titlepage & Copyright Page} "
"specification.  It is important for the @LaTeX{} output format as the end of "
"preamble is where the @code{\\begin@{document@}} line is output.  In other "
"output formats it may be used to determine how some special output is "
"formatted, for example @ref{@code{@@copying}} output as a comment at the "
"beginning of output files, or the language used in file headers."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1050
#, no-wrap
msgid "First Line"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1051
#, no-wrap
msgid "The First Line of a Texinfo File"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1053
msgid "@nodedescription The first line of a Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1053
#, no-wrap
msgid "First line of a Texinfo file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1054
#, no-wrap
msgid "Beginning line of a Texinfo file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1055
#, no-wrap
msgid "Header of a Texinfo file"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1059
msgid ""
"Every Texinfo file that is to be the top-level input to @TeX{} must begin "
"with a line that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1073
msgid ""
"When the file is processed by @TeX{}, the @samp{\\input texinfo} command "
"tells @TeX{} to load the macros needed for processing a Texinfo file.  These "
"are in a file called @file{texinfo.tex}, which should have been installed on "
"your system along with either the @TeX{} or Texinfo software.  @TeX{} uses "
"the backslash, @samp{\\}, to mark the beginning of a command, exactly as "
"Texinfo uses @samp{@@}.  The @file{texinfo.tex} file causes the switch from "
"@samp{\\} to @samp{@@}; before the switch occurs, @TeX{} requires @samp{\\}, "
"which is why it appears at the beginning of the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1076
msgid ""
"You may optionally follow this line with a comment to tell GNU Emacs to use "
"Texinfo mode when the file is edited:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1084
msgid ""
"This may be useful when Emacs doesn't detect the file type from the file "
"extension automatically."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1086
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@setfilename}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1088
msgid "@nodedescription Give the name of the output file."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1088
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@setfilename}: Set the Output File Name"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1095
#, no-wrap
msgid "setfilename"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1095
msgid "Setting the Output File Name"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1096
#, no-wrap
msgid "output file name"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1104
msgid ""
"The @code{@@setfilename} line specifies the name of the output file to be "
"generated by @command{texi2any}.  This command is ignored for @TeX{} "
"formatting.  When present, it should be the first Texinfo command (that is, "
"after @samp{\\input texinfo}).  Write the @code{@@setfilename} command at "
"the beginning of a line and follow it on the same line by the Info file name."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1107
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setfilename @var{info-file-name}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1115
msgid ""
"The name must be different from the name of the Texinfo file.  There are two "
"conventions for choosing the name: you can either remove the extension (such "
"as @samp{.texi}) entirely from the input file name, or (recommended) replace "
"it with the @samp{.info} extension.  It is not advised to base the "
"@code{@@setfilename} name on a entirely different name than the input file "
"name."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1116
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ignored before @code{@@setfilename}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1117
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{\\input} source line ignored"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1122
msgid ""
"When a @code{@@setfilename} line is present, the Texinfo processors ignore "
"everything written before the @code{@@setfilename} line.  This is why the "
"very first line of the file (the @code{\\input} line) does not show up in "
"the output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1127
msgid ""
"If there is no @code{@@setfilename} line, @command{texi2any} uses the input "
"file name to determine the output name (@pxref{Minimum}).  The "
"@code{\\input} line is still ignored in this processing, as well as leading "
"blank lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1132
msgid ""
"When producing another output format, @command{texi2any} will replace any "
"final extension with the output format-specific extension (@samp{html} when "
"generating HTML, for example), or add a dot followed by the extension "
"(@samp{.html} for HTML) if the given name has no extension."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1136
msgid ""
"@@-commands are not allowed in @code{@@setfilename}, except for @code{@@@@}, "
"@code{@@@{}, @code{@@@}} and associated @@-commands such as "
"@code{@@atchar@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1145
msgid ""
"@code{@@setfilename} used to be required by the Texinfo processors and some "
"other programs.  This should not be the case any more; @code{@@setfilename} "
"can be omitted.  If the Texinfo input is processed from standard input, "
"without an input file name to deduce the base file name from, "
"@code{@@setfilename} could still be relevant.  This is not the only way, "
"however: @option{--output} option specifies the output file name on the "
"@command{texi2any} command-line (@pxref{Invoking @command{texi2any}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1146
#, no-wrap
msgid "Length of file names"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1147
#, no-wrap
msgid "File name collision"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1148
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info file name, choosing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1161
msgid ""
"Although an explicit @samp{.info} extension is preferable, some operating "
"systems cannot handle long file names.  You can run into a problem even when "
"the file name you specify is itself short enough.  This occurs because the "
"Info formatters split a long Info file into short indirect subfiles, and "
"name them by appending @samp{-1}, @samp{-2}, @dots{}, @samp{-10}, "
"@samp{-11}, and so on, to the original file name.  (@xref{Tag and Split "
"Files}.)  The subfile name @file{texinfo.info-10}, for example, is too long "
"for old systems with a 14-character limit on filenames; so the Info file "
"name for this document could be @file{texinfo} rather than "
"@file{texinfo.info} on such a system.  @code{@@setfilename} is a way to "
"specify an alternative name."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1163
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@settitle}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1165
msgid "@nodedescription Create a title for the printed work."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1165
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@settitle}: Set the Document Title"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1168
#, no-wrap
msgid "settitle"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1169
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document title, specifying"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1172
msgid "A Texinfo file should contain a line that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1175
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@settitle @var{title}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1183
msgid ""
"Write the @code{@@settitle} command at the beginning of a line and follow it "
"on the same line by the title.  Do not write anything else on the line.  The "
"@code{@@settitle} command should precede everything that generates actual "
"output.  The best place for it is right after the @code{@@setfilename} "
"command, if present (described in the previous section)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1187
msgid ""
"This command gives the title to use in a header or footer for double-sided "
"printed output, in case such headings are output.  For more on headings for "
"printed output, see @ref{Heading Generation}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1188
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<title>} HTML tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1191
msgid ""
"In HTML, @var{title} serves as the document @samp{<title>} and it becomes "
"the default document description in the @samp{<head>} part."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1199
msgid ""
"When the title page is used in the output, as is generally the case for "
"printed output, the title in the @code{@@settitle} command does not affect "
"the title as it appears on the title page.  Thus, the two do not need not to "
"match exactly.  A practice we recommend is to include the version or edition "
"number of the manual in the @code{@@settitle} title; on the title page, the "
"version number generally appears as a @code{@@subtitle} so it would be "
"omitted from the @code{@@title}.  @xref{Titlepage & Copyright Page}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1201 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1203
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1204
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preamble"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1203
msgid "@nodedescription Start of the Texinfo file up to first content."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1205
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo Preamble"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1212
msgid ""
"The @dfn{preamble} starts at the beginning of the Texinfo file and continues "
"until the first directly output material.  It typically includes the file "
"header (@pxref{Texinfo File Header}), the @code{@@copying} block specifying "
"the document permissions (@pxref{@code{@@copying}}) and the title page "
"specifications (@pxref{Titlepage & Copyright Page})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1219
msgid ""
"The preamble may contain commands that affect document formatting as a whole "
"but which do not produce output, or do not produce output straight away, "
"such as @code{@@settitle} (@pxref{@code{@@settitle}}), "
"@code{@@documentlanguage}, (@pxref{@code{@@documentlanguage}}), commands "
"setting the headings, controlling indentation or hyphenation, or the table "
"of contents (@pxref{Contents})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1231
msgid ""
"Any text that starts a paragraph, @@-commands that are formatted as "
"quotations, tables, lists and so on, and @code{@@node} (@pxref{Nodes})  and "
"chapter structuring commands (@pxref{Chapter Structuring}) end the preamble."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1234
msgid ""
"The concept of the preamble is significant for @LaTeX{} output, as the "
"@code{\\begin@{document@}} line is output at the end of the preamble."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1238
msgid ""
"In plaintext, the preamble is simply output as usual at the beginning of the "
"document; for example, a @code{@@contents} in the preamble is output as the "
"table of contents (@pxref{Contents})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1244
msgid ""
"There is not much special treatment of the preamble for HTML and Info output "
"either.  However, some settings current at the very end of the preamble may "
"be used for the document as a whole, regardless of what follows.  This may "
"apply to commands specifying the indentation, or the language "
"(@pxref{@code{@@documentlanguage}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1249
msgid ""
"For example, for the following document, the HTML and Info copying comments "
"are formatted with @code{@@documentlanguage} set to @samp{pt}, as it is the "
"last @code{@@documentlanguage} before the end of the preamble."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1254
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@documentlanguage fr\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1258
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@copying\n"
"The copying information @@error@{@} some text\n"
"@@end copying\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1260
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@documentlanguage pt\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1262
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Text ending the preamble\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1264
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@documentlanguage de\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1266
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Top\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1271
#, no-wrap
msgid "Start and End of Header"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1273
msgid "@nodedescription Formatting a region in Emacs requires this."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1273
#, no-wrap
msgid "Start and End of Header for Emacs"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1275
msgid "Start of Header"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1275
#, no-wrap
msgid "Start of header line"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1281
msgid ""
"In Emacs, start- and end-of-header lines can be used to enclose commands "
"that globally affect the document in the Texinfo preamble.  This allows you "
"to format only part of a Texinfo file for Info or printing.  "
"@xref{@code{texinfo-format} commands}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1283
msgid "A start-of-header line is a Texinfo comment that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1286
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@c %**start of header\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1292
msgid ""
"Write the start-of-header line on the second line of a Texinfo file.  Follow "
"the start-of-header commands that globally affect the document formatting, "
"such as @code{@@settitle}, @code{@@synindex} or @code{@@footnotestyle}; and "
"then by an @w{end-of-header} line."
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1294
msgid "End of Header"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1294
#, no-wrap
msgid "End of header line"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1297
msgid "A end-of-header line is a Texinfo comment that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1300
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@c %**end of header\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1306
msgid ""
"The odd string of characters, @samp{%**}, is to ensure that no other comment "
"is accidentally taken for a start-of-header line.  You can change it if you "
"wish by setting the @code{tex-start-of-header} and/or @code{tex-end-of-"
"header} Emacs variables.  @xref{Texinfo Mode Printing}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1309
msgid ""
"The start- and end-of-header lines are not part of the Texinfo format "
"specification, which is why they are implemented with comments."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1311 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1313
#, no-wrap
msgid "Directory Category"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1313
msgid "@nodedescription A category for a manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1315
#, no-wrap
msgid "dircategory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1318
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@dircategory} command to specify a category for the manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1323
msgid ""
"@code{@@dircategory} commands are usually followed by @code{@@direntry} "
"blocks, which are used by @command{install-info}.  @xref{Installing Dir "
"Entries}, for an example of how these commands are used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1327
msgid ""
"The first @code{@@dircategory} command in a manual is the category for the "
"entire manual.  Subsequent uses of @code{@@dircategory} set the category for "
"following @code{@@direntry} blocks only."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1328
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dir categories, choosing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1329
#, no-wrap
msgid "Categories, choosing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1334
msgid ""
"There is no consistency in the use of directory categories across manuals, "
"and there is unlikely ever to be, as authors work on Texinfo documentation "
"independently with no central co-ordination that would result in such "
"consistency."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1338
msgid ""
"It is possible for users or operating system distributions to edit "
"categories in Info or Texinfo files, if a logical organization within "
"@code{dir} files is something that they value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1343
msgid ""
"Authors of manuals may consider using some of the following categories for "
"manuals.  For a package that installs many manuals (such as Emacs), it may "
"be a good idea to use package-specific category, or categories, to group its "
"manuals."
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1383
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Archiving\n"
"Audio\n"
"Business and productivity\n"
"Compression\n"
"Database\n"
"Dictionaries\n"
"Documentation translation\n"
"Editors\n"
"Education\n"
"Email\n"
"Fonts\n"
"Games\n"
"Graphics\n"
"Health\n"
"Interface\n"
"Internet applications\n"
"Live communications\n"
"Localization\n"
"Mathematics\n"
"Music\n"
"Network applications\n"
"Printing\n"
"Security\n"
"Science\n"
"Software development\n"
"Software libraries\n"
"Spreadsheets\n"
"System administration\n"
"Telephony\n"
"Text creation and manipulation\n"
"Version control\n"
"Video\n"
"Web authoring\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1386 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1389
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document Permissions"
msgstr ""

#.  old node name
#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1389
msgid "@nodedescription Ensuring your manual is free."
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1389
msgid "Software Copying Permissions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1390
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document permissions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1391
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copying permissions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1397
msgid ""
"This segment describes the document and contains the copyright notice and "
"copying permissions.  This is done with the @code{@@copying} command.  A "
"real manual includes more text here, according to the license under which it "
"is distributed."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1402
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@copying\n"
"This is a short example of a complete Texinfo file, version 1.0.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1405
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Copyright @@copyright@{@} 2026 Free Software Foundation, Inc.\n"
"@@end copying\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1413
msgid ""
"The copyright notice and copying permissions for a document need to appear "
"in several places in the various Texinfo output formats.  Therefore, Texinfo "
"provides a command (@code{@@copying}) to declare this text once, and another "
"command (@code{@@insertcopying}) to insert the text at appropriate points."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1418
msgid ""
"For plain text output, you must insert the copyright information explicitly "
"if you want it to appear, as it it not otherwise output.  For instance, you "
"could have the following near the start of the Texinfo file:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1423
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifplaintext\n"
"@@insertcopying\n"
"@@end ifplaintext\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1427
msgid ""
"You could include other title-like information for the plain text output in "
"the same place."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1434
msgid ""
"If the document is a software manual, the software is typically under a "
"different license---for GNU and many other free software packages, software "
"is usually released under the GNU GPL, and manuals are released under the "
"GNU FDL@.  It is helpful to state the license of the software of the manual, "
"but giving the complete text of the software license is not necessarily "
"required."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1436
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@copying}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1438
msgid "@nodedescription Declare the document's copying permissions."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1438
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@copying}: Declare Copying Permissions"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1441
#, no-wrap
msgid "copying"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1449
msgid ""
"The @code{@@copying} command should be given very early in the document; the "
"recommended location is right after the header material (@pxref{Texinfo File "
"Header}).  It conventionally consists of a sentence or two about what the "
"program is, identification of the documentation itself, the legal copyright "
"line, and the copying permissions.  Here is a skeletal example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1454
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@copying\n"
"This manual is for @var{program} (version @var{version}, updated\n"
"@var{date}), which @dots{}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1456
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Copyright @@copyright@{@} @var{years} @var{copyright-owner}.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1461
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@quotation\n"
"Permission is granted to @dots{}\n"
"@@end quotation\n"
"@@end copying\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1465
msgid ""
"The @code{@@quotation} has no legal significance; it's there to improve "
"readability in some contexts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1472
msgid ""
"The text of @code{@@copying} appears as a comment at the beginning of Info "
"and HTML output files.  This information is also output at the beginning of "
"the DocBook output files using appropriate markup.  It is @emph{not} output "
"implicitly in plain text or printed output; it's up to you to use "
"@code{@@insertcopying} to emit the copying information.  See the next "
"section for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1477
msgid ""
"The @code{@@copyright@{@}} command generates a @samp{c} inside a circle when "
"the output format supports this glyph (print and HTML always do, for "
"instance).  When the glyph is not supported in the output, it generates the "
"three-character sequence @samp{(C)}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1480
msgid "The copyright notice itself has the following legally-prescribed form:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1483
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copyright @copyright{} @var{years} @var{copyright-owner}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1485
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copyright word, always in English"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1489
msgid ""
"The word `Copyright' must always be written in English, even if the document "
"is otherwise written in another language.  This is due to international law."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1490
#, no-wrap
msgid "Years, in copyright line"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1495
msgid ""
"The list of years should include all years in which a version was completed "
"(even if it was released in a subsequent year).  It is simplest for each "
"year to be written out individually and in full, separated by commas."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1496
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copyright holder for FSF works"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1497
#, no-wrap
msgid "Holder of copyright for FSF works"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1498
#, no-wrap
msgid "Owner of copyright for FSF works"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1502
msgid ""
"The copyright owner (or owners) is whoever holds legal copyright on the "
"work.  In the case of works assigned to the FSF, the owner is `Free Software "
"Foundation, Inc.'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1508
msgid ""
"The copyright `line' may actually be split across multiple lines, both in "
"the source document and in the output.  This often happens for documents "
"with a long history, having many different years of publication.  If you do "
"use several lines, do not indent any of them (or anything else in the "
"@code{@@copying} block) in the source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1514
msgid ""
"@xref{Copyright Notices,,, maintain, GNU Maintainer Information}, for "
"additional information.  @xref{GNU Sample Texts}, for the full text to be "
"used in GNU manuals.  @xref{GNU Free Documentation License}, for the license "
"itself under which GNU and other free manuals are distributed."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1516
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@insertcopying}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1518
msgid "@nodedescription Where to insert the permissions."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1518
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@insertcopying}: Include Permissions Text"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1521
#, no-wrap
msgid "insertcopying"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1522
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copying text, including"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1523
#, no-wrap
msgid "Permissions text, including"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1524
#, no-wrap
msgid "Including permissions text"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1528
msgid ""
"The @code{@@insertcopying} command is simply written on a line by itself, "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1531 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1738
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@insertcopying\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1536
msgid ""
"This inserts the text previously defined by @code{@@copying}.  To meet legal "
"requirements, it must be used on the copyright page in the printed manual "
"(@pxref{Copyright})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1545
msgid ""
"The @code{@@copying} command itself causes the permissions text to appear in "
"an Info file @emph{before} the first node.  The text is also copied into the "
"beginning of each split Info output file, as is legally necessary.  This "
"location implies a human reading the manual using Info does @emph{not} see "
"this text (except when using the advanced Info command @kbd{g *}). This does "
"not matter for legal purposes, because the text is present.  But to get a "
"visible text in the output, @code{@@insertcopying} should be used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1550
msgid ""
"Similarly, the @code{@@copying} text is automatically included at the "
"beginning of each HTML output file, as an HTML comment.  Again, this text is "
"not visible without @code{@@insertcopying} (unless the reader views the HTML "
"source)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1555
msgid ""
"The permissions text defined by @code{@@copying} also appears automatically "
"at the beginning of the DocBook output files using appropriate markup.  "
"@code{@@insertcopying} can be used to output the permission text within "
"normal text."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1557
#, no-wrap
msgid "Titlepage & Copyright Page"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1559
msgid "@nodedescription Creating the title and copyright pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1559
#, no-wrap
msgid "Title and Copyright Pages"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1561
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copyright page"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1562
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printed permissions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1563
#, no-wrap
msgid "Permissions, printed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1569
msgid ""
"The first page of a printed book is usually the @dfn{title page}, which "
"contains the name of the book and the author.  Copyright and publication "
"information is usually printed on the next page; we refer to this page as "
"the @dfn{copyright page}.  As this is on the back (verso)  of the title "
"page, this page is also known as the @dfn{title verso}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1574
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document metadata"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1581
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides commands for you to provide the document title, any "
"subtitle, the name of the author (or names of several authors), and copying "
"and publication information for the document.  This @dfn{metadata} is used "
"for the contents of the title page and copyright page.  Likewise, this "
"information appears in the output in some form for other output formats, "
"even if not displayed as a title page."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1585
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides at least three different ways to provide this metadata and "
"to generate the title and copyright pages, which are described in the "
"following subsections."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1589
msgid ""
"In HTML, the best way to get a title page similar to printed manuals is to "
"set the @code{NO_TOP_NODE_OUTPUT} customization variable "
"(@pxref{@code{NO_TOP_NODE_OUTPUT}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1590
#, no-wrap
msgid "shorttitlepage"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1591
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bastard title page"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1592
#, no-wrap
msgid "Title page @subentry bastard"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1599
msgid ""
"For sufficiently simple documents, and for the bastard title page in "
"traditional book front matter, Texinfo also provides a command "
"@code{@@shorttitlepage} which takes the rest of the line as the title.  The "
"argument is typeset on a page by itself and followed by a blank page.  In "
"HTML, @code{@@shorttitlepage} can play the same role as @code{@@settitle}, "
"if @code{@@settitle} is not set.  @xref{@code{@@settitle}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1601
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document Metadata"
msgstr ""

#. type: nodedescriptionblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1605
msgid ""
"The @code{@@documentinfo}, @code{@@copying}, @code{@@publication}, and "
"@code{@@maketitle} commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1606
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document Metadata and the @code{@@maketitle} Command"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1608
#, no-wrap
msgid "documentinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1611
msgid ""
"The simplest way to define document metadata used for the title page is "
"using the @code{@@documentinfo} command.  Here is a template:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1620
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@documentinfo\n"
"@@title @var{name-of-manual-when-printed}\n"
"@@subtitle @var{subtitle-if-any}\n"
"@@subtitle @var{second-subtitle}\n"
"@@author @var{author}\n"
"@@end documentinfo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1630
msgid ""
"You should only use the @code{@@title} command once within the "
"@code{@@documentinfo} block.  You can use the @code{@@subtitle} command more "
"than once, and the order that any @code{@@subtitle} commands appear in the "
"block is kept when the Texinfo file is processed.  Likewise, you can use "
"@code{@@author} more than once.  (However, the order of @code{@@title}, "
"@code{@@subtitle}, and @code{@@author} commands relative to each other is "
"not significant.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1634
msgid ""
"The information for the copyright page appears within the @code{@@copying} "
"and @code{@@publication} commands.  @xref{Document Permissions} for more "
"discussion of @code{@@copying}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1635
#, no-wrap
msgid "publication"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1642
msgid ""
"The @code{@@publication} command works very similarly to @code{@@copying}.  "
"The Texinfo processors save the contents of this block when they encounter "
"it in the Texinfo source.  You use this command to provide information about "
"the publication of the document, such as the address of the publisher, or "
"ISBN@.  You use this block for information that would go an ``imprint'' or "
"``colophon'' in a printed book."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1643
#, no-wrap
msgid "maketitle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1647
msgid ""
"Finally, to actually output the title and copyright pages, you use the "
"@code{@@maketitle} command.  Write this command on a line by itself.  This "
"draws together the available information to produce these two pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1649
msgid "These commands would be used like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1659
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@copying\n"
"@var{copying-info}\n"
"@@end copying\n"
"@@publication\n"
"Published by @var{publisher-name}\n"
"@var{publisher-address}\n"
"@@end publication\n"
"@@maketitle\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1662
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@titlepage}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1664
msgid "@nodedescription Create a title for the printed document."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1664 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:21102
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@titlepage"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1667 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1668
#, no-wrap
msgid "titlepage"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1667
#, no-wrap
msgid "Title page"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1674
msgid ""
"An alternative to using @code{@@maketitle} to create title and copyright "
"pages is to use the @code{@@titlepage} command.  Any material enclosed "
"between @code{@@titlepage} and @code{@@end titlepage} lines is directly "
"output for a printed manual.  Here is a basic example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1679
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@titlepage\n"
"@@title Sample Title\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1684
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@c The following two commands start the copyright page.\n"
"@@page\n"
"@@vskip 0pt plus 1filll\n"
"@@insertcopying\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1686
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Published by @dots{}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1689
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Cover art by @dots{}\n"
"@@end titlepage\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1694
msgid ""
"Start the material for the title page and following copyright page with "
"@code{@@titlepage} on a line by itself and end it with @code{@@end "
"titlepage} on a line by itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1700
msgid ""
"The material before the @samp{@@page} line is used for the title page.  The "
"following subsubsections explain the two ways that the @code{@@titlepage} "
"command might be used within a Texinfo document to produce a title page, the "
"first closer to the previous method with @code{@@documentinfo}, the second "
"with more explicit formatting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1703
msgid ""
"However, the copyright page is always produced in the same way with "
"@code{@@titlepage}."
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1706
msgid "Copyright"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1706
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copyright Page"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1707
#, no-wrap
msgid "page@r{, within @code{@@titlepage}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1714
msgid ""
"By using the @code{@@page} command, you can force a page break within the "
"region delineated by the @code{@@titlepage} and @code{@@end titlepage} "
"commands and thereby create more than one unnumbered page.  This is how the "
"copyright page is produced.  (The @code{@@titlepage} command might perhaps "
"have been better named the @code{@@titleandadditionalpages} command, but "
"that would have been rather long!)"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1715
#, no-wrap
msgid "vskip @r{@TeX{} vertical skip}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1716
#, no-wrap
msgid "filll @r{@TeX{} dimension}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1720
msgid ""
"To push the copyright notice and the other text on the copyright page "
"towards the bottom of the page, use the following incantation after "
"@code{@@page}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1723
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@vskip 0pt plus 1filll\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1732
msgid ""
"The @code{@@vskip} command inserts whitespace in the @TeX{} output; it is "
"ignored in all other output formats.  The @samp{0pt plus 1filll} means to "
"put in zero points of mandatory whitespace, and as much optional whitespace "
"as needed to push the following text to the bottom of the page.  Note the "
"use of three @samp{l}s in the word @samp{filll}; this is correct."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1735
msgid ""
"To insert the copyright text itself, write @code{@@insertcopying} next "
"(@pxref{Document Permissions}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1744
msgid ""
"(This is required for outputting the copying text when using "
"@code{@@titlepage}, unlike with @code{@@maketitle} where this text is output "
"automatically)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1747
msgid ""
"Follow the copying text by the publisher, ISBN numbers, cover art credits, "
"and other such information."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1750
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@title @@subtitle @@author}"
msgstr ""

#. type: nodedescriptionblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1753
msgid "The @code{@@title}, @code{@@subtitle}, and @code{@@author} commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1754
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@title}, @code{@@subtitle}, and @code{@@author}"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1757
msgid "title subtitle author"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1757
#, no-wrap
msgid "title"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1758
#, no-wrap
msgid "subtitle"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1759
#, no-wrap
msgid "author"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1768
msgid ""
"One way to use @code{@@titlepage} is to use @code{@@title}, "
"@code{@@subtitle}, and @code{@@author} commands inside the "
"@code{@@titlepage} block.  With this method, you do not specify any of the "
"actual formatting of the title page.  You specify the text you want, and "
"Texinfo does the formatting.  The usual formatting consist of black rules "
"under the title and author lines and the subtitle text set flush to the "
"right-hand side of the page."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1772
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{@@title}, @code{@@subtitle}, and @code{@@author} "
"commands to create a title page in which the vertical and horizontal spacing "
"is done for you automatically."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1779
msgid ""
"Write the @code{@@title}, @code{@@subtitle}, or @code{@@author} commands at "
"the beginning of a line followed by the title, subtitle, or author.  The "
"@code{@@author} command may be used for a quotation in an @code{@@quotation} "
"block (@pxref{@code{@@quotation}}); except for that, it is an error to use "
"any of these commands outside of @code{@@titlepage}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1785
msgid ""
"The @code{@@title} command normally produces a line in which the title is "
"set flush to the left-hand side of the page in a larger than normal font.  "
"The title is underlined with a black rule.  The title must be given on a "
"single line in the source file; it will be broken into multiple lines of "
"output is needed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1791
msgid ""
"For long titles, the @code{@@*} command may be used to specify the line "
"breaks in long titles if the automatic breaks do not suit.  Such explicit "
"line breaks are generally reflected in all output formats; if you only want "
"to specify them for the printed output, use a conditional "
"(@pxref{Conditionals}).  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1794
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@title This Long Title@@inlinefmt@{tex,@@*@} Is Broken in @@TeX@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1798
msgid ""
"The @code{@@subtitle} command normally sets subtitles in a normal-sized font "
"flush to the right-hand side of the page."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1804
msgid ""
"The @code{@@author} command normally sets the names of the author or authors "
"in a middle-sized font flush to the left-hand side of the page on a line "
"near the bottom of the title page.  The names are followed by a black rule "
"that is thinner than the rule that normally underlines the title."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1808
msgid ""
"There are two ways to use the @code{@@author} command: you can write the "
"name or names on the remaining part of the line that starts with an "
"@code{@@author} command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1811
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@author by Jane Smith and John Doe\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1816
msgid ""
"or you can write the names one above each other by using multiple "
"@code{@@author} commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1821
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@author Jane Smith\n"
"@@author John Doe\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1826 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1897
msgid "A template for this method looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1837
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@titlepage\n"
"@@title @var{name-of-manual-when-printed}\n"
"@@subtitle @var{subtitle-if-any}\n"
"@@subtitle @var{second-subtitle}\n"
"@@author @var{author}\n"
"@@page\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end titlepage\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1841
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@titlefont @@center @@sp}"
msgstr ""

#. type: nodedescriptionblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1844
msgid "The @code{@@titlefont}, @code{@@center}, and @code{@@sp} commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1845
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@titlefont}, @code{@@center}, and @code{@@sp}"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1848
msgid "titlefont center sp"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1848
#, no-wrap
msgid "titlefont"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1849
#, no-wrap
msgid "center"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1850
#, no-wrap
msgid "sp @r{(titlepage line spacing)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1857
msgid ""
"Texinfo also provides a second method for creating a title page, using "
"typesetting commands that are not to be used in the main text.  This method "
"uses the @code{@@titlefont}, @code{@@sp}, and @code{@@center} commands to "
"generate a title page in which the words on the page are centered."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1861
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@titlefont} command to select a large font suitable for the "
"title itself.  You can use @code{@@titlefont} more than once if you have an "
"especially long title."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1866
msgid ""
"For HTML output, each @code{@@titlefont} command produces an @code{<h1>} "
"heading, but the HTML document @code{<title>} is not affected.  For that, "
"you could put a @code{@@settitle} command before the @code{@@titlefont} "
"command (@pxref{@code{@@settitle}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1869 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6349
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8494 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9141
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11413 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11466
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11736 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13730
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:18134
msgid "For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1872
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@titlefont@{Texinfo@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1876
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@center} command at the beginning of a line to center the "
"remaining text on that line.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1879
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@center @@titlefont@{Texinfo@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1884
msgid ""
"centers the title, which in this example is ``Texinfo'' printed in the title "
"font."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1886
msgid "Use the @code{@@sp} command to insert vertical space.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1889 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10703
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@sp 2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1895
msgid ""
"This inserts two blank lines on the printed page.  (@xref{@code{@@sp}}, for "
"more information about the @code{@@sp} command.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1909
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@titlepage\n"
"@@sp 10\n"
"@@center @@titlefont@{@var{name-of-manual-when-printed}@}\n"
"@@sp 2\n"
"@@center @var{subtitle-if-any}\n"
"@@sp 2\n"
"@@center @var{author}\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end titlepage\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1913
msgid "The spacing of the example fits an 8.5 by 11 inch manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1915
#, no-wrap
msgid "Title Page Version"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1917
msgid "@nodedescription Version of a program or edition of a manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1917
#, no-wrap
msgid "Displaying the Version or Edition on the Title Page"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1931
msgid ""
"When you write a manual about a computer program, you should write the "
"version of the program to which the manual applies on the title page.  You "
"would typically include these in a subtitle.  If the manual changes more "
"frequently than the program or is independent of it, you should also include "
"an edition number@footnote{We have found that it is helpful to refer to "
"versions of independent manuals as `editions' and versions of programs as "
"`versions'; otherwise, we find we are liable to confuse each other in "
"conversation by referring to both the documentation and the software with "
"the same words.} for the manual.  This helps readers keep track of which "
"manual is for which version of the program.  (The `Top' node should also "
"contain this information; see @ref{The Top Node}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1933 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1935
#, no-wrap
msgid "Heading Generation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1935
msgid ""
"@nodedescription Turn on page headings after the title and copyright pages."
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1938
msgid "end titlepage"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1938
#, no-wrap
msgid "Generating page headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1944
msgid ""
"Texinfo has two standard page heading formats, one for documents printed on "
"one side of each sheet of paper (single-sided printing), and the other for "
"documents printed on both sides of each sheet (double-sided printing)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1946
msgid "In full generality, you can control the headings in different ways:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1951
msgid ""
"The conventional way is to write a @code{@@setchapternewpage} command before "
"the title page commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1957
msgid ""
"Most documents are formatted with the standard single-sided or double-sided "
"headings, (sometimes) using @code{@@setchapternewpage odd} for double-sided "
"printing and (almost always) no @code{@@setchapternewpage} command for "
"single-sided printing (@pxref{@code{@@setchapternewpage}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1963
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can use the @code{@@headings} command to prevent page "
"headings from being generated or to start them for either single or double-"
"sided printing. To turn off headings, write @code{@@headings off}.  "
"@xref{@code{@@headings}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1967
msgid ""
"Or, you may specify your own page heading and footing format.  "
"@xref{Headings}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1970
#, no-wrap
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1972
msgid "@nodedescription How to create a table of contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1972
#, no-wrap
msgid "Generating a Table of Contents"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1973
#, no-wrap
msgid "Table of contents"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1974
#, no-wrap
msgid "Contents, table of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1975
#, no-wrap
msgid "Short table of contents"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1976
#, no-wrap
msgid "contents"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1977
#, no-wrap
msgid "summarycontents"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1978
#, no-wrap
msgid "shortcontents"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1985
msgid ""
"The @code{@@chapter}, @code{@@section}, and other structuring commands "
"(@pxref{Chapter Structuring}) supply the information to make up a table of "
"contents, but they do not cause an actual table to appear in the manual.  To "
"do this, you must use the @code{@@contents} and/or @code{@@summarycontents} "
"command(s)."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1987 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20120
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@contents"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1994
msgid ""
"Generates a table of contents in a printed manual, including all chapters, "
"sections, subsections, etc., as well as appendices and unnumbered chapters.  "
"Headings generated by @code{@@majorheading}, @code{@@chapheading}, and the "
"other @code{@@@dots{}heading} commands do not appear in the table of "
"contents (@pxref{Structuring Command Types})."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1995 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20930
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@shortcontents"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1996 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:21018
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@summarycontents"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:1998
msgid "(@code{@@summarycontents} is a synonym for @code{@@shortcontents}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2003
msgid ""
"Generates a short or summary table of contents that lists only the chapters, "
"appendices, and unnumbered chapters.  Sections, subsections and "
"subsubsections are omitted.  Only a long manual needs a short table of "
"contents in addition to the full table of contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2012
msgid ""
"Both contents commands should be written on a line by themselves, and placed "
"near the beginning of the file, after the @code{@@end titlepage} "
"(@pxref{@code{@@titlepage}}), before any sectioning command.  The contents "
"commands automatically generate a chapter-like heading at the top of the "
"first table of contents page, so don't include any sectioning command such "
"as @code{@@unnumbered} before them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2017
msgid ""
"Since an Info file uses menus instead of tables of contents, the Info "
"formatting commands ignore the contents commands.  But the contents are "
"included in plain text output and in other output formats, such as HTML."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2022
msgid ""
"In HTML output, the links in the short table of contents point to "
"corresponding entries in the full table of contents rather than the text of "
"the document. The links in the full table of contents point to the main text "
"of the document."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2024
msgid "@code{@@shortcontents} is not implemented for @LaTeX{} output."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2026
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Top Node"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2028
msgid "@nodedescription Creating the `Top' node"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2028
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `Top' Node"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2029
#, no-wrap
msgid "Top node"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2030
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node, @samp{Top}"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2032
msgid "Top Node Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2037
msgid ""
"The `Top' node is the node in which a reader enters an Info manual.  As "
"such, it should contain a very brief description of the manual (including "
"the version number).  The contents of the `Top' node do not appear in "
"printed output nor in DocBook output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2041
msgid ""
"It is conventional to write a line with a @code{@@top} command containing "
"the title of the document immediately after the @code{@@node Top} line "
"(@pxref{@code{@@top} Command})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2045
msgid ""
"We repeat the short description from the beginning of the @samp{@@copying} "
"text, but there's no need to repeat the copyright information, so we don't "
"use @samp{@@insertcopying} here."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2053
msgid ""
"In the Info output (and sometimes HTML output), the `Top' node usually "
"contains a top-level @dfn{menu} listing the chapters, and possibly a "
"@dfn{detailed menu} listing all the nodes in the entire document.  It is not "
"usually necessary to give such a menu in the Texinfo source, as "
"@command{texi2any} generates these menus automatically.  However, the "
"following example shows how you would use the @code{@@menu} command to give "
"a menu in the `Top' node:"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2055
#, no-wrap
msgid "Top node example"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2059
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Top\n"
"@@top Short Sample\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2061
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is a short sample Texinfo file.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2068
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@menu\n"
"* First Chapter::    The first chapter is the\n"
"                       only chapter in this sample.\n"
"* Index::            Complete index.\n"
"@@end menu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2072
#, no-wrap
msgid "Master Menu Parts"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2073
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parts of a Master Menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2074
#, no-wrap
msgid "Master menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2075
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu, master"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2076
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parts of a master menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2083
msgid ""
"A @dfn{master menu} is the main menu.  It is customary to include a detailed "
"menu listing all the nodes in the document in this menu.  Like any other "
"menu, a master menu is enclosed in @code{@@menu} and @code{@@end menu} and "
"does not appear in the printed output nor in DocBook output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2087
msgid ""
"The master menu contains entries for the major nodes in the Texinfo file: "
"the nodes for the chapters, chapter-like sections, and the appendices, "
"followed by nodes for the indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2088
#, no-wrap
msgid "detailmenu"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2089
#, no-wrap
msgid "Detailed menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2098
msgid ""
"You may choose to follow these entries with a @dfn{detailed menu}.  This "
"lists other, lower-level nodes, often ordered by chapter.  These items may "
"be a convenience for an inquirer who can go directly to a particular node "
"when searching for specific information, rather than going through an "
"intermediate menu.  If you use a detailed menu in your master menu, mark it "
"with the @code{@@detailmenu @dots{} @@end detailmenu} environment."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2103
msgid ""
"Each section in the menu can be introduced by a descriptive line.  So long "
"as the line does not begin with an asterisk, it will not be treated as a "
"menu entry.  (@xref{Writing a Menu}, for more information.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2106
msgid ""
"For example, the master menu for this manual looks like the following (but "
"has many more entries):"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2113
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@menu\n"
"* Copying Conditions::  Your rights.\n"
"* Overview::            Texinfo in brief.\n"
"@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2117
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Variables Index::\n"
"* General Index::\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2122
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@detailmenu\n"
"--- The Detailed Node Listing ---\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2124
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Overview of Texinfo\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Reporting Bugs:: @dots{}\n"
"@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Beginning a Texinfo File\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2136
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Sample Beginning:: @dots{}\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end detailmenu\n"
"@@end menu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2140 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2142
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Body of the Document"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2142
msgid "@nodedescription The rest of the nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2144
#, no-wrap
msgid "Frontmatter, text in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2150
msgid ""
"The body segment contains all the text of the document.  A manual is divided "
"into one or more @dfn{nodes} (@pxref{Nodes}).  The example illustrates a "
"chapter made of three nodes, one for introductory material in the chapter, "
"and two sections.  The introductory material contains an enumerated list."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2155
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node First Chapter\n"
"@@chapter First Chapter\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@item\n"
"This is the second item.\n"
"@@end enumerate\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2181
#, no-wrap
msgid "Second section of first chapter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2188
msgid ""
"In the Info output, the @samp{First Chapter} node will contain a menu "
"listing the two sections in the chapter.  Similarly, when this node is "
"output in its own HTML file, it will contain a table of contents for the "
"chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2190
msgid "Here is what the contents of this chapter will look like:"
msgstr ""

#. type: chapheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2194
#, no-wrap
msgid "1. First Chapter"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2197
msgid "This is the first chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2199
msgid "Here is a numbered list."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2203
msgid "This is the first item."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2206
msgid "This is the second item."
msgstr ""

#. type: heading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2208
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.1 First Section"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2211
msgid "First section of first chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: heading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2212
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.2 Second Section"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2215
msgid "Second section of first chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2220
msgid ""
"(In the Info and HTML output, the chapter would also be split into nodes)."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2222
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ending a File"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2224
msgid "@nodedescription What is at the end of a Texinfo file?"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2224
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ending a Texinfo File"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2225
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ending a Texinfo file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2226
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo file ending"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2227
#, no-wrap
msgid "File ending"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2232
msgid ""
"The end of a Texinfo file should include commands to create indices "
"(@pxref{Printing Indices & Menus}), and the @code{@@bye} command to mark the "
"last line to be processed.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2236
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Index\n"
"@@unnumbered Index\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2242
#, no-wrap
msgid "bye"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2247
msgid "File End"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2247
msgid ""
"A @code{@@bye} command terminates Texinfo processing.  It should be on a "
"line by itself.  Anything following @code{@@bye} is completely ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2249 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2251
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2251
msgid "@nodedescription Writing nodes, the basic unit of Texinfo."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2253 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2287
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:15450
#, no-wrap
msgid "node"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2253
msgid "@@node"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2254
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node, defined"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2261
msgid ""
"A @dfn{node} is a region of text that begins at a @code{@@node} command, and "
"continues until the next @code{@@node} command.  To specify a node, write a "
"@code{@@node} command at the beginning of a line, and follow it with the "
"name of the node.  Info readers display one node at a time, and provide "
"commands for the user to move to related nodes.  The HTML output can be "
"similarly navigated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2267
msgid ""
"Nodes are used as the targets of cross-references.  Cross-references, such "
"as the one at the end of this sentence, are made with @code{@@xref} and "
"related commands; see @ref{Cross References}.  Cross-references can be "
"sprinkled throughout the text.  Other @@-commands may also be the target of "
"cross-references (@pxref{@code{@@anchor}}, @pxref{Floats})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2278
msgid ""
"Normally, you put a node command immediately before each chapter structuring "
"command---for example, an @code{@@section} or @code{@@subsection} line.  "
"(@xref{Chapter Structuring}.)  You should do this even if you do not intend "
"to format the file for Info.  This is because printed output uses both "
"@code{@@node} names and chapter-structuring names in the output for cross-"
"references.  The only time you are likely to use the chapter structuring "
"commands without also using nodes is if you are writing a document that "
"contains no cross references and will only be printed, not transformed into "
"Info, HTML, or other formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2280
#, no-wrap
msgid "Writing a Node"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2282
msgid "@nodedescription How to write a @code{@@node} line."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2282
#, no-wrap
msgid "Writing a @code{@@node} Line"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2283
#, no-wrap
msgid "Writing a @code{@@node} line"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2284
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@node} line writing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2285
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node line writing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2290
msgid ""
"Write @code{@@node} at the beginning of a line followed by the name of the "
"node, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2293
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@node @var{node-name}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2298
msgid ""
"After you have inserted a @code{@@node} line, you should immediately write "
"the @@-command for the associated chapter or section (if any)  and insert "
"its name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2305
msgid ""
"You may optionally follow the node name argument to @code{@@node} with up to "
"three optional arguments on the rest of the same line, separating the "
"arguments with commas.  These are the names of the `Next', `Previous', and "
"`Up' pointers, in that order.  Hence, the template for a fully-written-out "
"node line with `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2308
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@node @var{node-name}, @var{next}, @var{previous}, @var{up}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2317
msgid ""
"The @var{node-name} argument must be present, but the others are optional.  "
"If you wish to specify some but not others, just insert commas as needed, as "
"in: @samp{@@node mynode,,,uppernode}.  Any spaces before or after each name "
"on the @code{@@node} line are ignored.  However, if your Texinfo document is "
"hierarchically organized, as virtually all are, we recommend leaving off all "
"the pointers and letting @code{texi2any} determine them."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2318
#, no-wrap
msgid "Creating pointers with @code{texi2any}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2319
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pointer creation with @code{texi2any}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2320
#, no-wrap
msgid "Automatic pointer creation with @code{texi2any}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2321
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implicit pointer creation with @code{texi2any}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2329
msgid ""
"The @code{texi2any} program automatically determines node pointers for a "
"hierarchically organized document.  For it to do this, each @code{@@node} "
"command should be followed immediately by a sectioning command such as "
"@code{@@chapter} or @code{@@section} (except that comment lines may "
"intervene).  Finally, you must follow the `Top' @code{@@node} line with a "
"line beginning with @code{@@top} to mark the top-level node in the file.  "
"@xref{@code{@@top} Command}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2335
msgid ""
"Even when you explicitly specify all pointers, you cannot write the nodes in "
"the Texinfo source file in an arbitrary order.  You must write the nodes in "
"the order you wish them to appear in the output.  For Info format one can "
"imagine that the order may not matter, but it matters for the other formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2343
msgid ""
"In most cases, you will want to take advantage of the pointer creation "
"feature, and not redundantly specify node pointers that the programs can "
"determine.  However, Texinfo documents are not required to be organized "
"hierarchically or in fact to contain sectioning commands at all (for "
"example, if you never intend the document to be printed), so node pointers "
"may still be specified explicitly, in full generality."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2347
msgid ""
"If you are using GNU Emacs, and want explicit pointers, you can use the "
"update node commands provided by Texinfo mode to insert the names of the "
"pointers.  (@xref{Updating Nodes and Menus}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2354
msgid ""
"Alternatively, you can insert the `Next', `Previous', and `Up' pointers "
"yourself.  If you do this in Emacs, you may find it helpful to use the "
"Texinfo mode keyboard command @kbd{C-c C-c n}.  This command inserts "
"@samp{@@node} and a comment line listing the names of the pointers in their "
"proper order.  The comment line helps you keep track of which arguments are "
"for which pointers."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2356
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node Names"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2358
msgid "@nodedescription How to choose node names."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2358
#, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing Node Names"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2360
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node names, choosing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2363
msgid ""
"The name of a node identifies the node.  For all the details of node names, "
"@pxref{Node Line Requirements})."
msgstr ""

#.  previous node name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2366
msgid "Node Line Tips"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2366
msgid "Here are some suggestions for node names:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2370
msgid "Try to pick node names that are informative but short."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2374
msgid ""
"In the Info file, the file name, node name, and pointer names are all "
"inserted on one line, which may run into the right edge of the window.  "
"(This does not cause a problem with Info, but is ugly.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2379
msgid ""
"Try to pick node names that differ from each other near the beginnings of "
"their names.  This way, it is easy to use automatic name completion in Info."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2386
msgid ""
"Conventionally, node names are capitalized in the same way as section and "
"chapter titles.  In this manual, initial and significant words are "
"capitalized; others are not.  In other manuals, just initial words and "
"proper nouns are capitalized.  Either way is fine; we recommend just being "
"consistent."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2390
msgid ""
"Node names should not contain user-defined macros.  @xref{Macro Details} for "
"other constraints on the use of macros."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2398
msgid ""
"In HTML output, any characters in the node name other than plain ASCII "
"letters, numbers or spaces will be changed in the file name.  (@xref{HTML "
"Xref Node Name Expansion}.)  This can make the URLs for the pages in your "
"manual less user-friendly; for example, in this manual the @samp{@@dots} "
"node is output as @file{__0040dots.html}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2405
msgid ""
"Because node names are used in cross-references, it is not desirable to "
"casually change them once published.  When you delete or rename a node, it "
"is usually a good idea to define an @code{@@anchor} with the old name.  That "
"way, references from other manuals, from mail archives, and so on are not "
"invalidated.  @xref{@code{@@anchor}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2408
msgid ""
"The pointers from a given node enable you to reach other nodes and consist "
"simply of the names of those nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2415
msgid ""
"Normally, a node's `Up' pointer contains the name of the node whose menu "
"mentions that node.  The node's `Next' pointer contains the name of the node "
"that follows the present node in that menu and its `Previous' pointer "
"contains the name of the node that precedes it in that menu.  When a node's "
"`Previous' node is the same as its `Up' node, both pointers name the same "
"node."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2419
msgid ""
"Usually, the first node of a Texinfo file is the `Top' node, and its `Up' "
"pointer points to the @file{dir} file, which contains the main menu for all "
"of Info."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2421
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node Line Requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2423
msgid "@nodedescription Keep names unique."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2423
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@node} Line Requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2425
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node line requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2426
#, no-wrap
msgid "Restrictions on node names"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2429
msgid "Names used with @code{@@node} have several requirements:"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2432
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unique node names requirement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2433
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node names must be unique"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2435
msgid "All the node names in a single Texinfo file must be unique."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2442
msgid ""
"This means, for example, that if you end every chapter with a summary, you "
"must name each summary node differently.  You cannot just call them all "
"``Summary''.  You may, however, duplicate the titles of chapters, sections, "
"and the like.  Thus you can end each chapter with a section called "
"``Summary'', so long as the node names for those sections are all different."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2445
msgid ""
"Node names, anchor names (@pxref{@code{@@anchor}}), and float labels "
"(@pxref{@code{@@float}}) must all be unique."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2447
#, no-wrap
msgid "Commands in node names"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2448
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{at} @@-commands @subentry in node names"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2456
msgid ""
"Node names can contain @@-commands@footnote{Prior to the Texinfo 5 release "
"in 2013, this feature was supported in an ad hoc way (the @option{--commands-"
"in-node-names} option to @command{makeinfo}).  Now it is part of the "
"language.  }.  For example, using @code{@@TeX@{@}} in a node name results in "
"the @TeX{} logo being output, as it would be in normal text.  Cross-"
"references should use @code{@@TeX@{@}} just as the node name does."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2463
msgid ""
"Some commands do not make sense in node names; for instance, environments "
"(e.g., @code{@@quotation}), commands that read a whole line as their "
"argument (e.g., @code{@@sp}), and plenty of others.  For the complete list "
"of commands that are allowed, and their expansion for HTML identifiers and "
"file names, @pxref{HTML Xref Command Expansion}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2465
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parentheses in node name"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2469
msgid ""
"A node name may not start with a left parenthesis preceding a right "
"parenthesis, as in @code{(not)allowed}, since this syntax is used to specify "
"an external manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2471
#, no-wrap
msgid "Colon in node name"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2472
#, no-wrap
msgid "Comma in node name"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2473
#, no-wrap
msgid "Period in node name"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2474
#, no-wrap
msgid "Characters, invalid in node name"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2475
#, no-wrap
msgid "Invalid characters in node names"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2476
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node names, invalid characters in"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2487
msgid "Info Node Names Constraints"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2487
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, you cannot reliably use periods, commas, or colons within a "
"node name; these can confuse some Info readers.  @command{texi2any} quotes "
"problematic node names and labels by default, but some Info readers do not "
"recognize this syntax.  Node name and label quoting causes @code{DEL} "
"characters (@samp{CTRL-?}, character number 127, often rendered as "
"@samp{^?}) to appear around the name.  To remove node names and labels "
"quoting, you can set the customization variable "
"@code{INFO_SPECIAL_CHARS_QUOTE} to @samp{0} (@pxref{Info and Plaintext "
"Customization Variables})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2493
msgid ""
"@command{texi2any} warns about such problematic usage in node names, menu "
"items, and cross-references.  If you don't want to see the warnings, you can "
"set the customization variable @code{INFO_SPECIAL_CHARS_WARNING} to @samp{0} "
"(@pxref{Info and Plaintext Customization Variables})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2498
msgid ""
"If you insist on using these characters in node names, in order not to "
"confuse the Texinfo processors you must still escape those characters, by "
"using either special insertions (@pxref{Inserting a Comma}) or @code{@@asis} "
"(@pxref{@code{@@asis}}).  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2501
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@node foo@@asis@{::@}bar@@comma@{@} baz\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2505
msgid ""
"As an example of avoiding the special characters, the following is a section "
"title in this manual:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2508
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@section @@code@{@@@@unnumbered@}, @@code@{@@@@appendix@}: Chapters with@enddots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2512
msgid "But the corresponding node name lacks the commas and the subtitle:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2515
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@node @@code@{@@@@unnumbered @@@@appendix@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2517
#, no-wrap
msgid "Case in node name"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2520
msgid "Case is significant in node names."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2521
#, no-wrap
msgid "White space in node name"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2522
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spaces in node name"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2527
msgid ""
"Spaces before and after names on the @samp{@@node} line are ignored.  "
"Multiple whitespace characters ``inside'' a name are collapsed to a single "
"space.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2534
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node foo bar\n"
"@@node  foo bar,\n"
"@@node foo bar ,\n"
"@@node foo  bar,\n"
"@@node  foo  bar ,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2548
msgid ""
"all define the same node, namely @samp{foo bar}.  In menu entries, a single "
"internal space should be used in node names or some versions of some Info "
"readers will not find the node."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2551
#, no-wrap
msgid "First Node"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2553
msgid "@nodedescription How to write a `Top' node."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2553
#, no-wrap
msgid "The First Node"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2554
#, no-wrap
msgid "Top node is first"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2555
#, no-wrap
msgid "First node"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2563
msgid ""
"The first node of a Texinfo file is the @dfn{Top} node, except in an "
"included file (@pxref{Include Files}).  The Top node should contain a short "
"summary and a master menu.  @xref{The Top Node} for more information on the "
"Top node contents and examples.  Straight text before the Top node outside "
"of any node should be avoided.  Such text, if present, is not output for "
"DocBook."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2566
msgid "Here is a description of the node pointers to be used in the Top node:"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2569
#, no-wrap
msgid "Up node of Top node"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2570
#, no-wrap
msgid "(dir) as Up node of Top node"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2574
msgid ""
"The Top node (which must be named @samp{top} or @samp{Top}) should have as "
"its `Up' node the name of a node in another file, where there is a menu that "
"leads to this file.  Specify the file name in parentheses."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2581
msgid ""
"Usually, all Info files are available through a single virtual Info tree, "
"constructed from multiple directories.  In this case, use @samp{(dir)} as "
"the parent of the Top node; this specifies the top-level node in the "
"@file{dir} file, which contains the main menu for the Info system as a "
"whole.  (Each directory with Info files is intended to contain a file named "
"@file{dir}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2590
msgid ""
"That's fine for Info, but for HTML output, one might well want the Up link "
"from the Top node to go to some specific place.  For example, for GNU the "
"natural place would be @url{https://www.gnu.org/manual/} (a web page "
"collecting links to most GNU manuals), better specified as just @code{/"
"manual/} if the manual will be installed on @code{www.gnu.org}.  This can be "
"specified with the @code{TOP_NODE_UP_URL} customization variable "
"(@pxref{File Names and Links Customization for HTML}), as in"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2593
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ @kbd{texi2any --html -c TOP_NODE_UP_URL=/manual/} ...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2599
#, no-wrap
msgid "Prev node of Top node"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2601
msgid "The `Prev' node of the Top node is usually omitted."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2603
#, no-wrap
msgid "Next node of Top node"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2606
msgid ""
"The `Next' node of the Top node should be the first chapter in your document."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2611
msgid ""
"@xref{Installing an Info File}, for more information about installing an "
"Info file in the @file{info} directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2614
msgid ""
"It is usually best to leave the pointers off entirely and let the tools "
"implicitly define them, with this simple result:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2617
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@node Top\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2620
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@top} Command"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2622
msgid "@nodedescription How to use the @code{@@top} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2622
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @code{@@top} Sectioning Command"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#.  another old name
#.  yet another name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2627
msgid "top command"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2627
msgid "makeinfo top"
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2627
msgid "makeinfo top command"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2627
#, no-wrap
msgid "top"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2632
msgid ""
"The @code{@@top} command is a special sectioning command that you should "
"only use after a @samp{@@node Top} line at the beginning of a Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2635
msgid ""
"It produces the same sort of output as @code{@@unnumbered} "
"(@pxref{@code{@@unnumbered @@appendix}}).  In @LaTeX{} @code{\\part*} is "
"used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2639
msgid ""
"@code{@@top} is ignored when raising or lowering sections.  That is, it is "
"never lowered and nothing can be raised to it (@pxref{Raise/lower sections})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2644
msgid ""
"It used to be conventional to wrap the @samp{Top} node in an "
"@code{@@ifnottex} conditional so that it would not appear in printed output "
"(@pxref{Conditionals}).  Thus, a Top node often looked like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2649
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifnottex\n"
"@@node Top\n"
"@@top @var{your-manual-title}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2652
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@var{very-high-level-summary}\n"
"@@end ifnottex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2656
msgid ""
"This is no longer necessary, as the @samp{Top} node is now never output for "
"printed output.  The @samp{Top} node is not output for DocBook either."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2658 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2660
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo Document Structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2660
msgid "@nodedescription Double structure of documents."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2661
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo document structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2662
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document structure, of Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2663
#, no-wrap
msgid "Structure, of Texinfo documents"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2664
#, no-wrap
msgid "Double structure, of Texinfo documents"
msgstr ""

#.  old node name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2667
msgid "Two Paths"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2672
msgid ""
"Nodes can contain @dfn{menus}, which contain the names of @dfn{child nodes} "
"within the parent node; for example, a node corresponding to a chapter would "
"have a menu of the sections in that chapter.  The menus allow the user to "
"move to the child nodes in the Info output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2687
msgid ""
"In addition, nodes contain @dfn{node pointers} that name other nodes.  The "
"`Next' and `Previous' pointers link nodes at the same sectioning level into "
"a chain.  As you might imagine, the `Next' pointer links to the next node, "
"and the `Previous' pointer links to the previous node.  In general, `Next' "
"and `Previous' refer to nodes at the @emph{same hierarchical level} in the "
"manual, not necessarily to the next node within the Texinfo file.  In the "
"Texinfo file, the subsequent node may be at a lower level---a section-level "
"node most often follows a chapter-level node, for example.  Thus, for "
"example, all the nodes that are at the level of sections within a chapter "
"are linked together, and the order in this chain is the same as the order of "
"the children in the menu of the parent chapter.  Each child node records the "
"parent node name as its `Up' pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2692
msgid ""
"Since the `Top' node is the only node at that level, `Next' refers to the "
"first following node, which is almost always a chapter or chapter-level "
"node.  This is an exception to the rule of `Next' being at the same "
"hierarchical level."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2693
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{accesskey} @subentry in HTML output of nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2706
msgid ""
"The Info and HTML output for each node includes links to the `Next', "
"`Previous', and `Up' nodes.  The HTML also uses the @code{accesskey} "
"attribute with the values @samp{n}, @samp{p}, and @samp{u} respectively.  "
"This allows people using web browsers to follow the navigation using "
"(typically) @kbd{M-@var{letter}}, e.g., @kbd{M-n} for the `Next' node, from "
"anywhere within the node.  Node pointers and menus provide structure for "
"Info files just as chapters, sections, subsections, and the like provide "
"structure for printed books.  The two structures are theoretically distinct; "
"in practice, however, the tree structure of printed books is essentially "
"always used for the node and menu structure also, as this leads to a "
"document which is easy to follow."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2713
msgid ""
"Typically, the sectioning structure and the node structure are completely "
"parallel, with one node for each chapter, section, etc., and with the nodes "
"following the same hierarchical arrangement as the sectioning.  Thus, if a "
"node is at the logical level of a chapter, its child nodes are at the level "
"of sections; similarly, the child nodes of sections are at the level of "
"subsections."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2720
msgid ""
"It is technically possible to create Texinfo documents with only one "
"structure or the other, or for the two structures not to be parallel, or for "
"either the sectioning or node structure to be different from the "
"conventional structure.  To the best of our knowledge, however, all the "
"Texinfo manuals currently in general use do follow the conventional parallel "
"structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2722
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node Menu Illustration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2724
msgid "@nodedescription A diagram, and sample nodes and menus."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2724
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node and Menu Illustration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2728
msgid ""
"Here is a diagram that illustrates a Texinfo file with three chapters, each "
"of which contains two sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2734
msgid ""
"The ``root'' is at the top of the diagram and the ``leaves'' are at the "
"bottom.  This is how such a diagram is drawn conventionally; it illustrates "
"an upside-down tree.  For this reason, the root node is called the `Top' "
"node, and `Up' node pointers carry you closer to the root."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2747
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                         Top\n"
"                          |\n"
"        -------------------------------------\n"
"       |                  |                  |\n"
"    Chapter 1          Chapter 2          Chapter 3\n"
"       |                  |                  |\n"
"    --------           --------           --------\n"
"   |        |         |        |         |        |\n"
"Section  Section   Section  Section   Section  Section\n"
"  1.1      1.2       2.1      2.2       3.1      3.2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2753
msgid ""
"Using explicit pointers (not recommended, but shown for purposes of the "
"example), the fully-written command to start Chapter@tie{}2 would be this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2758
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node     Chapter 2,  Chapter 3, Chapter 1, Top\n"
"@@comment  node-name,  next,      previous,  up\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2768
msgid ""
"This @code{@@node} line says that the name of this node is ``Chapter@tie{}"
"2'', the name of the `Next' node is ``Chapter 3'', the name of the "
"`Previous' node is ``Chapter@tie{}1'', and the name of the `Up' node is "
"``Top''.  You can (and should) omit writing out these node names if your "
"document is hierarchically organized, but the pointer relationships still "
"obtain."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2772
msgid ""
"To go to Sections 2.1 and 2.2 using Info, you need a menu inside Chapter 2.  "
"(@xref{Menus}.)  You would write the menu just before the beginning of "
"Section 2.1, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2779
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   @@menu\n"
"   * Sect. 2.1::    Description of this section.\n"
"   * Sect. 2.2::    Description.\n"
"   @@end menu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2783
msgid "The automatic pointers for the node for Sect.@: 2.1 correspond to:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2788
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node     Sect. 2.1, Sect. 2.2, ,         Chapter 2\n"
"@@comment  node-name, next,      previous, up\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2793
msgid ""
"Note that no `Prev' pointer is generated, since there is no other node at "
"the same hierarchical level before Sect.@: 2.1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2795
msgid ""
"Using explicit pointers, the node for Sect.@: 2.1 could be written like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2800
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node     Sect. 2.1, Sect. 2.2, Chapter 2, Chapter 2\n"
"@@comment  node-name, next,      previous,  up\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2812
msgid ""
"With automatic pointers, the `Next' and `Previous' pointers of a node lead "
"to other nodes at the same level---from chapter to chapter or from section "
"to section.  As shown, when using explicit pointers, the pointers can also "
"lead somewhere else, here, for example, the `Previous' pointer points up.  "
"An `Up' pointer usually leads to a node at the level above (closer to the "
"`Top' node; and a `Menu' leads to nodes at a level below (closer to "
"`leaves').  (A cross-reference can point to a node at any level; see "
"@ref{Cross References}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2818
msgid ""
"Technically, explicit node pointers can carry you to any node, regardless of "
"the structure of the document; even to nodes in a different Info file.  "
"However, it would be very confusing for readers to have the `Next', "
"`Previous' and `Up' pointers lead to nodes that do not correspond, even "
"loosely, to the next, previous and up node."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2826
msgid ""
"A @code{@@node} command and a chapter structuring command are conventionally "
"used together, in that order, often followed by indexing commands.  (As "
"shown in the example above, you may follow the @code{@@node} line with a "
"comment line, e.g., to show which pointer is which if explicit pointers are "
"used.)  The Texinfo processors use this construct to determine the "
"relationships between nodes and sectioning commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2830
msgid ""
"Here is the beginning of the chapter in this manual called ``Ending a "
"Texinfo File''.  This shows a @code{@@node} line followed by a "
"@code{@@chapter} line, and then by indexing lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2838
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Ending a File\n"
"@@chapter Ending a Texinfo File\n"
"@@cindex Ending a Texinfo file\n"
"@@cindex Texinfo file ending\n"
"@@cindex File ending\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2842 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2846
#, no-wrap
msgid "Node Descriptions"
msgstr ""

#. type: nodedescriptionblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2845
msgid "@code{@@nodedescription} and @code{@@nodedescriptionblock}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2848
#, no-wrap
msgid "nodedescription"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2853
msgid ""
"You can provide a short description of the purpose of a node by using the "
"@code{@@nodedescription} command following the @code{@@node} line.  Such a "
"description might elaborate on or extend the information in the node name "
"itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2854
#, no-wrap
msgid "nodedescriptionblock"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2857
msgid ""
"You can also use a @code{@@nodedescriptionblock} environment to provide a "
"node description. This may be useful for longer descriptions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2864
msgid ""
"@command{texi2any} uses the content you provide with these commands when "
"outputting menus for Info output format (and, optionally, for HTML).  "
"@command{texi2any} uses the description after a menu entry for the node if "
"it is generating the menu automatically, or if no description for the menu "
"entry was provided in an explicit @code{@@menu} block. (@xref{Menus})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2866
msgid "Here is an example of using these commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2870
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Tools\n"
"@@chapter Tools\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2872
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This chapter is on different tools you can use.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2876
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Screwdrivers\n"
"@@nodedescription Flathead and Phillips.\n"
"@@section Screwdrivers\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2878
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This section is about screwdrivers.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2885
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Drills\n"
"@@nodedescriptionblock\n"
"Making holes in things with power screwdrivers, drill drivers, combi\n"
"drills, impact drivers, hammer drills, breakers and demolition drills.\n"
"@@end nodedescriptionblock\n"
"@@section Drills\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2887
#, no-wrap
msgid "This section is about drills.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2891
msgid ""
"In Info output, @command{texi2any} would output the @samp{Tools} node with a "
"menu as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2894 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3010
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Menu:\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2900
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Screwdrivers::          Flathead and Phillips.\n"
"* Drills::                Making holes in things with power\n"
"                          screwdrivers, drill drivers, combi\n"
"                          drills, impact drivers, hammer drills,\n"
"                          breakers and demolition drills.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2903 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2905
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2906
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2905
msgid "@nodedescription Listing subordinate nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2907
#, no-wrap
msgid "menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2914
msgid ""
"@dfn{Menus} contain pointers to subordinate nodes.  In Info output, you use "
"menus to go to such nodes.  @command{texi2any} can output menus in HTML "
"output, but does not do so by default (@pxref{HTML Output Structure "
"Customization}, @code{FORMAT_MENU}).  Menus have no role in printed manuals "
"or other output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2918
msgid ""
"Menus are automatically generated by @command{texi2any} when outputting Info "
"or HTML with menus for nodes followed by a sectioning command, without an "
"explicit @code{@@menu} block, and with automatic pointers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2926
msgid ""
"It is often more convenient to let @command{texi2any} generate menus for "
"you, as you do not then have the burden of updating menu blocks in your "
"Texinfo source when you add, remove, or relocate nodes.  In the usual case "
"of a hierarchically organized manual with sectioning commands associated "
"with nodes, and with node pointers left out, you should only write menus if "
"you want exact control over the contents and formatting of menus in Info."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2928 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2930
#, no-wrap
msgid "Writing a Menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2930
msgid "@nodedescription What is a menu?"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2931
#, no-wrap
msgid "Writing a menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2932
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu writing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2937
msgid ""
"A menu consists of a @code{@@menu} command on a line by itself, followed by "
"menu entry lines or menu comment lines, and then followed by an @code{@@end "
"menu} command on a line by itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2939
msgid "A menu looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2944 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3023
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@menu\n"
"Larger Units of Text\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2949 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3028
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Files::                       All about handling files.\n"
"* Multiples: Buffers.           Multiple buffers; editing\n"
"                                 several files at once.\n"
"@@end menu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2952
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spaces, in menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2956
msgid ""
"In a menu, every line that begins with an @w{@samp{* }} is a @dfn{menu "
"entry}.  (Note the space after the asterisk.)  In the example above, the two "
"lines starting with @w{@samp{* }} are menu entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2957
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu comment"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2963
msgid ""
"A line that does not start with an @w{@samp{* }} may also appear in a menu.  "
"Such a line is not a menu entry but rather a @dfn{menu comment} line.  In "
"the example above, the line @samp{Larger Units of Text} is a menu comment "
"line.  Menu comment lines and any empty lines are kept in the output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2964
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hierarchical documents, and menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2972
msgid ""
"Technically, menus can carry you to any node, regardless of the structure of "
"the document; even to nodes in a different Info file.  However, the "
"@command{texi2any} implicit pointer creation feature (@pxref{Writing a "
"Node}) and GNU Emacs Texinfo mode updating commands work only to create "
"menus of subordinate nodes in a hierarchically structured document.  In a "
"hierarchically structured document, it is much better to use cross-"
"references to refer to arbitrary nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2976
msgid ""
"In Info, a user selects a node with the @kbd{m} (@code{Info-menu})  "
"command.  The menu entry name is what the user types after the @kbd{m} "
"command."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2976
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{accesskey} @subentry in HTML output of menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2982
msgid ""
"In the HTML output, the @code{accesskey} attribute is used with the values "
"@samp{1}@dots{}@samp{9} for the first nine entries.  This allows people "
"using web browsers to follow the first menu entries using (typically) @kbd{M-"
"@var{digit}}, e.g., @kbd{M-1} for the first entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2984
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2986
msgid "@nodedescription Two and three part menu entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2986
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Menu Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2987
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu example"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2988
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:2993
msgid "A menu looks like this in Texinfo:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3000
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@menu\n"
"* menu entry name: Node name.   A short description.\n"
"* Node name::                   This form is preferred.\n"
"@@end menu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3006 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3034
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4522 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4584
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5199 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5329
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5447 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5882
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6990 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7048
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7114 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7142
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7274 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7361
msgid "This produces:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3013
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* menu entry name: Node name.   A short description.\n"
"* Node name::                   This form is preferred.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3018
msgid "Here is an example as you might see it in a Texinfo file:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3039
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Menu:\n"
"Larger Units of Text\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3043
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Files::                       All about handling files.\n"
"* Multiples: Buffers.           Multiple buffers; editing\n"
"                                 several files at once.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3051
msgid ""
"In this example, the menu has two entries.  @samp{Files} is both a menu "
"entry name and the name of the node referred to by that name.  "
"@samp{Multiples} is the menu entry name; it refers to the node named "
"@samp{Buffers}. The line @samp{Larger Units of Text} is a comment; it "
"appears in the menu, but is not an entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3055
msgid ""
"Since no file name is specified with either @samp{Files} or @samp{Buffers}, "
"they must be the names of nodes in the same Info file (@pxref{Other Info "
"Files, , Referring to Other Info Files})."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3057 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3059
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu Location"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3059
msgid "@nodedescription Menus go at the ends of nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3060
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu location"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3061
#, no-wrap
msgid "Location of menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3067
msgid ""
"There may be at most one menu in a node.  A menu is conventionally located "
"at the end of a node, without any regular text or additional commands "
"between the @code{@@end menu} and the beginning of the next node."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3068
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info format, and menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3074
msgid ""
"This convention is useful, since a reader who uses the menu could easily "
"miss any such text.  Also, any such post-menu text will be considered part "
"of the menu in Info output (which has no marker for the end of a menu).  "
"Thus, a line beginning with @samp{* } will likely be incorrectly handled."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3079
msgid ""
"It's usually best if a node with a menu does not contain much text.  If you "
"find yourself with a lot of text before a menu, we generally recommend "
"moving all but a couple of paragraphs into a new subnode.  Otherwise, it is "
"easy for readers to miss the menu."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3081
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu Parts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3083
msgid "@nodedescription A menu entry has three parts."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3083
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Parts of a Menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3084
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parts of a menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3085
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu parts"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3086
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@menu} parts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3089
msgid "A menu entry has three parts, only the second of which is required:"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3093
msgid "The menu entry name (optional)."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3096
msgid "The name of the node (required)."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3099
msgid "A description of the item (optional)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3103
msgid ""
"The template for a generic menu entry looks like this (but see the next "
"section for one more possibility):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3106
#, no-wrap
msgid "* @var{menu-entry-name}: @var{node-name}.   @var{description}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3111
msgid ""
"Follow the menu entry name with a single colon, and follow the node name "
"with tab, comma, newline, or the two characters period and space (@samp{. })."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3120
msgid ""
"The third part of a menu entry is a descriptive phrase or sentence.  Menu "
"entry names and node names are often short; the description explains to the "
"reader what the node is about.  A useful description complements the node "
"name rather than repeats it.  The description, which is optional, can spread "
"over multiple lines; if it does, some authors prefer to indent the second "
"line while others prefer to align it with the first (and all others).  It's "
"up to you.  An empty line, or the next menu entry, ends a description."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3127
msgid ""
"Space characters in a menu are preserved as-is in the Info output; this "
"allows you to format the menu as you wish.  Unfortunately you must type node "
"names without any extra spaces or some versions of some Info readers will "
"not find the node (@pxref{Node Line Requirements})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3131
msgid ""
"@command{texi2any} warns and protect names when the text of a menu item (and "
"node names and cross-references) contains a problematic construct that could "
"interfere with its parsing in Info.  @xref{Info Node Names Constraints}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3133 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3135
#, no-wrap
msgid "Less Cluttered Menu Entry"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3135
msgid "@nodedescription Two part menu entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3136
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two part menu entry"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3137
#, no-wrap
msgid "Double-colon menu entries"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3138
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menu entries with two colons"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3139
#, no-wrap
msgid "Less cluttered menu entry"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3140
#, no-wrap
msgid "Uncluttered menu entry"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3145
msgid ""
"When the menu entry name and node name are the same, you can write the name "
"immediately after the asterisk and space at the beginning of the line and "
"follow the name with two colons."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3148
msgid "For example, write"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3151
#, no-wrap
msgid "* Name::                        @var{description}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3156
msgid "instead of"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3159
#, no-wrap
msgid "* Name: Name.                   @var{description}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3163
msgid ""
"We recommend using the node name for the menu entry name whenever possible, "
"since it reduces visual clutter in the menu."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3165
#, no-wrap
msgid "Other Info Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3167
msgid "@nodedescription How to refer to a different Info file."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3167
#, no-wrap
msgid "Referring to Other Info Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3168
#, no-wrap
msgid "Referring to other Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3169
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes in other Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3170
#, no-wrap
msgid "Other Info files' nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3171
#, no-wrap
msgid "Going to other Info files' nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3172
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info; other files' nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3177
msgid ""
"You can create a menu entry that enables a reader in Info to go to a node in "
"another Info file by writing the file name in parentheses just before the "
"node name.  Some examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3184
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@menu\n"
"* @var{first-entry-name}:(@var{filename})@var{nodename}.     @var{description}\n"
"* (@var{filename})@var{second-node}::                  @var{description}\n"
"@@end menu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3190
msgid ""
"For example, to refer directly to the @samp{Outlining} and @samp{Rebinding} "
"nodes in the @cite{Emacs Manual}, you could write a menu like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3199
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@menu\n"
"* Outlining: (emacs)Outline Mode. The major mode for\n"
"                                   editing outlines.\n"
"* (emacs)Rebinding::              How to redefine the\n"
"                                   meaning of a key.\n"
"@@end menu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3204
msgid ""
"If you do not list the node name, but only name the file, then Info presumes "
"that you are referring to the `Top' node.  Examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3210
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Info: (info).         Documentation browsing system.\n"
"* (emacs)::             The extensible, self-documenting\n"
"                         text editor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3217
msgid ""
"The GNU Emacs Texinfo mode menu updating commands only work with nodes "
"within the current buffer, so you cannot use them to create menus that refer "
"to other files.  You must write such menus by hand.  @xref{Updating Nodes "
"and Menus}."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3219 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3221
#, no-wrap
msgid "Chapter Structuring"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3221
msgid "@nodedescription Creating chapters, sections, appendices, etc."
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3223
msgid "Structuring"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3223
#, no-wrap
msgid "Chapter structuring"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3224
#, no-wrap
msgid "Structuring of chapters"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3225
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sectioning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3232
msgid ""
"Texinfo's @dfn{chapter structuring} commands divide a document into a "
"hierarchy of chapters, sections, subsections, and subsubsections.  These "
"commands generate large headings in the text, like the one above.  They also "
"provide information for generating the table of contents (@pxref{Contents,, "
"Generating a Table of Contents})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3235
msgid ""
"Normally you put a @code{@@node} command immediately before each chapter "
"structuring command.  @xref{Nodes}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3237
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tree Structuring"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3239
msgid "@nodedescription A manual is like an upside down tree @dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3239
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tree Structure of Sections"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3240
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tree structuring"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3247
msgid ""
"A Texinfo file is usually structured like a book with chapters, sections, "
"subsections, and the like.  This structure can be visualized as a tree (or "
"rather as an upside-down tree) with the root at the top and the levels "
"corresponding to chapters, sections, subsection, and subsubsections."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3250
msgid ""
"Here is a diagram that shows a Texinfo file with three chapters, each with "
"two sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3263
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"                         Top\n"
"                          |\n"
"        -------------------------------------\n"
"       |                  |                  |\n"
"    Chapter 1          Chapter 2          Chapter 3\n"
"       |                  |                  |\n"
"    --------           --------           --------\n"
"   |        |         |        |         |        |\n"
"Section  Section   Section  Section   Section  Section\n"
"  1.1      1.2       2.1      2.2       3.1      3.2\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3269
msgid ""
"In a Texinfo file that has this structure, the beginning of Chapter 2 would "
"be written like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3274
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node    Chapter 2\n"
"@@chapter Chapter 2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3280
msgid ""
"For purposes of example, here is how it would be written with explicit node "
"pointers:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3285
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node    Chapter 2,  Chapter 3, Chapter 1, Top\n"
"@@chapter Chapter 2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3291
msgid ""
"The chapter structuring commands are described in the sections that follow; "
"the @code{@@node} command is described in the previous chapter "
"(@pxref{Nodes})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3293 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3294
#, no-wrap
msgid "Structuring Command Types"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3299
msgid ""
"The chapter structuring commands fall into four groups, each of which "
"contains structuring commands at the levels of chapters, sections, "
"subsections, and subsubsections:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3305
msgid ""
"The @code{@@chapter}-like commands and @code{@@appendix}-like commands "
"produce numbered or lettered entries both in the body of a document and in "
"its table of contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3312
msgid ""
"The @code{@@unnumbered}-like commands produce unnumbered entries both in the "
"body of a document and in its table of contents.  The @code{@@top} command, "
"which has a special use, is a member of this group (@pxref{@code{@@top} "
"Command}).  An @code{@@unnumbered} section is a normal part of the document "
"structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3318
msgid ""
"The @code{@@heading}-like commands produce simple unnumbered headings that "
"do not appear in a table of contents, are not associated with nodes, and "
"cannot be cross-referenced.  These heading commands never start a new page."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3325
msgid ""
"In printed output, the chapter structuring commands produce headings in the "
"document.  When a @code{@@setchapternewpage} command says to do so, the "
"@code{@@chapter}, @code{@@unnumbered}, and @code{@@appendix} commands start "
"new pages in the printed manual; the @code{@@heading} commands do not.  "
"@xref{@code{@@setchapternewpage}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3330
msgid ""
"In Info and plain text output, the command causes the title to appear on a "
"line by itself, with a line of an ASCII character (@samp{*}, @samp{=}, "
"@dots{}) inserted underneath.  For example, the ``Chapter Structuring'' "
"heading could be:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3335
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"5 Chapter Structuring\n"
"*********************\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3343
msgid ""
"The underlining character is the same for all the commands at the same "
"level.  For instance, it is the same for the chapter-level commands "
"@code{@@chapter}, @code{@@apppendix}, @code{@@unnumbered} and "
"@code{@@chapheading}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3348
msgid ""
"In HTML, the chapter-level commands produce an @code{<h2>}-level header by "
"default (controlled by the @code{CHAPTER_HEADER_LEVEL} customization "
"variable, @pxref{HTML Customization of Specific Output}).  The heading "
"element level is adjusted for the other commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3354
msgid ""
"In the DocBook output, the appropriate level of element is used.  The "
"produced element includes all following sections up to the next command at "
"the same or higher level.  For example, a @code{<chapter>} element is "
"produced for @code{@@chapter}, and contains any sections or subsections in "
"the chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3356 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7155
msgid "Here is a summary:"
msgstr ""

#. type: tex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3359
#, no-wrap
msgid "{\\globaldefs=1 \\smallfonts \\rm}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3362
#, no-wrap
msgid "@tab                              @tab                       @tab No new page"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3363
#, no-wrap
msgid "@i{Numbered}           @tab @i{Unnumbered}               @tab @i{Lettered/numbered} @tab @i{Unnumbered}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3364
#, no-wrap
msgid "In contents            @tab In contents                  @tab In contents           @tab Not in contents"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3365
#, no-wrap
msgid "@tab @code{@@top}                 @tab                       @tab @code{@@majorheading}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3366
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@chapter}       @tab @code{@@unnumbered}          @tab @code{@@appendix} @tab @code{@@chapheading}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3367
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@section}       @tab @code{@@unnumberedsec}       @tab @code{@@appendixsec} @tab @code{@@heading}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3368
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@subsection}    @tab @code{@@unnumberedsubsec}    @tab @code{@@appendixsubsec} @tab @code{@@subheading}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3369
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@subsubsection} @tab @code{@@unnumberedsubsubsec} @tab @code{@@appendixsubsubsec} @tab @code{@@subsubheading}"
msgstr ""

#. type: tex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3373
#, no-wrap
msgid "{\\globaldefs=1 \\textfonts \\rm}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3376
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@chapter}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3377
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@chapter}: Chapter Structuring"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3380 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:15443
#, no-wrap
msgid "chapter"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3387
msgid ""
"@code{@@chapter} identifies a chapter in the document--the highest level of "
"the normal document structuring hierarchy.  Write the command at the "
"beginning of a line and follow it on the same line by the title of the "
"chapter.  The chapter is numbered automatically, starting from@tie{}1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3391
msgid ""
"For example, the present chapter in this manual is entitled ``Chapter "
"Structuring''; the @code{@@chapter} line looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3394
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@chapter Chapter Structuring\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3398
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@unnumbered @@appendix}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3399
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@unnumbered}, @code{@@appendix}: Chapters with Other Labeling"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3402
msgid "unnumbered & appendix"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3402
#, no-wrap
msgid "unnumbered"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3403
#, no-wrap
msgid "appendix"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3410
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@unnumbered} command to start a chapter-level element that "
"appears without chapter numbers of any kind.  Use the @code{@@appendix} "
"command to start an appendix that is labeled by letter (`A', `B', @dots{}) "
"instead of by number; appendices are also at the chapter level of "
"structuring."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3414
msgid ""
"Write an @code{@@appendix} or @code{@@unnumbered} command at the beginning "
"of a line and follow it on the same line by the title, just as with "
"@code{@@chapter}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3415
#, no-wrap
msgid "centerchap"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3423
msgid ""
"Texinfo also provides a command @code{@@centerchap}, which is analogous to "
"@code{@@unnumbered}, but centers its argument in the printed and HTML "
"outputs.  This kind of stylistic choice is not usually offered by Texinfo.  "
"You are recommended not to use this command, as it may be removed in future "
"releases of Texinfo."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3424
#, no-wrap
msgid "DocBook and prefatory sections"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3425
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preface, etc., and DocBook"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3428
msgid ""
"With @code{@@unnumbered}, if the name of the associated node is one of these "
"English words (case-insensitive):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3431
#, no-wrap
msgid "Acknowledgements  Colophon  Dedication  Preface\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3433
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<acknowledgements>} DocBook tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3434
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<colophon>} DocBook tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3435
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<dedication>} DocBook tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3436
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<preface>} DocBook tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3437
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<chapter>} DocBook tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3443
msgid ""
"then the DocBook output uses corresponding special tags (@code{<preface>}, "
"etc.)@: instead of the default @code{<chapter>}. The argument to "
"@code{@@unnumbered} itself can be anything, and is output as the element "
"title as usual."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3445
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@majorheading @@chapheading}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3446
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@majorheading}, @code{@@chapheading}: Chapter-level Headings"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3449
msgid "majorheading & chapheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3449
#, no-wrap
msgid "majorheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3450
#, no-wrap
msgid "chapheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3454
msgid ""
"The @code{@@majorheading} and @code{@@chapheading} commands produce chapter-"
"like headings in the body of a document."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3458
msgid ""
"However, neither command produces an entry in the table of contents, and "
"neither command causes @TeX{} to start a new page in a printed manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3462
msgid ""
"In @TeX{}, a @code{@@majorheading} command generates a larger vertical "
"whitespace before the heading than a @code{@@chapheading} command but is "
"otherwise the same."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3467
msgid ""
"In other output formats, the @code{@@majorheading} and @code{@@chapheading} "
"commands produce a similar output to @code{@@chapter}.  The difference is "
"the lack of numbering and the lack of any association with nodes.  "
"@xref{@code{@@chapter}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3469
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@section}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3470
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@section}: Sections Below Chapters"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3473 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:15447
#, no-wrap
msgid "section"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3482
msgid ""
"An @code{@@section} command identifies a section within a chapter unit, "
"whether created with @code{@@chapter}, @code{@@unnumbered}, or "
"@code{@@appendix}, following the numbering scheme of the chapter-level "
"command.  Thus, within a @code{@@chapter} chapter numbered `1', the sections "
"are numbered `1.1', `1.2', etc.; within an @code{@@appendix} ``chapter'' "
"labeled `A', the sections are numbered `A.1', `A.2', etc.; within an "
"@code{@@unnumbered} chapter, the section gets no number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3486
msgid ""
"To make a section, write the @code{@@section} command at the beginning of a "
"line and follow it on the same line by the section title.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3489
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@section This is a section\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3492
msgid "Section titles are listed in the table of contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3494
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@unnumberedsec @@appendixsec @@heading}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3495
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@unnumberedsec}, @code{@@appendixsec}, @code{@@heading}"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3498
msgid "unnumberedsec appendixsec heading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3498
#, no-wrap
msgid "unnumberedsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3499
#, no-wrap
msgid "appendixsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3500
#, no-wrap
msgid "heading"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3506
msgid ""
"The @code{@@unnumberedsec}, @code{@@appendixsec}, and @code{@@heading} "
"commands are, respectively, the unnumbered, appendix-like, and heading-like "
"equivalents of the @code{@@section} command (see the previous section)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3511
msgid ""
"@code{@@unnumberedsec} and @code{@@appendixsec} do not need to be used in "
"ordinary circumstances, because @code{@@section} may also be used within "
"@code{@@unnumbered} and @code{@@appendix} chapters; again, see the previous "
"section."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3513
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@unnumberedsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3517
msgid ""
"The @code{@@unnumberedsec} command may be used within an unnumbered chapter "
"or within a regular chapter or appendix to produce an unnumbered section."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3518
#, no-wrap
msgid "appendixsection"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3519
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@appendixsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3520
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@appendixsection"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3523
msgid ""
"@code{@@appendixsection} is a longer spelling of the @code{@@appendixsec} "
"command; the two are synonymous."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3526
msgid ""
"Conventionally, the @code{@@appendixsec} or @code{@@appendixsection} command "
"is used only within appendices."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3527
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@heading"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3533
msgid ""
"You may use the @code{@@heading} command (almost) anywhere for a section-"
"style heading that will not appear in the table of contents.  The "
"@code{@@heading}-series commands can appear inside most environments, for "
"example, though pathological and useless locations such as an argument to "
"another command, etc., are not allowed."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3537
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@subsection}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3538
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@subsection}: Subsections Below Sections"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3541
#, no-wrap
msgid "subsection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3545
msgid ""
"Subsections are to sections as sections are to chapters; "
"@pxref{@code{@@section}}.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3548
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@subsection This is a subsection\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3551
msgid "Subsection titles are listed in the table of contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3553
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@unnumberedsubsec @@appendixsubsec @@subheading}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3554
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @code{@@subsection}-like Commands"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3557
msgid "unnumberedsubsec appendixsubsec subheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3557
#, no-wrap
msgid "unnumberedsubsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3558
#, no-wrap
msgid "appendixsubsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3559
#, no-wrap
msgid "subheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3560
#, no-wrap
msgid "Subsection-like commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3566
msgid ""
"The @code{@@unnumberedsubsec}, @code{@@appendixsubsec}, and "
"@code{@@subheading} commands are, respectively, the unnumbered, appendix-"
"like, and heading-like equivalents of the @code{@@subsection} command.  "
"(@xref{@code{@@subsection}}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3571
msgid ""
"@code{@@unnumberedsubsec} and @code{@@appendixsubsec} do not need to be used "
"in ordinary circumstances, because @code{@@subsection} may also be used "
"within sections of @code{@@unnumbered} and @code{@@appendix} chapters "
"(@pxref{@code{@@section}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3579
msgid ""
"An @code{@@subheading} command produces a heading like that of a subsection "
"except that it is not numbered and does not appear in the table of "
"contents.  Similarly, an @code{@@unnumberedsubsec} command produces an "
"unnumbered heading like that of a subsection and an @code{@@appendixsubsec} "
"command produces a subsection-like heading labeled with a letter and "
"numbers; both of these commands produce headings that appear in the table of "
"contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3581
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@subsubsection}"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3582
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@subsubsection} and Other Subsub Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3585
#, no-wrap
msgid "subsubsection"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3586
#, no-wrap
msgid "unnumberedsubsubsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3587
#, no-wrap
msgid "appendixsubsubsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3588
#, no-wrap
msgid "subsubheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3589
#, no-wrap
msgid "Subsub sectioning commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3593
msgid ""
"The fourth and lowest level sectioning commands in Texinfo are the `subsub' "
"commands.  They are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3595
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@subsubsection"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3599
msgid ""
"Subsubsections are to subsections as subsections are to sections.  "
"(@xref{@code{@@subsection}}.)  Subsubsection titles appear in the table of "
"contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3600
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@unnumberedsubsubsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3604
msgid ""
"Unnumbered subsubsection titles appear in the table of contents, but lack "
"numbers.  Otherwise, unnumbered subsubsections are the same as "
"subsubsections."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3605
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@appendixsubsubsec"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3609
msgid ""
"Conventionally, appendix commands are used only for appendices and are "
"lettered and numbered appropriately.  They also appear in the table of "
"contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3610
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@subsubheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3613
msgid ""
"The @code{@@subsubheading} command may be used anywhere that you want a "
"small heading that will not appear in the table of contents."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3620
msgid ""
"As with subsections, @code{@@unnumberedsubsubsec} and "
"@code{@@appendixsubsubsec} do not need to be used in ordinary circumstances, "
"because @code{@@subsubsection} may also be used within subsections of "
"@code{@@unnumbered} and @code{@@appendix} chapters "
"(@pxref{@code{@@section}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3622
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@part}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3624
msgid "@nodedescription Collections of chapters."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3624
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@part}: Groups of Chapters"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3625
#, no-wrap
msgid "part"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3626
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part pages"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3632
msgid ""
"The final sectioning command is @code{@@part}, to mark a @dfn{part} of a "
"manual, that is, a group of chapters or (rarely) appendices.  This behaves "
"quite differently from the other sectioning commands, to fit with the way "
"such ``parts'' are conventionally used in books."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3637
msgid ""
"No @code{@@node} command is associated with @code{@@part}.  Just write the "
"command on a line by itself, including the part title, at the place in the "
"document you want to mark off as starting that part.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3640
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@part Part I:@@* The beginning\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3644
msgid ""
"As can be inferred from this example, no automatic numbering or labeling of "
"the @code{@@part} text is done.  The text is taken as-is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3649
msgid ""
"Because parts are not associated with nodes, no general text can follow the "
"@code{@@part} line.  To produce the intended output, it must be followed by "
"a chapter-level command (including its node).  Thus, to continue the example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3652
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@part Part I:@@* The beginning\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3656
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Introduction\n"
"@@chapter Introduction\n"
"...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3668
msgid ""
"In the @TeX{} output, the @code{@@part} text is included in both the normal "
"and short tables of contents (@pxref{Contents}), without a page number "
"(since that is the normal convention).  In addition, a ``part page'' is "
"output in the body of the document, with just the @code{@@part} text.  In "
"the example above, the @code{@@*} causes a line break on the part page (but "
"is replaced with a space in the tables of contents).  This part page is "
"always forced to be on an odd (right-hand) page, regardless of the chapter "
"pagination (@pxref{@code{@@setchapternewpage}}).  In the @LaTeX{} output, "
"the @code{@@part} is output as @code{\\part}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3672
msgid ""
"In the HTML output, the @code{@@part} text is similarly included in the "
"tables of contents, and a heading is included in the main document text, as "
"part of the following chapter or appendix node."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3676
msgid ""
"In the DocBook output, the @code{<part>} element includes all the following "
"chapters, up to the next @code{<part>}.  A @code{<part>} containing chapters "
"is also closed at an appendix."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3678
msgid "In the Info and plain text output, @code{@@part} has no effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3681
msgid ""
"@code{@@part} is ignored when raising or lowering sections (see next "
"section).  That is, it is never lowered and nothing can be raised to it."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3683
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raise/lower sections"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3685
msgid "@nodedescription How to change commands' hierarchical level."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3685
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raise/lower Sections: @code{@@raisesections} and @code{@@lowersections}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3686
#, no-wrap
msgid "raisesections"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3687
#, no-wrap
msgid "lowersections"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3688
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raising and lowering sections"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3689
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lowering and raising sections"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3690
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sections, raising and lowering"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3695
msgid ""
"The @code{@@raisesections} and @code{@@lowersections} commands implicitly "
"raise and lower the hierarchical level of following chapters, sections and "
"the other sectioning commands (excluding parts)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3701
msgid ""
"That is, the @code{@@raisesections} command changes sections to chapters, "
"subsections to sections, and so on.  Conversely, the @code{@@lowersections} "
"command changes chapters to sections, sections to subsections, and so on.  "
"Thus, a @code{@@lowersections} command cancels a @code{@@raisesections} "
"command, and vice versa."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3702
#, no-wrap
msgid "Include files, and section levels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3708
msgid ""
"As a practical matter, you generally only want to raise or lower large "
"chunks, usually in external files.  You can use @code{@@lowersections} to "
"include text written as an outer or standalone Texinfo file in another "
"Texinfo file as an inner, included file (@pxref{Include Files}).  Typical "
"usage looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3713
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@lowersections\n"
"@@include somefile.texi\n"
"@@raisesections\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3718
msgid ""
"(Without the @code{@@raisesections}, all the subsequent sections in the main "
"file would also be lowered)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3722
msgid ""
"If the included file being lowered has a @code{@@top} node, you'll need to "
"conditionalize its inclusion with a flag (@pxref{@code{@@set @@value}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3728
msgid ""
"Any menus in the final result have to take the raising and lowering into "
"account, so arbitrarily sprinkling @code{@@raisesections} and "
"@code{@@lowersections} commands throughout the document will likely lead to "
"errors (unless the menus in your document are all generated automatically)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3736
msgid ""
"Repeated use of the commands continues to raise or lower the hierarchical "
"level a step at a time.  An attempt to raise above `chapter' reproduces "
"chapter commands; an attempt to lower below `subsubsection' reproduces "
"subsubsection commands.  Also, lowered subsubsections and raised chapters "
"will not work with @command{texi2any}'s feature of implicitly determining "
"node pointers, since the menu structure cannot be represented correctly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3739
msgid ""
"Write each @code{@@raisesections} and @code{@@lowersections} command on a "
"line of its own."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3741 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3743
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unstructured Nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3743
msgid "@nodedescription Nodes outside the main document structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3745
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unstructured nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3748
msgid ""
"All the examples of nodes in this chapter so far have used a @code{@@node} "
"line followed by a structuring command (such as @code{@@chapter})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3754
msgid ""
"However, you can also create nodes without using a structuring command.  You "
"may find this useful if you do not want your manual (or parts of a manual) "
"to have a hierarchical structure, with chapters superior to sections, "
"sections superior to sub-sections and so on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3763
msgid ""
"To do this, follow the @code{@@node} line with one of the heading commands.  "
"For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3768
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Topic\n"
"@@heading All about the Topic\n"
"@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3774
msgid ""
"The argument to the heading command will be printed as usual.  When the "
"@code{xrefautomaticsectiontitle} flag is on "
"(@pxref{@code{xrefautomaticsectiontitle}}), this argument will also be used "
"as the text for any cross-references to the node."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3779
msgid ""
"Such nodes are not part of a hierarchical document structure.  This means "
"that they are not listed in any table of contents, and automatic node "
"direction pointers (@samp{Next}, @samp{Prev} or @samp{Up}; @pxref{Writing a "
"Node}) do not link to or from these nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3780
#, no-wrap
msgid "xrefname"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3784
msgid ""
"If you wish to create an unstructured node @emph{without} printing a heading "
"at the start, you can use the @code{@@xrefname} command in place of the "
"heading command.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3789
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Topic\n"
"@@xrefname All about the Topic\n"
"@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3795
msgid ""
"The @code{@@xrefname} command provides the text for cross-references to the "
"node when the @code{xrefautomaticsectiontitle} flag is on, so "
"@samp{@@xref@{Topic@}} would produce output like ``See `All about the "
"Topic@w{'}''."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3798
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross References"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3800
msgid "@nodedescription Writing cross-references."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3800 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3802
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3801
#, no-wrap
msgid "Making cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3803
#, no-wrap
msgid "References"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3807
msgid ""
"@dfn{Cross-references} are used to refer the reader to other parts of the "
"same or different Texinfo files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3815
msgid ""
"Use cross-references to provide access to information that is too detailed "
"for the current context, or incidental to it.  An online help system or a "
"reference manual is not like a novel; few read such documents in sequence "
"from beginning to end.  Instead, people look up what they need.  For this "
"reason, such creations should contain many cross-references to help readers "
"find other information that they may not have read."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3824
msgid ""
"In a printed manual, a cross-reference results in a page reference, unless "
"it is to another manual altogether, in which case the cross-reference names "
"that manual.  In Info, a cross-reference results in an entry that you can "
"follow using the Info @samp{f} command.  (@xref{Help-Xref,, Following cross-"
"references, info, Info}.)  In HTML, a cross-reference results in an "
"hyperlink.  In DocBook, the @code{<link>} element is used for cross-"
"references unless it is to another manual, in which case the cross-reference "
"names that manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3833
msgid ""
"The various cross-reference commands use nodes, anchors "
"(@pxref{@code{@@anchor}}) or float labels (@pxref{@code{@@float}}) to define "
"cross-reference locations.  When @TeX{} generates a DVI file, it records "
"each cross-reference location page number and uses the page numbers in "
"making references.  Thus, even if you are writing a manual that will only be "
"printed, and not used online, you must nonetheless write @code{@@node} lines "
"(or @code{@@anchor} anchors) in order to name the places to which you make "
"cross-references."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3837
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross Reference Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3839
msgid "@nodedescription A summary of the different commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3839
#, no-wrap
msgid "Different Cross-reference Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3840
#, no-wrap
msgid "Different cross-reference commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3843
msgid "There are three different cross-reference commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3845 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4366
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xref"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3849
msgid ""
"Used to start a sentence with an Info cross-reference saying @samp{*Note "
"@var{name}: @var{node}.} or with @w{`See @dots{}'} in other output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3850 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4380
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@ref"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3855
msgid ""
"Used within or, more often, at the end of a sentence; produces an Info cross-"
"reference saying @samp{*note @var{name}: @var{node}.}, and just the "
"reference in other output formats, without the preceding `See'."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3856 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4441
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@pxref"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3861
msgid ""
"Used within parentheses, at the end of a sentence, or otherwise before "
"punctuation, to make a reference.  Its output starts with a lowercase "
"@samp{*note} in Info, and with a lowercase `see' in the other output "
"formats.  (@samp{p} is for `parenthesis'.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3868
msgid ""
"Additionally, there are commands to produce references to documents outside "
"the Texinfo system.  The @code{@@cite} command is used to make references to "
"books and manuals.  @code{@@url} produces a @acronym{URL}, for example a "
"reference to a page on the World Wide Web."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3870
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross Reference Parts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3872
msgid "@nodedescription A cross-reference has several parts."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3872
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parts of a Cross-reference"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3873
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-reference parts"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3874
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parts of a cross-reference"
msgstr ""

#.  merged node
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3876
msgid "Reference Syntax"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3882
msgid ""
"A cross-reference command requires only one argument, which is the name of "
"the node to which it refers.  A cross-reference command may contain up to "
"four additional arguments.  The template for a full five argument cross-"
"reference looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3887
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@xref@{@var{node-name}, @var{online-label}, @var{printed-label},\n"
"@var{manual-name}, @var{printed-manual-title}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3891
msgid "The five possible arguments for a cross-reference are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3901
msgid ""
"The node or anchor name.  This is the location to which the cross-reference "
"takes you.  In a printed document, the location of the node provides the "
"page reference only for references within the same document.  Use "
"@code{@@node} to define the node (@pxref{Writing a Node}), @code{@@anchor} "
"(@pxref{@code{@@anchor}}), or @code{@@float} (@pxref{@code{@@float}}) with a "
"label.  This argument is required (except for reference to whole manuals)."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3905
msgid ""
"Write a node name in a cross-reference in exactly the same way as in the "
"@code{@@node} line, including the same capitalization; otherwise, the "
"processors may not find the reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3910
msgid ""
"A label for online output.  It is usually omitted; then the topic "
"description (third argument) is used if it was specified; if that was "
"omitted as well, the node name is used."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3915
msgid ""
"A label for printed output.  Often, this is the title or topic of the "
"section.  This is used as the name of the reference in the printed manual.  "
"If omitted, the node name is used."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3919
msgid ""
"The name of the manual to which the reference refers, if it is outside the "
"current manual, in a different Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3923
msgid ""
"The title of the printed manual to which the reference refers, from a "
"different Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3928
msgid ""
"Whitespace before and after the commas separating these arguments is "
"ignored.  To include a comma in one of the arguments, use @code{@@comma@{@}} "
"(@pxref{Inserting a Comma})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3931
msgid ""
"Cross-references with one, two, three, four, and five arguments are "
"described separately in following sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3932
#, no-wrap
msgid "Comma after cross-reference"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3938
msgid ""
"When processing with TeX, a comma is automatically inserted after the page "
"number for cross-references to within the same manual, unless the closing "
"brace of the argument is followed by non-whitespace (such as a comma or "
"period).  This gives you the choice of whether to have a comma there in "
"other output formats.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3941
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xref@{Another Section@} for more info\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3947
msgid ""
"with TeX produces @w{`See Another Section, page @var{ppp}, for more info'}, "
"and in the Info output produces @w{@samp{*Note Another Section:: for more "
"info}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3952
msgid ""
"If an unwanted comma is added, follow the argument with a command such as "
"@samp{@@:}.  For example, @w{@samp{@@xref@{Hurricanes@}@@: --- for the "
"details}} produces"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3955
msgid "See Hurricanes, page @var{ppp} --- for the details"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3959
msgid "instead of @w{`See Hurricanes, page @var{ppp}, --- for the details'}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3963
msgid ""
"@command{texi2any} warns and protect names when the text of a cross-"
"reference (and node names and menu items) contains a problematic construct "
"that could interfere with its parsing in Info.  @xref{Info Node Names "
"Constraints}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3966
msgid "@raisesections"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3966
#, no-wrap
msgid "One Argument"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3968
msgid "@nodedescription @code{@@xref} with one argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3968
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@xref} with One Argument"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3969
#, no-wrap
msgid "One-argument form of cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3973
msgid ""
"The simplest form of @code{@@xref} takes one argument, the name of another "
"node in the same Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3977 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4018
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4067 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4175
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4393 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5416
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5837 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6002
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6549 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7346
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8142 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9090
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10859 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11059
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11217 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12582
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13595 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:21493
msgid "For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3980
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xref@{Tropical Storms@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3984 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4077
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4105 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4219
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4248 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4312
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5299 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5360
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5424 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5492
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5519 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5546
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5577 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5603
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5799 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6013
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6177 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6216
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6281 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6472
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6512 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6561
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6586 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6632
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9097 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9316
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9992 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10086
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10128 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10495
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10871 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11071
msgid "produces"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3987
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note Tropical Storms::.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3991 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4032
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4084 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4112
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4228 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4257
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4348 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4474
msgid "in Info and"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3994
msgid "See Section 3.1 [Tropical Storms], page 24."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:3998 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4091
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4119 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4236
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4265 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4355
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4481
msgid "in a printed manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4000
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4002
msgid "@nodedescription @code{@@xref} with two arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4002
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@xref} with Two Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4003
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two-argument form of cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4006
msgid ""
"With two arguments, the second is used as a label for the online output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4010 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4057
msgid "The template is like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4013
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xref@{@var{node-name}, @var{online-label}@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4021
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xref@{Electrical Effects, Lightning@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4025 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4719
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5649 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5697
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6676 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7504
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9279 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9648
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10620 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10936
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11228 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11576
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11599 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11623
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11647 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13246
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13267 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13368
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13606
msgid "produces:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4028
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note Lightning: Electrical Effects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4035
msgid "See Section 5.2 [Electrical Effects], page 57."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4039
msgid "in a printed manual, where the node name is printed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4043
msgid ""
"The second argument to cross-references shares constraints with node names.  "
"The potentially problematic character in this context is the colon.  "
"@xref{Info Node Names Constraints}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4045
#, no-wrap
msgid "Three Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4047
msgid "@nodedescription @code{@@xref} with three arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4047
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@xref} with Three Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4048
#, no-wrap
msgid "Three-argument form of cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4053
msgid ""
"A third argument replaces the node name in the printed output.  The third "
"argument should be the name of the section in the printed output, or else "
"state the topic discussed by that section."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4061
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xref@{@var{node-name}, @var{online-label}, @var{printed-label}@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4072
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@xref@{Electrical Effects, Lightning, Thunder and Lightning@},\n"
"for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4080
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note Lightning: Electrical Effects, for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4087 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4115
msgid "See Section 5.2 [Thunder and Lightning], page 57, for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4095
msgid ""
"If a third argument is given and the second one is empty, then the third "
"argument serves for both.  (Note how two commas, side by side, mark the "
"empty second argument.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4100
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@xref@{Electrical Effects, , Thunder and Lightning@},\n"
"for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4108
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note Thunder and Lightning: Electrical Effects, for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4123
msgid ""
"The third argument to cross-references shares constraints with node names.  "
"The potentially problematic character in this context is the colon.  "
"@xref{Info Node Names Constraints}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4128
msgid ""
"As a practical matter, it is often best to write cross-references with just "
"the first argument if the node name and the section title are the same (or "
"nearly so), and with the first and third arguments only if the node name and "
"title are different."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4129 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4134
#, no-wrap
msgid "xrefautomaticsectiontitle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4134
msgid ""
"Texinfo offers a setting to use the section title instead of node names by "
"default in cross-references (an explicitly specified third argument still "
"takes precedence):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4137
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xrefautomaticsectiontitle on\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4141
msgid ""
"This setting is also used for cross-references to anchors defined with "
"@code{@@namedanchor} (@pxref{@code{@@anchor}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4150
msgid ""
"Typically this line would be given near the beginning of the document and "
"used for the whole manual.  But you can turn it off if you want "
"(@code{@@xrefautomaticsectiontitle off}), for example, if you're including "
"some other sub-document that doesn't have suitable section names.  This "
"setting also applies to node headers in HTML: if "
"@code{@@xrefautomaticsectiontitle} is on, the sections names are used in "
"node headers instead of the node names when possible.  This setting has no "
"effect in Info or plain text output."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4152
#, no-wrap
msgid "Four and Five Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4154
msgid "@nodedescription @code{@@xref} with four and five arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4154
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@xref} with Four and Five Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4155
#, no-wrap
msgid "Four- and five argument forms of cross-references"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4160
msgid ""
"In a cross-reference, a fourth argument specifies the name of another "
"manual, different from the file in which the reference appears, and a fifth "
"argument specifies its title as a printed manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4164
msgid "The full template is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@xref@{@var{node-name}, @var{online-label}, @var{printed-label},\n"
"@var{manual-name}, @var{printed-manual-title}@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4179
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@xref@{Electrical Effects, Lightning, Thunder and Lightning,\n"
"weather, An Introduction to Meteorology@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4183
msgid "produces this output in Info:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4186
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note Lightning: (weather)Electrical Effects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4193
msgid ""
"As you can see, the name of the manual is enclosed in parentheses and "
"precedes the name of the node.  In HTML, the manual name and the node name "
"are used to construct the hyperlink URL (@pxref{HTML Xref}), while the link "
"text is based on the label."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4196
msgid "In a printed manual, the reference looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4200 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4232
msgid ""
"See section ``Thunder and Lightning'' in @cite{An Introduction to "
"Meteorology}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4206
msgid ""
"The title of the printed manual is typeset like @code{@@cite}; and the "
"reference lacks a page number since the page a reference refers when that "
"reference is to another manual cannot be known."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4211
msgid ""
"Next case: often, you will leave out the second argument when you use the "
"long version of @code{@@xref}.  In this case, the third argument, the topic "
"description, will be used as the cross-reference name in online formats.  "
"For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4215
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@xref@{Electrical Effects, , Thunder and Lightning,\n"
"weather, An Introduction to Meteorology@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4223
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note Thunder and Lightning: (weather)Electrical Effects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4240
msgid ""
"Next case: If the node name and the section title are the same in the other "
"manual, you may also leave out the section title.  In this case, the node "
"name is used in both instances.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@xref@{Electrical Effects,,,\n"
"weather, An Introduction to Meteorology@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4252
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note (weather)Electrical Effects::.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4261
msgid ""
"See section ``Electrical Effects'' in @cite{An Introduction to Meteorology}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4274
msgid ""
"In general, there is no reason to have a manual name argument without a "
"printed manual argument, unless no printed manual is generated.  You may "
"also want to refer to another manual file that is within a single printed "
"manual---when multiple Texinfo files are incorporated into the same printed "
"manual but can create separate output files in other output formats.  In "
"this case, you need to specify only the fourth argument, and not the fifth.  "
"If the printed manual title argument is missing, the manual name will be "
"used instead in printed output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4280
msgid ""
"A printed manual title argument without an online manual argument is of "
"little use unless only a printed manual is generated from the Texinfo "
"source.  The result in online formats depends on the format, and can be, for "
"example, an empty manual name or a reference to the printed manual formatted "
"in a similar way to the printed output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4284
msgid ""
"Finally, it's also allowed to leave out all the arguments @emph{except} the "
"fourth and fifth, to refer to another manual as a whole.  See the next "
"section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4286
msgid "@lowersections"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4288 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4290
#, no-wrap
msgid "Referring to a Manual as a Whole"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4290
msgid "@nodedescription Refer to an entire manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4291
#, no-wrap
msgid "Manual, referring to as a whole"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4292
#, no-wrap
msgid "Referring to an entire manual"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4294
msgid "Top Node Naming"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4299
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, you must always name a node in a cross-reference.  However, it's "
"not unusual to want to refer to another manual as a whole, rather than a "
"particular section within it.  In this case, giving any section name is an "
"unnecessary distraction."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4305
msgid ""
"So, with cross-references to other manuals (@pxref{Four and Five "
"Arguments}), if the first argument is either @samp{Top} (capitalized just "
"that way) or omitted entirely, and the third argument is omitted, the "
"printed output includes no node or section name.  (The Info output includes "
"@samp{Top} if it was given.)  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4308
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xref@{Top,,, make, The GNU Make Manual@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4316
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note (make)Top::.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4321 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24506
msgid "and"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4324
msgid "See @cite{The GNU Make Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4329
msgid ""
"Info readers will go to the Top node of the manual whether or not the `Top' "
"node is explicitly specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4334
msgid ""
"It's also possible (and is historical practice) to refer to a whole manual "
"by specifying the `Top' node and an appropriate entry for the third argument "
"to the @code{@@xref} command.  Using this idiom, to make a cross-reference "
"to @cite{The GNU Make Manual}, you would write:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4337
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@xref@{Top,, Overview, make, The GNU Make Manual@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4341 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4465
msgid "which produces"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4344
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Note Overview: (make)Top.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4351
msgid "See section ``Overview'' in @cite{The GNU Make Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4362
msgid ""
"In this example, @samp{Top} is the name of the first node, and "
"@samp{Overview} is the name of the first section of the manual.  There is no "
"widely-used convention for naming the first section in a printed manual, "
"this is just what the Make manual happens to use.  This arbitrariness of the "
"first name is a principal reason why omitting the third argument in whole-"
"manual cross-references is preferable."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4364
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@xref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4366
msgid "@nodedescription Begin a reference with `See' @dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4369
#, no-wrap
msgid "xref"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4370
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-references using @code{@@xref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4371
#, no-wrap
msgid "References using @code{@@xref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4376
msgid ""
"The @code{@@xref} command generates a cross-reference for the beginning of a "
"sentence.  Examples of using @code{@@xref} are in previous sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4378
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4380
msgid "@nodedescription A reference for the last part of a sentence."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4383
#, no-wrap
msgid "ref"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4384
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-references using @code{@@ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4385
#, no-wrap
msgid "References using @code{@@ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4390
msgid ""
"@code{@@ref} is nearly the same as @code{@@xref} except that it does not "
"generate a `See' in the output, just the reference itself.  This makes it "
"useful as the last part of a sentence."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4394
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hurricanes"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4397
#, no-wrap
msgid "For more information, @@pxref@{This@}, and @@ref@{That@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4401
msgid "produces in Info:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4404
#, no-wrap
msgid "For more information, *note This::, and *note That::.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4408
msgid "and in printed output:"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4412
msgid ""
"For more information, see Section 1.1 [This], page 1, and Section 1.2 "
"[That], page 2."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4418
msgid ""
"The @code{@@ref} command can tempt writers to express themselves in a manner "
"that is suitable for a printed manual but looks awkward in the Info format.  "
"Bear in mind that your audience could be using both the printed and other "
"output formats such as Info.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4419
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sea surges"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4422
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sea surges are described in @@ref@{Hurricanes@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4426
msgid "looks ok in the printed output:"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4429
msgid "Sea surges are described in Section 6.7 [Hurricanes], page 72."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4433
msgid "but is awkward to read in Info, ``note'' being a verb:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4436
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sea surges are described in *note Hurricanes::.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4439
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@pxref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4441
msgid "@nodedescription How to write a parenthetical cross-reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4444
#, no-wrap
msgid "pxref"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4445
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-references using @code{@@pxref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4446
#, no-wrap
msgid "References using @code{@@pxref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4453
msgid ""
"The parenthetical reference command, @code{@@pxref}, is nearly the same as "
"@code{@@xref}, but it is best used within parentheses.  The command differs "
"from @code{@@xref} in that the reference is typeset with a lowercase `see' "
"rather than an uppercase `See'.  In Info, @samp{*note} is output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4456
msgid "With one argument, a parenthetical cross-reference looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4457
#, no-wrap
msgid "Flooding"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4460
#, no-wrap
msgid "@dots{} storms cause flooding (@@pxref@{Hurricanes@}) @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4469
#, no-wrap
msgid "@dots{} storms cause flooding (*note Hurricanes::) @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4477
msgid ""
"@dots{} storms cause flooding (see Section 6.7 [Hurricanes], page 72) @dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4492
msgid ""
"The effect of @samp{@@pxref@{@var{node-name}@}} is identical to that of "
"@samp{@@ref@{@var{node-name}@}} in Info output.  Hence, it may be "
"preferable, when not inside parentheses, to write @samp{see @@ref@{@var{node-"
"name}@}} rather than @samp{@@pxref@{@var{node-name}@}} when you want the "
"word ``see'' to occur in the output, as Info readers cannot reliably insert "
"this word based on the @samp{*note} cross-reference marker (for example, "
"using the @code{Info-hide-note-references} variable; see @ref{Help-Inv, Help-"
"Inv, Invisible text in Emacs Info, info, Info: An Introduction})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4496
msgid ""
"In past versions of Texinfo, it was not allowed to write punctuation after a "
"@code{@@pxref}, so it could be used @emph{only} before a right parenthesis.  "
"This is no longer the case."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4498
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@anchor}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4500
msgid "@nodedescription Defining arbitrary cross-reference targets"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4500
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@anchor}: Defining Arbitrary Cross-reference Targets"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4503
#, no-wrap
msgid "anchor"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4504
#, no-wrap
msgid "Anchors"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4505
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-reference targets, arbitrary"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4506
#, no-wrap
msgid "Targets for cross-references, arbitrary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4513
msgid ""
"You may wish to create cross-references to arbitrary positions in a "
"document, not just to the beginning of nodes.  The @code{@@anchor} command "
"marks such a position in your document, called an @dfn{anchor}.  Put an "
"@code{@@anchor} command just before the position you wish to refer to.  For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4518
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This marks the @@anchor@{x-spot@}spot.\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@xref@{x-spot,,the spot@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4527 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4589
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This marks the spot.\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"See [the spot], page 1.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4535
msgid ""
"The brace-delimited argument to @code{@@anchor}, here @samp{x-spot}, is the "
"@dfn{anchor name}, which plays the same role as a node name.  The anchor "
"name is used to create cross-references to the anchor, as with an "
"@code{@@xref} or other cross-reference command, as shown (@pxref{Cross "
"References}).  As you can see, the @code{@@anchor} command itself produces "
"no output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4539
msgid ""
"Anchor names share the same constraints as nodes on the characters that can "
"be included (@pxref{Info Node Names Constraints}).  Anchor names, node names "
"and float labels may not conflict."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4543
msgid ""
"You can put the @code{@@anchor} command on a line by itself if that helps "
"readability of the source.  Whitespace (including newlines) is ignored after "
"@code{@@anchor}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4553
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes, deleting or renaming"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4557
msgid ""
"When you delete or rename a node, it is usually a good idea to define an "
"@code{@@anchor} with the old name.  That way, any links to the old node, "
"whether from other Texinfo manuals or general web pages, keep working."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4562
msgid ""
"By default, links to anchors are unaffected by the "
"@code{xrefautomaticsectiontitle} setting (see @ref{Three Arguments,, "
"@code{xrefautomaticsectiontitle}}).  This means that the anchor name will be "
"used as label text in the cross-reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4563
#, no-wrap
msgid "namedanchor"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4566
msgid ""
"However, you can define a @dfn{named anchor} using @code{@@namedanchor}.  "
"The template is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4569
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@namedanchor@{@var{anchor-name}, @var{xref-name}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4574
msgid ""
"Use this exactly as you would @code{@@anchor}, except the additional "
"@var{xref-name} parameter gives the text to use if "
"@code{xrefautomaticsectiontitle} is on.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4580
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This marks the @@namedanchor@{x-spot, the spot@}spot.\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@set xrefautomaticsectiontitle on\n"
"@@xref@{x-spot@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4591
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@link}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4593
msgid "@nodedescription How to make a plain link."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4593
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@link}: Plain, unadorned hyperlink"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4594
#, no-wrap
msgid "link"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4595
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-references using @code{@@link}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4596
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plain link"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4597
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plain hyperlink"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4602
msgid ""
"@code{@@link} produces a plain hyperlink in output formats that support it, "
"including in HTML, DocBook, @LaTeX{} and online PDF@.  The template is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4605
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@link@{@var{node-name}, @var{label}, @var{manual-name}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4613
msgid ""
"@var{node-name} is the name of the target node or anchor.  Either or both of "
"@var{label} and @var{manual-name} can be omitted.  @var{label}, if given, is "
"the text to use for the link.  @var{manual-name} is the name of the external "
"manual that the target appears within; if not given, the reference is to the "
"current manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4617
msgid ""
"@code{@@link} has similar output to @code{@@ref}, except that it does not "
"produce any extra text around the link label in Info or printed output that "
"would mark it as a cross-reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4625
msgid ""
"Be careful about using @code{@@link} to produce links that are necessary for "
"a user to move around a manual, as these links will do nothing in some "
"output formats.  @code{@@link} is best used to add convenience links that "
"are nonetheless not essential for a reader to understand the text of the "
"manual.  For example, you might use @code{@@link} in a code sample to "
"reference documentation of a symbol in a programming library."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4628
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@inforef}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4630
msgid "@nodedescription How to refer to an Info-only file."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4630
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@inforef}: Cross-references to Info-only Material"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4633
#, no-wrap
msgid "inforef"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4634
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-references using @code{@@inforef}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4635
#, no-wrap
msgid "References using @code{@@inforef}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4644
msgid ""
"@code{@@inforef} is used for making cross-references to Info documents---"
"even from a printed manual.  This was originally used for Info files that "
"were not generated from any Texinfo source.  The command is now obsolete and "
"should not be used.  In addition to having little use, similar output can be "
"obtained with @code{@@xref}, @code{@@ref} or @code{@@pxref} with the Info "
"file name as the fourth argument and no fifth argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4647
msgid ""
"The command takes either two or three arguments, in the following order:"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4651
msgid "The node name."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4654
msgid "The cross-reference name (optional)."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4657
msgid "The Info file name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4661
msgid "The template is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4664
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inforef@{@var{node-name}, @var{cross-reference-name}, @var{info-file-name}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4667
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@url}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4669
msgid "@nodedescription How to refer to a uniform resource locator."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4669
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@url}, @code{@@uref@{@var{url}[, @var{text}][, @var{replacement}]@}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4673 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4684
#, no-wrap
msgid "uref"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4673 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4675
#, no-wrap
msgid "url"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4673
#, no-wrap
msgid "URL @subentry referring to"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4676
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{href}, producing HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4683
msgid ""
"@code{@@url} produces a reference to a uniform resource locator (URL@:).  It "
"takes one mandatory argument, the URL@:, and two optional arguments which "
"control the text that is displayed.  In HTML and PDF output, @code{@@url} "
"produces a link you can follow.  (To merely indicate a URL without creating "
"a link people can follow, use @code{@@indicateurl}, "
"@pxref{@code{@@indicateurl}}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4690
msgid ""
"@code{@@uref} is a synonym for @code{@@url}.  (Originally, @code{@@url} had "
"the meaning of @code{@@indicateurl} and @code{@@uref} was required to "
"produce a working link, but in practice @code{@@url} was almost always "
"misused.  So we've changed the meaning.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4694
msgid ""
"The second argument, if specified, is the text to display (the default is "
"the URL itself); in output formats other than HTML, the URL is output in "
"addition to this text."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4695
#, no-wrap
msgid "Man page, reference to"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4700
msgid ""
"The third argument, if specified, is the text to display, but in this case "
"the URL is not output in any format.  This is useful when the text is "
"already sufficiently referential, as in a man page.  Also, if the third "
"argument is given, the second argument is ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4702 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4704
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@url} Examples"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4704
msgid "@nodedescription Examples of using all the forms of @code{@@url}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4706
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@url}, examples of using"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4707
#, no-wrap
msgid "URL @subentry examples of displaying"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4712
msgid ""
"First, here is an example of the simplest form of @code{@@url}, with just "
"one argument.  The given URL is both the target and the visible text of the "
"link:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4715
#, no-wrap
msgid "The official GNU ftp site is @@url@{https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4721
#, no-wrap
msgid "The official GNU ftp site is @url{https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4723
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two-argument form of @code{@@url}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4726
msgid "Here is an example of the two-argument form:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4729
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The official @@url@{https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu, GNU ftp site@}\n"
"holds programs and texts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4733 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5765
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10220 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10357
msgid "which produces:"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4736
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The official @url{https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu, GNU ftp site}\n"
"holds programs and texts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4740
msgid "The HTML output is this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4743
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The official <a href=\"https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu\">GNU ftp site</a>\n"
"holds programs and texts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4747
msgid "In other formats, the output is like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4750
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The official GNU ftp site (https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu)\n"
"holds programs and texts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4752
#, no-wrap
msgid "Three-argument form of @code{@@url}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4755
msgid "Finally, an example of the three-argument form:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4757
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @@url@{/man.cgi/1/ls,,ls@} program @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4761
msgid "which, except for HTML, produces:"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4763
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @url{/man.cgi/1/ls,,ls} program @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4767
msgid "but with HTML:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4769
#, no-wrap
msgid "The <a href=\"/man.cgi/1/ls\">ls</a> program @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4774
msgid ""
"By the way, some people prefer to display URLs in the unambiguous format:"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4777
#, no-wrap
msgid "<URL:http://@var{host}/@var{path}>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4780
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<URL...>} convention, not used"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4785
msgid ""
"You can use this form in the input file if you wish.  We feel it's not "
"necessary to include the @samp{<URL:} and @samp{>} in the output, since to "
"be useful any software that tries to detect URLs in text already has to "
"detect them without the @samp{<URL:}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4787 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4789
#, no-wrap
msgid "URL Line Breaking"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4789
msgid "@nodedescription How lines are broken within @code{@@url} text."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4791
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line breaks @subentry URLs"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4792
#, no-wrap
msgid "Breakpoints within URLs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4798
msgid ""
"@TeX{} allows line breaking within URLs at only a few characters (which are "
"special in URLs): @samp{&}, @samp{.}, @samp{#}, @samp{?}, and @samp{/} (but "
"not between two @samp{/} characters).  A tiny amount of stretchable space is "
"also inserted around these characters to help with line breaking."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4802
msgid ""
"For HTML output, modern browsers will also do line breaking within displayed "
"URLs.  If you need to allow breaks at other characters you can insert "
"@code{@@/} as needed (@pxref{Line Breaks})."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4803
#, no-wrap
msgid "urefbreakstyle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4809
msgid ""
"By default, in @TeX{} any such breaks at special characters will occur after "
"the character.  Some people prefer such breaks to happen before the special "
"character.  This can be controlled with the @code{@@urefbreakstyle} command "
"(this command has effect only in @TeX{}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4812
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@urefbreakstyle @var{how}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4816
msgid "where the argument @var{how} is one of these words:"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4817
#, no-wrap
msgid "after@r{, value for @code{@@urefbreakstyle}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4818
#, no-wrap
msgid "before@r{, value for @code{@@urefbreakstyle}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4819
#, no-wrap
msgid "none@r{, value for @code{@@urefbreakstyle}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4821
#, no-wrap
msgid "after"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4823
msgid "(the default) Potentially break after the special characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4823
#, no-wrap
msgid "before"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4825
msgid "Potentially break before the special characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4825 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24314
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24359
#, no-wrap
msgid "none"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4828
msgid ""
"Do not consider breaking at the special characters at all; any potential "
"breaks must be manually inserted."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4831 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4833
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@url} PDF Output Format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4833
msgid "@nodedescription A special option to hide links in PDF output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4835
#, no-wrap
msgid "PDF output of URLs"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4836
#, no-wrap
msgid "URLs, PDF output of"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4843
msgid ""
"If the ultimate purpose of a PDF is only to be viewed online, perhaps "
"similar to HTML in some inchoate way, you may not want the URLs to be "
"included in the visible text (just as URLs are not visible to readers of web "
"pages).  Texinfo provides a PDF-specific option for this, which must be used "
"inside @code{@@tex}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4844
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\urefurlonlylinktrue"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4849
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@tex\n"
"\\global\\urefurlonlylinktrue\n"
"@@end tex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4855
msgid ""
"The result is that @code{@@url@{https://www.gnu.org, GNU@}} has the visible "
"output of just `GNU', with a link target of @url{https://www.gnu.org}.  "
"Ordinarily, the visible output would include both the label and the url: "
"`GNU (@url{https://www.gnu.org})'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4861
msgid ""
"This option only has effect when the PDF output is produced with the "
"pdf@TeX{} program, not with other ways of getting from Texinfo to PDF (e.g., "
"@TeX{} to DVI to PDF)@.  Consequently, it is ok to specify this option "
"unconditionally within @code{@@tex}, as shown above.  It is ignored when DVI "
"is being produced."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4863
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@cite}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4865
msgid "@nodedescription How to refer to books not in the Info system."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4865
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@cite}@{@var{reference}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4868
#, no-wrap
msgid "cite"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4874
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@cite} command for the name of a book that lacks a companion "
"Info file.  For example, we could refer to @cite{A Book}.  The command "
"selects a slanted font in the printed manual, and generates quotation marks "
"in the Info file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4878
msgid ""
"If a book is written in Texinfo, it is better to use a cross-reference "
"command since a reader can easily follow such a reference in Info.  "
"@xref{@code{@@xref}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4879 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4881
#, no-wrap
msgid "PDF Colors"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4881
msgid "@nodedescription Colorizing URLs and other links in PDF output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4883
#, no-wrap
msgid "Colored links, in PDF output"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4884
#, no-wrap
msgid "Links, coloring in PDF output"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4885
#, no-wrap
msgid "URLs, coloring in PDF output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4892
msgid ""
"By default, URLs and cross-reference links are printed in black in PDF "
"output.  Very occasionally, however, you may want to highlight such ``live'' "
"links with a different color, as is commonly done on web pages.  Texinfo "
"provides a PDF-specific option for specifying these colors, which must be "
"used inside @code{@@tex}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4893
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\linkcolor"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4894
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\urlcolor"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4900
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@tex\n"
"\\global\\def\\linkcolor@{1 0 0@}  % red\n"
"\\global\\def\\urlcolor@{0 1 0@}   % green\n"
"@@end tex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4905
msgid ""
"@code{\\urlcolor} changes the color of @code{@@url} output (both the actual "
"URL and any textual label), while @code{\\linkcolor} changes the color for "
"cross-references to nodes, etc.  They are independent."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4906
#, no-wrap
msgid "RGB color specification."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4909
msgid ""
"The three given values must be numbers between 0 and 1, specifying the "
"amount of red, green, and blue respectively."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4915
msgid ""
"These definitions only have an effect when the PDF output is produced with "
"the pdf@TeX{} program, not with other ways of getting from Texinfo to PDF "
"(e.g., @TeX{} to DVI to PDF)@.  Consequently, it is ok to specify this "
"option unconditionally within @code{@@tex}, as shown above.  It is ignored "
"when DVI is being produced."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4918
msgid ""
"We do not recommend colorizing just for fun; unless you have a specific "
"reason to use colors, best to skip it."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4922
#, no-wrap
msgid "Marking Text"
msgstr ""

#. type: nodedescriptionblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4926
msgid ""
"Marking words and phrases as code, keyboard input, meta-syntactic variables, "
"and the like."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4927
#, no-wrap
msgid "Marking Text, Words and Phrases"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4928
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paragraph, marking text within"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4929
#, no-wrap
msgid "Marking words and phrases"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4930
#, no-wrap
msgid "Words and phrases, marking them"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4931
#, no-wrap
msgid "Marking text within a paragraph"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4932
#, no-wrap
msgid "Text, marking up"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4940
msgid ""
"In Texinfo, you can mark words and phrases in a variety of ways.  The "
"Texinfo processors use this information to determine how to highlight the "
"text.  You can specify, for example, whether a word or phrase is a defining "
"occurrence, a metasyntactic variable, or a symbol used in a program.  Also, "
"you can emphasize text, in several different ways."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4942
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4944
msgid "@nodedescription How to indicate definitions, files, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4944
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indicating Definitions, Commands, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4946
#, no-wrap
msgid "Highlighting text"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4947
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indicating commands, definitions, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4954
msgid ""
"Texinfo has commands for indicating just what kind of object a piece of text "
"refers to.  For example, email addresses are marked by @code{@@email}; that "
"way, the result can be a live link to send email when the output format "
"supports it.  If the email address was simply marked as ``print in a "
"typewriter font'', that would not be possible."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4956
#, no-wrap
msgid "Useful Highlighting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4958
msgid "@nodedescription Highlighting provides useful information."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4958
#, no-wrap
msgid "Highlighting Commands are Useful"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4961
msgid "The commands serve a variety of purposes:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4963 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20099
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@code@{@var{sample-code}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4966
msgid ""
"Indicate text that is a literal example of a piece of a program.  "
"@xref{@code{@@code}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4967 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20646
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@kbd@{@var{keyboard-characters}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4969
msgid "Indicate keyboard input.  @xref{@code{@@kbd}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4970 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20654
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@key@{@var{key-name}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4973
msgid ""
"Indicate the conventional name for a key on a keyboard.  @xref{@code{@@key}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4974 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20883
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@samp@{@var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4977
msgid ""
"Indicate text that is a literal example of a sequence of characters.  "
"@xref{@code{@@samp}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4978
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@verb@{@var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4981
msgid "Write a verbatim sequence of characters.  @xref{@code{@@verb}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4982 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:21168
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@var@{@var{metasyntactic-variable}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4984
msgid "Indicate a metasyntactic variable.  @xref{@code{@@var}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4985 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20365
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@env@{@var{environment-variable}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4987
msgid "Indicate an environment variable.  @xref{@code{@@env}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4988
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@file@{@var{file-name}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4990
msgid "Indicate the name of a file.  @xref{@code{@@file}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4991 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20112
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@command@{@var{command-name}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4994
msgid "Indicate the name of a command.  @xref{@code{@@command}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4995
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@option@{@var{option}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4998
msgid "Indicate a command-line option.  @xref{@code{@@option}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:4999 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20288
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@dfn@{@var{term}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5002 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20290
msgid ""
"Indicate the introductory or defining use of a term.  @xref{@code{@@dfn}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5003 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20075
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@cite@{@var{reference}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5005
msgid "Indicate the name of a book.  @xref{@code{@@cite}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5006 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:19962
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@abbr@{@var{abbreviation}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5008
msgid "Indicate an abbreviation, such as `Comput.'."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5009 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:19966
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@acronym@{@var{acronym}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5011
msgid "Indicate an acronym.  @xref{@code{@@acronym}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5012
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@indicateurl@{@var{uniform-resource-locator}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5016
msgid ""
"Indicate an example (that is, nonfunctional) uniform resource locator.  "
"@xref{@code{@@indicateurl}}.  (Use @code{@@url} (@pxref{@code{@@url}}) for "
"live URLs.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5017
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@email@{@var{email-address}[, @var{displayed-text}]@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5019 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20348
msgid "Indicate an electronic mail address.  @xref{@code{@@email}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5023
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5025
msgid "@nodedescription Indicating program code."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5025
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@code}@{@var{sample-code}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5028 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5167
#, no-wrap
msgid "code"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5030
#, no-wrap
msgid "Syntactic tokens, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5034
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@code} command to indicate text that is a piece of a program "
"and which consists of entire syntactic tokens.  Enclose the text in braces."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5035
#, no-wrap
msgid "Expressions in a program, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5036
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keywords, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5037
#, no-wrap
msgid "Reserved words, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5041
msgid ""
"Thus, you should use @code{@@code} for an expression in a program, for the "
"name of a variable or function used in a program, or for a keyword in a "
"programming language."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5046
msgid ""
"Use @code{@@code} for command names in languages that resemble programming "
"languages, such as Texinfo.  For example, @code{@@code} and @code{@@samp} "
"are produced by writing @samp{@@code@{@@@@code@}} and "
"@samp{@@code@{@@@@samp@}} in the Texinfo source, respectively."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5047
#, no-wrap
msgid "Case, not altering in @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5057
msgid ""
"It is incorrect to alter the case of a word inside a @code{@@code} command "
"when it appears at the beginning of a sentence.  Most computer languages are "
"case sensitive.  In C, for example, @code{Printf} is different from the "
"identifier @code{printf}, and most likely is a misspelling of it.  Even in "
"languages which are not case sensitive, it is confusing to a human reader to "
"see identifiers spelled in different ways.  Pick one spelling and always use "
"that.  If you do not want to start a sentence with a command name written "
"all in lowercase, you should rearrange the sentence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5060
msgid ""
"The @code{@@code} argument is typeset in a typewriter (monospace) font.  "
"where the output format allows this.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5063
#, no-wrap
msgid "The function returns @@code@{nil@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5067
msgid "produces this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5070
msgid "The function returns @code{nil}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5073
msgid ""
"Here are some cases for which it is preferable @emph{not} to use "
"@code{@@code}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5077
msgid "For shell command names, such as @command{ls} (use @code{@@command})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5080
msgid "For environment variables, such as @env{TEXINPUTS} (use @code{@@env})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5084
msgid ""
"For shell options, such as @samp{-c}, when such options stand alone (use "
"@code{@@option})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5089
msgid ""
"An entire shell command often looks better if written using @code{@@samp} "
"rather than @code{@@code}.  In this case, the rule is to choose the more "
"pleasing format."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5095
msgid ""
"For a string of characters shorter than a syntactic token.  For example, if "
"you are writing about @samp{goto-ch}, which is just a part of the name for "
"the @code{goto-char} Emacs Lisp function, you should use @code{@@samp}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5106
msgid ""
"In general, when writing about the characters used in a token; for example, "
"do not use @code{@@code} when you are explaining what letters or printable "
"symbols can be used in the names of functions.  (Use @code{@@samp}.)  Also, "
"you should not use @code{@@code} to mark text that is considered input to "
"programs unless the input is written in a language that is like a "
"programming language.  For example, you should not use @code{@@code} for the "
"keystroke commands of GNU Emacs (use @code{@@kbd} instead) although you may "
"use @code{@@code} for the names of the Emacs Lisp functions that the "
"keystroke commands invoke."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5115
msgid ""
"By default, @TeX{} will consider breaking lines at @samp{-} and @samp{_} "
"characters within @code{@@code} and related commands.  This can be "
"controlled with @code{@@allowcodebreaks} "
"(@pxref{@code{@@allowcodebreaks}}).  In the HTML output breaking lines is up "
"to the browser's behavior.  For Info, it seems better never to make such "
"breaks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5125
msgid ""
"For Info and plaintext, quotation characters are usually output around the "
"output of the @code{@@code} command and related commands (e.g., "
"@code{@@kbd}, @code{@@command}) except in typewriter-like contexts such as "
"the @code{@@example} environment (@pxref{@code{@@example}}) and "
"@code{@@code} itself, etc.  To control which quoting characters are inserted "
"by @command{texi2any} in the output of @samp{@@code}, etc., see the "
"@code{OPEN_QUOTE_SYMBOL} and @code{CLOSE_QUOTE_SYMBOL} customization "
"variables (@pxref{Customization Variables for Multiple Formats})."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5127
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@kbd}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5129
msgid "@nodedescription Showing keyboard input."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5129
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@kbd}@{@var{keyboard-characters}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5132
#, no-wrap
msgid "kbd"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5133
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keyboard input"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5137
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@kbd} command for characters of input to be typed by users.  "
"For example, to refer to the characters @kbd{M-a}, write:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5140
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@kbd@{M-a@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5144
msgid "and to refer to the characters @kbd{M-x shell}, write:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5147
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@kbd@{M-x shell@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5149
#, no-wrap
msgid "User input"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5150
#, no-wrap
msgid "Slanted typewriter font, for @code{@@kbd}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5155
msgid ""
"By default, the @code{@@kbd} command produces a different font (slanted "
"typewriter instead of normal typewriter, where the output format allows), so "
"users can distinguish the characters that they are supposed to type from "
"those that the computer outputs."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5156
#, no-wrap
msgid "kbdinputstyle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5162
msgid ""
"Since the usage of @code{@@kbd} varies from manual to manual, you can "
"control the font switching with the @code{@@kbdinputstyle} command.  This "
"command has no effect on Info output.  Write this command at the beginning "
"of a line with a single word as an argument, one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5163
#, no-wrap
msgid "distinct@r{, value for @code{@@kbdinputstyle}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5164
#, no-wrap
msgid "example@r{, value for @code{@@kbdinputstyle}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5165
#, no-wrap
msgid "code@r{, value for @code{@@kbdinputstyle}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5169
msgid "Always use the same font for @code{@@kbd} as @code{@@code}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5169 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:6259
#, no-wrap
msgid "example"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5172
msgid ""
"Use the distinguishing font for @code{@@kbd} only in @code{@@example} and "
"similar environments."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5172
#, no-wrap
msgid "distinct"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5174
msgid "(the default) Always use the distinguishing font for @code{@@kbd}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5180
msgid ""
"You can embed another @@-command inside the braces of a @code{@@kbd} "
"command.  Here, for example, is the way to describe a command that would be "
"described more verbosely as ``press the @samp{r} key and then press the "
"@key{RETURN} key'':"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5183
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@kbd@{r @@key@{RET@}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5188
msgid ""
"This produces: @kbd{r @key{RET}}.  (The present manual uses the default for "
"@code{@@kbdinputstyle}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5191
msgid ""
"You also use the @code{@@kbd} command if you are spelling out the letters "
"you type; for example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5195
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To give the @@code@{logout@} command,\n"
"type the characters @@kbd@{l o g o u t @@key@{RET@}@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5203
msgid ""
"To give the @code{logout} command, type the characters @kbd{l o g o u t "
"@key{RET}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5208
msgid ""
"(Also, this example shows that you can add spaces for clarity.  If you "
"explicitly want to mention a space character as one of the characters of "
"input, write @kbd{@@key@{SPC@}} for it.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5210
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@key}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5212
msgid "@nodedescription Specifying keys."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5212
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@key}@{@var{key-name}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5215
#, no-wrap
msgid "key"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5219
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@key} command for the conventional name for a key on a "
"keyboard, as in:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5222
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@key@{RET@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5227
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{@@key} command within the argument of an @code{@@kbd} "
"command when the sequence of characters to be typed includes one or more "
"keys that are described by name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5230
msgid ""
"For example, to produce @kbd{C-x @key{ESC}} and @kbd{M-@key{TAB}} you would "
"type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5234
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@kbd@{C-x @@key@{ESC@}@}\n"
"@@kbd@{M-@@key@{TAB@}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:5237
