msgid "ridt.eps"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7964
msgid ""
"For example, the following will scale a file @file{ridt.eps} to one inch "
"vertically, with the width scaled proportionately:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7967
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@image@{ridt,,1in@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7969
#, no-wrap
msgid "epsf.tex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7976
msgid ""
"For @code{@@image} to work with @TeX{}, the file @file{epsf.tex} must be "
"installed somewhere that @TeX{} can find it.  (The standard location is "
"@file{@var{texmf}/tex/generic/dvips/epsf.tex}, where @var{texmf} is a root "
"of your @TeX{} directory tree.)  This file is included in the Texinfo "
"distribution and is also available from @uref{ftp://tug.org/tex/epsf.tex}, "
"among other places."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7981
msgid ""
"@code{@@image} can be used within a line as well as for displayed figures.  "
"Therefore, if you intend it to be displayed, be sure to leave a blank line "
"before the command, or the output will run into the preceding text."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7984
msgid ""
"Image scaling is presently implemented only in printed output, not in any "
"other sort of output."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7986 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7988
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7989
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnotes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7988
msgid "@nodedescription Writing footnotes."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7990
#, no-wrap
msgid "footnote"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:7998
msgid ""
"A @dfn{footnote} is for a reference that documents or elucidates the primary "
"text.@footnote{A footnote should complement or expand upon the primary text, "
"but a reader should not need to read a footnote to understand the primary "
"text.  For a thorough discussion of footnotes, see @cite{The Chicago Manual "
"of Style}, which is published by the University of Chicago Press.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8003
msgid ""
"Footnotes are distracting; use them sparingly at most, and it is best to "
"avoid them completely.  Standard bibliographical references are usually "
"better placed in a bibliography at the end of a document instead of in "
"footnotes throughout."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8005 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8007
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnote Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8007
msgid "@nodedescription How to write a footnote in Texinfo."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8014
msgid ""
"In Texinfo, footnotes are created with the @code{@@footnote} command.  This "
"command is followed immediately by a left brace, then by the text of the "
"footnote, and then by a terminating right brace.  Footnotes may be of any "
"length (they will be broken across pages if necessary), but are usually "
"short.  The template is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8017
#, no-wrap
msgid "ordinary text@@footnote@{@var{text of footnote}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8022
msgid ""
"As shown here, the @code{@@footnote} command should come right after the "
"text being footnoted, with no intervening space; otherwise, the footnote "
"marker might end up starting a line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8026
msgid ""
"For example, this clause is followed by a sample footnote@footnote{Here is "
"the sample footnote.}; in the Texinfo source, it looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8030
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@dots{}a sample footnote@@footnote@{Here is the sample\n"
"footnote.@}; in the Texinfo source@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8038
msgid ""
"As you can see, this source includes two punctuation marks next to each "
"other; in this case, @samp{.@};} is the sequence.  This is normal (the first "
"ends the footnote and the second belongs to the sentence being footnoted), "
"so don't worry that it looks odd.  (Another style, perfectly acceptable, is "
"to put the footnote after punctuation belonging to the sentence, as in "
"@samp{;@@footnote@{...}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8042
msgid ""
"In printed output formats, the reference mark for a footnote is a small, "
"superscripted number; the text of the footnote appears at the bottom of the "
"page, below a horizontal line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8047
msgid ""
"In Info, the reference mark for a footnote is a pair of parentheses with the "
"footnote number between them, like this: @samp{(1)}.  The reference mark is "
"followed by a cross-reference link to the footnote text if footnotes are put "
"in separate nodes (@pxref{Footnote Styles})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8051
msgid ""
"In the HTML output, footnote references are generally marked with a small, "
"superscripted number which is rendered as a hypertext link to the footnote "
"text."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8052
#, no-wrap
msgid "Critical editions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8053
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nested footnotes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8056
msgid ""
"Footnotes cannot be nested, and cannot appear in section headings of any "
"kind or other ``unusual'' places."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8060
msgid ""
"A final tip: footnotes in the argument of an @code{@@item} command for an "
"@code{@@table} must be entirely on the same line as the @code{@@item} (as "
"usual).  @xref{Two-column Tables}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8062 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8064
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnote Styles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8064
msgid "@nodedescription Controlling how footnotes appear."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8069
msgid ""
"Online formats have two footnote styles, which determine where the text of "
"the footnote is located, the @samp{end} and @samp{separate} footnote style."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8071
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{end} node footnote style"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8072
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnote style @subentry @samp{end}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8073 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:19816
#, no-wrap
msgid "end"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8079
msgid ""
"For Info, in the @samp{end} node style, all the footnotes for a single node "
"are placed at the end of that node.  The footnotes are separated from the "
"rest of the node by a line of dashes with the word @samp{Footnotes} within "
"it.  Each footnote begins with an @samp{(@var{n})} reference mark."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8084
msgid ""
"Here is an example of the Info output for a single footnote in the end-of-"
"node style:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8088
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"--------- Footnotes ---------\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8090
#, no-wrap
msgid "(1)  Here is a sample footnote.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8095
msgid ""
"In HTML, when the footnote style is @samp{end}, or if the output is not "
"split, footnotes are put at the end of each output file."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8096
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{separate} footnote style"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8097
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footnote style @subentry @samp{separate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8098
#, no-wrap
msgid "separate"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8105
msgid ""
"For Info, in the @samp{separate} node style, all the footnotes for a single "
"node are placed in an automatically constructed node of their own.  In this "
"style, a ``footnote reference'' follows each @samp{(@var{n})} reference mark "
"in the body of the node.  The footnote reference is actually a cross-"
"reference which you use to reach the footnote node."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8112
msgid ""
"The name of the node with the footnotes is constructed by appending "
"@w{@samp{-Footnotes}} to the name of the node that contains the footnotes. "
"(Consequently, the footnotes' node for the @file{Footnotes} node is "
"@w{@file{Footnotes-Footnotes}}!)  The footnotes' node has an `Up' node "
"pointer that leads back to its parent node."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8116
msgid ""
"Here is how the first footnote in this manual looks after being formatted "
"for Info in the separate node style:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8120
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"File: texinfo.info  Node: Overview-Footnotes, Up: Overview\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8123
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(1) The first syllable of \"Texinfo\" is pronounced like\n"
"\"speck\", not \"hex\". @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8128
msgid ""
"In HTML, when the footnote style is @samp{separate}, and the output is "
"split, footnotes are placed in a separate file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8133
msgid ""
"Unless your document has long and important footnotes (as in, say, Gibbon's "
"@cite{Decline and Fall @dots{}}), we recommend the @samp{end} style, as it "
"is simpler for readers to follow."
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8134 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:15064
#, no-wrap
msgid "footnotestyle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8139
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@footnotestyle} command to specify the footnote style.  Write "
"this command at the beginning of a line followed by an argument, either "
"@samp{end} for the end node style or @samp{separate} for the separate node "
"style."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8145
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@footnotestyle end\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: exdent
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8148 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22400
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22466
#, no-wrap
msgid "or"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8150
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@footnotestyle separate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8153
msgid "Write a @code{@@footnotestyle} command in the Texinfo file preamble."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8155 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8157
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8158
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8157
msgid "@nodedescription Creating indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8166
msgid ""
"Using Texinfo, you can generate indices without having to sort and collate "
"entries manually.  In an index, the entries are listed in alphabetical "
"order, together with information on how to find the discussion of each "
"entry.  In a printed manual, this information consists of page numbers.  In "
"other formats, links to the index entries location or to the associated "
"nodes are generated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8171
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides several predefined kinds of indices: an index for "
"functions, an index for variables, an index for concepts, and so on.  You "
"can combine indices or use them for other than their canonical purpose.  "
"Lastly, you can define your own new indices."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8173 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8175
#, no-wrap
msgid "Predefined Indices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8175
msgid "@nodedescription Use different indices for different kinds of entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8179
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides six predefined indices.  Here are their nominal meanings, "
"abbreviations, and the corresponding index entry commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8181 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8604
#, no-wrap
msgid "cp"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8182
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{cp} (concept) index"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8183
#, no-wrap
msgid "cindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8184
#, no-wrap
msgid "cpindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8186
msgid "(@code{@@cindex}) Concept index, for general concepts."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8186 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:8606
#, no-wrap
msgid "fn"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
