msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9831
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@textdegree@{@}} command to generate `@textdegree{}'.  Where "
"possible, this is the normal symbol for degrees."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9836
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@minus}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9838
msgid "@nodedescription How to insert a minus sign."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9838
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@minus} (@minus{}): Inserting a Minus Sign"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9841
#, no-wrap
msgid "minus"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9842
#, no-wrap
msgid "Minus sign"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9844
#, no-wrap
msgid "Em dash, compared to minus sign"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9845
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hyphen, compared to minus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9850
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@minus@{@}} command to generate a minus sign.  Where the "
"character encoding and font used in the output allow it, the symbol is the "
"customary length for a minus sign---a little longer than a hyphen, shorter "
"than an em-dash:"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9853
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@samp{@minus{}} is a minus sign generated with @samp{@@minus@{@}},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9855
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-' is a hyphen generated with the character @samp{-},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9857
#, no-wrap
msgid "`---' is an em-dash for text.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9862
msgid ""
"If you actually want to typeset some math that does a subtraction, it is "
"better to use @code{@@math}, as in @code{@@math@{a-b@}} (@pxref{Inserting "
"Math})."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9864
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@geq @@leq}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9866
msgid "@nodedescription How to insert greater/less-than-or-equal signs."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9866
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@geq} (@geq{}) and @code{@@leq} (@leq{}): Inserting Relations"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9869
msgid "geq leq"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9869
#, no-wrap
msgid "geq"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9870
#, no-wrap
msgid "leq"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9876
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@geq@{@}} and @code{@@leq@{@}} commands to generate greater-"
"than-or-equal and less-than-equal-signs, `@geq{}' and `@leq{}'.  When those "
"symbols are not available, the ASCII sequences @samp{>=} and @samp{<=} are "
"output."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9878 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9880
#, no-wrap
msgid "Glyphs for Programming"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9880
msgid ""
"@nodedescription Indicating results of evaluation, expansion of macros, "
"errors, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9882
#, no-wrap
msgid "Glyphs for programming"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9883
#, no-wrap
msgid "Examples, glyphs for"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9884
#, no-wrap
msgid "Programming, glyphs for"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9893
msgid ""
"In Texinfo, code is often illustrated in examples that are delimited by "
"@code{@@example} and @code{@@end example}.  In such examples, you can "
"indicate the results of evaluation or an expansion using @samp{@result{}} or "
"@samp{@expansion{}}.  Likewise, there are commands to insert glyphs to "
"indicate printed output, error messages, equivalence of expressions, the "
"location of point in an editor, and GUI operation sequences."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9897
msgid ""
"The glyph-insertion commands do not need to be used within an example, but "
"most often they are.  All glyph-insertion commands are followed by empty "
"braces."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9899 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9901
#, no-wrap
msgid "Glyphs Summary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9901
msgid "@nodedescription List of the glyph commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9904
msgid "Here is a summary of the glyph commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9906
#, no-wrap
msgid "@result{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9908
msgid "@code{@@result@{@}} indicates the result of an expression."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9909
#, no-wrap
msgid "@expansion{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9911
msgid "@code{@@expansion@{@}} indicates the results of a macro expansion."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9912
#, no-wrap
msgid "@print{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9914
msgid "@code{@@print@{@}} indicates printed output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9915
#, no-wrap
msgid "@error{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9917
msgid "@code{@@error@{@}} indicates the following text is an error message."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9918
#, no-wrap
msgid "@equiv{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9920
msgid "@code{@@equiv@{@}} indicates the exact equivalence of two forms."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9921
#, no-wrap
msgid "@point{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9923
msgid "@code{@@point@{@}} shows the location of point."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9924
#, no-wrap
msgid "@clicksequence{A @click{} B}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9928
msgid ""
"@code{@@clicksequence@{A @@click@{@} B@}} indicates a GUI operation "
"sequence: first A, then clicking B, or choosing B from a menu, or otherwise "
"selecting it."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9931
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@result}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9933
msgid "@nodedescription How to show the result of expression."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9933
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@result@{@}} (@result{}): Result of an Expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9936
#, no-wrap
msgid "result"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9937
#, no-wrap
msgid "Result of an expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9938
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indicating evaluation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9939
#, no-wrap
msgid "Evaluation glyph"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9940
#, no-wrap
msgid "Value of an expression, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9944
msgid ""
"Use the @code{@@result@{@}} command to indicate the result of evaluating an "
"expression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9948
msgid ""
"The @code{@@result@{@}} command is displayed as @samp{@result{}}, either a "
"double stemmed arrow or (when that is not available) the ASCII sequence "
"@samp{=>}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9950
msgid "Thus, the following,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9954
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(cdr '(1 2 3))\n"
"    @result{} (2 3)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9958
msgid "may be read as ``@code{(cdr '(1 2 3))} evaluates to @code{(2 3)}''."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9960
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@expansion}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9962
msgid "@nodedescription How to indicate an expansion."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9962
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@expansion@{@}} (@expansion{}): Indicating an Expansion"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9965 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9967
#, no-wrap
msgid "expansion"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9965
#, no-wrap
msgid "Expansion, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9966
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro expansion, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9972
msgid ""
"When an expression is a macro call, it expands into a new expression.  You "
"can indicate the result of the expansion with the @code{@@expansion@{@}} "
"command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9976
msgid ""
"The @code{@@expansion@{@}} command is displayed as @samp{@expansion{}}, "
"either a long arrow with a flat base or (when that is not available) the "
"ASCII sequence @samp{==>}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9979
msgid "For example, the following"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9987
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@example lisp\n"
"(third '(a b c))\n"
"    @@expansion@{@} (car (cdr (cdr '(a b c))))\n"
"    @@result@{@} c\n"
"@@end example\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:9998
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(third '(a b c))\n"
"    @expansion{} (car (cdr (cdr '(a b c))))\n"
"    @result{} c\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10003
msgid "which may be read as:"
msgstr ""

#. type: quotation
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10007
msgid ""
"@code{(third '(a b c))} expands to @code{(car (cdr (cdr '(a b c))))}; the "
"result of evaluating the expression is @code{c}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10012
msgid ""
"Often, as in this case, an example looks better if the "
"@code{@@expansion@{@}} and @code{@@result@{@}} commands are indented."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10014
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@print}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10016
msgid "@nodedescription How to indicate generated output."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10016
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@print@{@}} (@print{}): Indicating Generated Output"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10019
msgid "Print Glyph"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10019
#, no-wrap
msgid "print"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10020
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printed output, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10025
msgid ""
"Sometimes an expression will generate output during its execution.  You can "
"indicate such displayed output with the @code{@@print@{@}} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10029
msgid ""
"The @code{@@print@{@}} command is displayed as @samp{@print{}}, either a "
"horizontal dash butting against a vertical bar or (when that is not "
"available) the ASCII sequence @samp{-|}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10033
msgid ""
"In the following example, the printed text is indicated with "
"@samp{@print{}}, and the value of the expression follows on the last line."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10040
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(progn (print 'foo) (print 'bar))\n"
"    @print{} foo\n"
"    @print{} bar\n"
"    @result{} bar\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10045
msgid "In a Texinfo source file, this example is written as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10054
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@example lisp\n"
"(progn (print 'foo) (print 'bar))\n"
"    @@print@{@} foo\n"
"    @@print@{@} bar\n"
"    @@result@{@} bar\n"
"@@end example\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10058
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@error}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10060
msgid "@nodedescription How to indicate an error message."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10060
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@error@{@}} (@error{}): Indicating an Error Message"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10063
msgid "Error Glyph"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10063
#, no-wrap
msgid "Error message, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10064
#, no-wrap
msgid "error"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10068
msgid ""
"A piece of code may cause an error when you evaluate it.  You can designate "
"the error message with the @code{@@error@{@}} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10073
msgid ""
"The @code{@@error@{@}} command is displayed as @samp{@error{}}, either the "
"word `error' in a box in the printed output, the word error followed by an "
"arrow in other formats or (when no arrow is available)  @samp{error-->}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10076 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10119
msgid "Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10082
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@example lisp\n"
"(+ 23 'x)\n"
"@@error@{@} Wrong type argument: integer-or-marker-p, x\n"
"@@end example\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10090
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(+ 23 'x)\n"
"@error{} Wrong type argument: integer-or-marker-p, x\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10095
msgid ""
"This indicates that the following error message is printed when you evaluate "
"the expression:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10098
#, no-wrap
msgid "Wrong type argument: integer-or-marker-p, x\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10101
msgid "The word @samp{@error{}} itself is not part of the error message."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10103
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@equiv}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10105
msgid "@nodedescription How to indicate equivalence."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10105
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@equiv@{@}} (@equiv{}): Indicating Equivalence"
msgstr ""

#.  oldname
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10108
msgid "Equivalence"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10108
#, no-wrap
msgid "Equivalence, indicating"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10109
#, no-wrap
msgid "equiv"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10117
msgid ""
"Sometimes two expressions produce identical results.  You can indicate the "
"exact equivalence of two forms with the @code{@@equiv@{@}} command.  The "
"@code{@@equiv@{@}} command is displayed as @samp{@equiv{}}, either a "
"standard mathematical equivalence sign (three parallel horizontal lines) or "
"(when that is not available) as the ASCII sequence @samp{==}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10124
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@example lisp\n"
"(make-sparse-keymap) @@equiv@{@} (list 'keymap)\n"
"@@end example\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10131
#, no-wrap
msgid "(make-sparse-keymap) @equiv{} (list 'keymap)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10136
msgid ""
"This indicates that evaluating @code{(make-sparse-keymap)} produces "
"identical results to evaluating @code{(list 'keymap)}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10138
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@point}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10140
msgid "@nodedescription How to indicate the location of point."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10140
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@point@{@}} (@point{}): Indicating Point in a Buffer"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10143
msgid "Point Glyph"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10143
#, no-wrap
msgid "Point, indicating in a buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10144
#, no-wrap
msgid "point"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10150
msgid ""
"Sometimes you need to show an example of text in an Emacs buffer.  In such "
"examples, the convention is to include the entire contents of the buffer in "
"question between two lines of dashes containing the buffer name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10155
msgid ""
"You can use the @samp{@@point@{@}} command to show the location of point in "
"the text in the buffer.  (The symbol for point, of course, is not part of "
"the text in the buffer; it indicates the place @emph{between} two characters "
"where point is located.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10159
msgid ""
"The @code{@@point@{@}} command is displayed as @samp{@point{}}, either a "
"pointed star or (when that is not available) the ASCII sequence @samp{-!-}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10162
msgid ""
"The following example shows the contents of buffer @file{foo} before and "
"after evaluating a Lisp command to insert the word @code{changed}."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10168
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"---------- Buffer: foo ----------\n"
"This is the @point{}contents of foo.\n"
"---------- Buffer: foo ----------\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10179
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(insert \"changed \")\n"
"    @result{} nil\n"
"---------- Buffer: foo ----------\n"
"This is the changed @point{}contents of foo.\n"
"---------- Buffer: foo ----------\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10184
msgid "In a Texinfo source file, the example is written like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10190
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@example\n"
"---------- Buffer: foo ----------\n"
"This is the @@point@{@}contents of foo.\n"
"---------- Buffer: foo ----------\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10197
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(insert \"changed \")\n"
"    @@result@{@} nil\n"
"---------- Buffer: foo ----------\n"
"This is the changed @@point@{@}contents of foo.\n"
"---------- Buffer: foo ----------\n"
"@@end example\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10200 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10202
#, no-wrap
msgid "Click Sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10202
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting GUI usage sequences."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10204
#, no-wrap
msgid "Click sequences"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10205
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sequence of clicks"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10206
#, no-wrap
msgid "GUI click sequence"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10208
#, no-wrap
msgid "clicksequence"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10213
msgid ""
"When documenting graphical interfaces, it is necessary to describe sequences "
"such as `Click on @samp{File}, then choose @samp{Open}, then @dots{}'.  "
"Texinfo offers commands @code{@@clicksequence} and @code{click} to represent "
"this, typically used like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10216
#, no-wrap
msgid "@dots{} @@clicksequence@{File @@click@{@} Open@} @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10223
#, no-wrap
msgid "@dots{} @clicksequence{File @click{} Open} @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10225
#, no-wrap
msgid "click"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10226
#, no-wrap
msgid "arrow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10229
msgid ""
"The @code{@@click} command produces a right arrow; this glyph is also "
"available independently via the command @code{@@arrow@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10230
#, no-wrap
msgid "clickstyle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10235
msgid ""
"The @code{@@clickstyle} command that could be used to change the glyph "
"produced by @code{@@click} is deprecated, you should not use it anymore.  "
"You can use @code{@@alias} (@pxref{@code{@@alias}}) instead to change the "
"glyph produced by @code{@@click}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10237
msgid "You can replace:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10239
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@clickstyle @@result\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10241
msgid "by"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10243
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@alias click=result\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10246
msgid "The @code{@@clickstyle} command will be removed in a future release."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10248
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting Unicode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10250
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting a Unicode character by code point."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10250
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting Unicode: @code{@@U}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10252
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unicode character, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10253
#, no-wrap
msgid "Code point of Unicode character, inserting by"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10254
#, no-wrap
msgid "U"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10259
msgid ""
"The command @code{@@U@{@var{hex}@}} inserts a representation of the Unicode "
"character U+@var{hex}.  For example, @code{@@U@{0132@}} inserts the Dutch "
"`IJ' ligature (`@U{0132}')."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10264
msgid ""
"The @var{hex} value should be at least four hex digits; leading zeros are "
"@emph{not} added.  In general, @var{hex} must specify a valid normal Unicode "
"character; e.g., U+10FFFF (the very last code point) is invalid by "
"definition, and thus cannot be inserted this way."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10265
#, no-wrap
msgid "ASCII, source document portability using"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10269
msgid ""
"@code{@@U} is useful for inserting occasional glyphs for which Texinfo has "
"no dedicated command, while allowing the Texinfo source to remain purely 7-"
"bit ASCII for maximum portability."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10270
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unicode and @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10281
msgid ""
"This command has many limitations---the same limitations as inserting "
"Unicode characters in UTF-8 or another binary form.  First and most "
"importantly, @TeX{} knows nothing about most of Unicode.  Supporting "
"specific additional glyphs upon request is possible, but it's not viable for "
"@file{texinfo.tex} to support whole additional scripts (Japanese, Urdu, "
"@dots{}).  The @code{@@U} command does nothing to change this.  If the "
"specified character is not supported in @TeX{}, an error is given.  @LaTeX{} "
"output has more possibilities regarding UTF-8, but could require extra code "
"to load fonts and declare how UTF-8 characters are output.  "
"(@xref{@code{@@documentencoding}}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10282
#, no-wrap
msgid "Entity reference in HTML et al."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10283
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{&#x@var{hex};}, output from @code{@@U}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10289
msgid ""
"In HTML and DocBook, the output from @code{@@U} is always an entity "
"reference of the form @samp{&#x@var{hex};}, as in @samp{&#x0132;} for the "
"example above.  This should work even when an HTML document uses some other "
"encoding (say, Latin@tie{}1) and the given character is not supported in "
"that encoding."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10293
msgid ""
"In Info and plain text, if the output encoding is not UTF-8, the output is "
"the ASCII sequence @samp{U+@var{hex}}, as in the six ASCII characters "
"@samp{U+0132} for the example above."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10295
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inserting Math"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10297
msgid "@nodedescription Formatting mathematical expressions."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10297
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting Mathematics"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10300
#, no-wrap
msgid "math"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10301
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematical expressions, inserting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10302
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formulas, mathematical"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10306
msgid ""
"You can write a mathematical expression, equation or formula using the "
"@code{@@math} command.  Write the @TeX{} math notation between braces, like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10309
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@math@{\\partial_\\alpha \\partial^\\alpha A^\\beta = \\mu_0 J^\\beta@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10314
msgid ""
"@code{@@math} is formatted inline when used inside a paragraph, like "
"@math{\\partial_\\alpha \\partial^\\alpha A^\\beta = \\mu_0 J^\\beta} in "
"this example."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10319
msgid ""
"By default, the @code{@@math} command has no special effect on the Info "
"output, merely outputting the contents verbatim.  You can use the "
"experimental @code{INFO_MATH_IMAGES} variable to produce images; see "
"@ref{Info and Plaintext Customization Variables}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10324
msgid ""
"For printed output, @code{@@math} switches into @TeX{} ``math mode''.  In "
"that context, @samp{\\} must be used instead of @samp{@@} for plain @TeX{} "
"math control sequences for symbols, functions, and so on."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10325
#, no-wrap
msgid "Math output for HTML"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10326
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML output @subentry math"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10341
msgid ""
"By default, the HTML output is only emphasized.  @command{texi2any} provides "
"three options for displaying properly formatted mathematics for HTML.  You "
"can select these with the @code{HTML_MATH} variable (@pxref{HTML "
"Customization for Math}).  With @code{HTML_MATH} set to @samp{l2h}, "
"@command{texi2any} attempts to use the @command{latex2html} program to "
"produce image files for mathematical material.  With the @samp{t4h} setting, "
"@command{texi2any} attempts to use the @command{tex4ht} program.  With the "
"@samp{mathjax} setting, @command{texi2any} inserts references in the output "
"files to MathJax scripts to format the material.  The MathJax option "
"requires JavaScript to be enabled in the browser to work.  See also "
"@ref{MathJax Customization Variables}, @ref{@command{latex2html} "
"Customization Variables} and @ref{@command{tex4ht} Customization Variables}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10342
#, no-wrap
msgid "Displayed equations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10343
#, no-wrap
msgid "Equations, displayed"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10344
#, no-wrap
msgid "displaymath"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10347
msgid ""
"For displayed equations, you can use the @code{@@displaymath} command.  "
"Example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10353
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@displaymath\n"
"f(x) = @{1\\over\\sigma\\sqrt@{2\\pi@}@}\n"
"e^@{-@{1\\over2@}\\left(@{x-\\mu\\over\\sigma@}\\right)^2@}\n"
"@@end displaymath\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: displaymath
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10361
msgid ""
"f(x) = {1\\over\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-{1\\over2}\\left({x-"
"\\mu\\over\\sigma}\\right)^2}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10368
msgid ""
"In general, the contents of @code{@@math} or @code{@@displaymath} should be "
"plain @TeX{} only, with no interspersed Texinfo commands.  If you do use any "
"Texinfo commands, then you should mark these with @samp{@@} as usual, rather "
"than @samp{\\} (e.g.@: @samp{@@var} rather than @samp{\\var}), but we do not "
"guarantee which Texinfo commands will work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10373
msgid ""
"Although @code{@@sub} and @code{@@sup} may work inside math mode in some "
"contexts, it is better to use @TeX{}'s @samp{_} and @samp{^} characters to "
"denote subscripts and superscripts within mathematical expressions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10378
msgid ""
"@LaTeX{}-specific code will only work when the output format is @LaTeX{}, "
"and with the @code{HTML_MATH} options (although @command{tex4ht} needs "
"@code{T4H_MATH_CONVERSION} to be set to @samp{latex}; "
"@pxref{@command{tex4ht} Customization Variables})."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10380
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{\\} \\ @r{(literal \\ in @code{@@math})}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10390
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\mathopsup"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10396
msgid ""
"Due to the conflict with Texinfo's @code{@@sup} command, you can access the "
"plain @TeX{} command @code{\\sup} as @code{\\mathopsup} instead, in the "
"unlikely occurrence that you want to do this (but only when processing with "
"@TeX{}, not when outputting @LaTeX{} nor with any of the @code{HTML_MATH} "
"options)."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10398
#, no-wrap
msgid "Breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10400
msgid "@nodedescription Forcing or preventing line and page breaks."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10400
#, no-wrap
msgid "Forcing and Preventing Breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10402
#, no-wrap
msgid "Forcing line and page breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10403
#, no-wrap
msgid "Making line and page breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10404
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preventing line and page breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10405
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line breaks @subentry awkward"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10406
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page breaks, awkward"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10411
msgid ""
"Line and page breaks can sometimes occur in the `wrong' place in one or "
"another form of output.  It's up to you to ensure that text looks right in "
"all the output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10417
msgid ""
"For example, in a printed manual, page breaks may occur awkwardly in the "
"middle of an example; to prevent this, you can hold text together using a "
"grouping command that keeps the text from being split across two pages.  "
"Conversely, you may want to force a page break where none would normally "
"occur."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10420
msgid ""
"You can use the break, break prevention, or pagination commands to fix "
"problematic line and page breaks."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10422 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10424
#, no-wrap
msgid "Break Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10424
msgid "@nodedescription Summary of break-related commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10427
msgid "The break commands create or allow line and paragraph breaks:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10429 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:19899
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@*"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10431
msgid "Force a line break."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10432 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20978
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@sp @var{n}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10434
msgid "Skip @var{n} blank lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10435 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10531
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:19906
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@-"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10437
msgid "Insert a discretionary hyphen."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10438 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10539
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20520
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@hyphenation@{@var{hy-phen-a-ted words}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10440
msgid "Define hyphen points in @var{hy-phen-a-ted words}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10443
msgid "These commands hold text together on a single line:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10445 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:21201
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@w@{@var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10447
msgid "Prevent @var{text} from being split and hyphenated across two lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10448 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:21083
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@tie@{@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10450
msgid "Insert a normal interword space at which a line break may not occur."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10454
msgid ""
"The pagination commands apply only to printed output, since other output "
"formats do not have pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10456 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20787
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@page"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10458
msgid "Start a new page."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10459 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20477
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@group"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10461
msgid "Hold text together that must appear on one page."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10462
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@need @var{mils}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10464
msgid "Start a new page if not enough space on this one."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10467
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line Breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10469
msgid "@nodedescription Forcing line breaks."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10469
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@*} and @code{@@/}: Generate and Allow Line Breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10471
#, no-wrap
msgid "* @r{(force line break)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10472
#, no-wrap
msgid "/ @r{(allow line break)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10474
#, no-wrap
msgid "*"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10475
#, no-wrap
msgid "/"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10478
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line breaks @subentry controlling"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10479
#, no-wrap
msgid "Controlling line breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10480
#, no-wrap
msgid "Breaks in a line"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10481
#, no-wrap
msgid "Force line break"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10482
#, no-wrap
msgid "Allow line break"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10486
msgid ""
"The @code{@@*} command forces a line break in all output formats.  The "
"@code{@@/} command allows a line break (printed manual only)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10488
msgid "Here is an example with @code{@@*}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10491
#, no-wrap
msgid "This sentence is broken @@*into two lines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10500
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This sentence is broken\n"
"into two lines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10508
msgid ""
"The @code{@@/} command can be useful within long URLs or other identifiers "
"where @TeX{} can't find a good place to break.  @TeX{} will automatically "
"break URLs at the natural places (@pxref{URL Line Breaking}), so only use "
"@code{@@/} if you need it.  @code{@@/} has no effect on the other output "
"formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10510
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@- @@hyphenation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10512
msgid "@nodedescription Helping @TeX{} with hyphenation points."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10512
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@-} and @code{@@hyphenation}: Hyphenation in Printed Output"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10515
msgid "- and hyphenation"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10515
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{-} - @r{(discretionary hyphen)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10517
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{-} -"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10519
#, no-wrap
msgid "hyphenation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10520
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hyphenation, helping @TeX{} do"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10521
#, no-wrap
msgid "Fine-tuning, and hyphenation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10529
msgid ""
"Although @TeX{}'s hyphenation algorithm is generally pretty good, it does "
"miss useful hyphenation points from time to time.  (Or, far more rarely, "
"insert an incorrect hyphenation.)  So, for documents with an unusual "
"vocabulary or when fine-tuning for a printed edition, you may wish to "
"specify hyphenation points explicitly.  Texinfo supports two commands for "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10538
msgid ""
"Insert a discretionary hyphen, i.e., a place where a word can be broken "
"across lines with a hyphen.  This is especially useful when you notice that "
"an overfull hbox is due to @TeX{} missing a hyphenation (@pxref{Overfull "
"hboxes}).  @TeX{} will not insert any hyphenation points itself into a word "
"containing @code{@@-}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10541
msgid "Give hyphenation points for certain words.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10543
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@hyphenation@{man-u-script man-u-scripts@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10549
msgid ""
"As shown, you put a @samp{-} at each hyphenation point. @TeX{} only uses the "
"specified hyphenation points when the words match exactly, so give all "
"necessary variants, such as plurals."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10553
msgid ""
"Non-printed output is not hyphenated, so none of these commands have any "
"effect in other output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10555
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@allowcodebreaks}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10557
msgid "@nodedescription Controlling line breaks within @@code text."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10557
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@allowcodebreaks}: Control Line Breaks in @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10560
#, no-wrap
msgid "allowcodebreaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10561
#, no-wrap
msgid "Breaks, within @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10562
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{-} -, breakpoint within @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10563
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hyphen, breakpoint within @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10564
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dash, breakpoint within @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10565
#, no-wrap
msgid "_, breakpoint within @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10566
#, no-wrap
msgid "Underscore, breakpoint within @code{@@code}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10572
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, @TeX{} considers breaking lines at @samp{-} and @samp{_} "
"characters within @code{@@code} and related commands "
"(@pxref{@code{@@code}}), more or less as if they were ``empty'' hyphenation "
"points."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10578
msgid ""
"This is necessary since many manuals, especially for Lisp-family languages, "
"must document very long identifiers.  On the other hand, some manuals don't "
"have this problem, and you may not wish to allow a line break at the "
"underscore in, for example, @code{SIZE_MAX}, or even worse, after any of the "
"four underscores in @code{__typeof__}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10580
msgid "So Texinfo provides this command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10583
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@allowcodebreaks false\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10590
msgid ""
"to prevent from breaking at @samp{-} or @samp{_} within @code{@@code}. You "
"can go back to allowing such breaks with @code{@@allowcodebreaks true}. "
"Write these commands on lines by themselves."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10594
msgid ""
"These commands can be given anywhere in the document.  For example, you may "
"have just one problematic paragraph where you need to turn off the breaks, "
"but want them in general, or vice versa."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10596
msgid "This command has no effect except in @TeX{} output."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10598
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@w}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10600
msgid "@nodedescription Preventing unwanted line breaks in text."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10600
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@w}@{@var{text}@}: Prevent Line Breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10603
#, no-wrap
msgid "w"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10604
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line breaks, preventing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10608
msgid ""
"@code{@@w@{@var{text}@}} outputs @var{text}, while prohibiting line breaks "
"within @var{text}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10609
#, no-wrap
msgid "Non-breakable space, fixed"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10610
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unbreakable space, fixed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10613
msgid ""
"Thus, you can use @code{@@w} to produce a non-breakable space, fixed at the "
"width of a normal interword space:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10616
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@w@{ @} @@w@{ @} @@w@{ @} indentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10623
#, no-wrap
msgid "@w{ } @w{ } @w{ } indentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10630
msgid ""
"The space from @code{@@w@{@w{ }@}}, as well as being non-breakable, also "
"will not stretch or shrink.  Sometimes that is what you want, for instance "
"if you're doing manual indenting.  However, usually you want a normal "
"interword space that does stretch and shrink (in the printed output); for "
"that, see the @code{@@tie} command in the next section."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10631
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hyphenation, preventing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10635
msgid ""
"In printed output, you can also use the @code{@@w} command to prevent a long "
"name or phrase being automatically hyphenated, for example if it happens to "
"fall near the end of a line."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10636
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keyword expansion, preventing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10637
#, no-wrap
msgid "Version control keywords, preventing expansion of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10638
#, no-wrap
msgid "$Id expansion, preventing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10643
msgid ""
"You can also use @code{@@w} to avoid unwanted keyword expansion in source "
"control systems.  For example, to literally write @t{@w{$}Id$} in your "
"document, use @code{@@w@{$@}Id$}.  This trick isn't effective for output "
"files in some output formats, though."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10645
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@tie}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10647
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting an unbreakable but varying space."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10647
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@tie@{@}}: Inserting an Unbreakable Space"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10650
#, no-wrap
msgid "tie"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10651
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tied space"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10652
#, no-wrap
msgid "Non-breakable space, variable"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10653
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unbreakable space, variable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10659
msgid ""
"The @code{@@tie@{@}} command produces a normal interword space at which a "
"line break may not occur.  Always write it with following (empty)  braces, "
"as usual for commands used within a paragraph.  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10662
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@TeX@{@} was written by Donald E.@@tie@{@}Knuth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10668
#, no-wrap
msgid "@TeX{} was written by Donald E.@tie{}Knuth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10672
msgid ""
"There are two important differences between @code{@@tie@{@}} and "
"@code{@@w@{@w{ }@}}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10678
msgid ""
"The space produced by @code{@@tie@{@}} will stretch and shrink slightly "
"along with the normal interword spaces in the paragraph; the space produced "
"by @code{@@w@{@w{ }@}} will not vary."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10683
msgid ""
"@code{@@tie@{@}} allows hyphenation of the surrounding words, while "
"@code{@@w@{@w{ }@}} inhibits hyphenation of those words (for @TeX{}nical "
"reasons, namely that it produces an @samp{\\hbox})."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10687
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@sp}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10689
msgid "@nodedescription Inserting blank lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10689
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@sp @var{n}}: Insert Blank Lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10693
#, no-wrap
msgid "Space, inserting vertical"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10694
#, no-wrap
msgid "Blank lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10695
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line spacing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10700
msgid ""
"A line beginning with and containing only @code{@@sp @var{n}} generates "
"@var{n} blank lines of space.  @code{@@sp} also forces a paragraph break.  "
"For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10707
msgid "generates two blank lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10710
msgid ""
"The @code{@@sp} command is most often used inside a @code{@@titlepage} block."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10712
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@page}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10714
msgid "@nodedescription Forcing the start of a new page."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10714
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@page}: Start a New Page"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10717
#, no-wrap
msgid "page"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10718
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page breaks, forcing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10725
msgid ""
"A line containing only @code{@@page} starts a new page in a printed manual.  "
"In other formats, without the concept of pages, it starts a new paragraph.  "
"A @code{@@page} command is often used in a @code{@@titlepage} section of a "
"Texinfo file to start the copyright page."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10727
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@group}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10729
msgid "@nodedescription Preventing unwanted page breaks."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10729
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@group}: Prevent Page Breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10732
#, no-wrap
msgid "group"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10733
#, no-wrap
msgid "Group (hold text together vertically)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10734
#, no-wrap
msgid "Holding text together vertically"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10735
#, no-wrap
msgid "Vertically holding text together"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10743
msgid ""
"The @code{@@group} command is used to hold an example together on one page.  "
"Use it inside an @code{@@example} or similar construct to begin an "
"unsplittable vertical group, which will appear entirely on one page in the "
"printed output.  Terminate the group by a line containing only @code{@@end "
"group}.  This command has an effect in @TeX{} output only."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10747
msgid ""
"The @code{@@group} and @code{@@end group} commands need to be inside the "
"@code{@@example} and @code{@@end example} commands, thus:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10755
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@example\n"
"@@group\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end group\n"
"@@end example\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10764
msgid ""
"Although @code{@@group} would make sense conceptually in a wide variety of "
"contexts, its current implementation works only within @code{@@example} and "
"variants, and within @code{@@display}, @code{@@format}, @code{@@flushleft} "
"and @code{@@flushright}.  @xref{Quotations and Examples}.  (What all these "
"commands have in common is that each line of input produces a line of "
"output.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10780
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@need}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10782
msgid "@nodedescription Another way to prevent unwanted page breaks."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10782
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@need @var{mils}}: Prevent Page Breaks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10785
#, no-wrap
msgid "need"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10786
#, no-wrap
msgid "Need space at page bottom"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10787
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mils, argument to @code{@@need}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10794
msgid ""
"A line containing only @code{@@need @var{n}} starts a new page in a printed "
"manual if fewer than @var{n} mils (thousandths of an inch)  remain on the "
"current page.  Do not use braces around the argument @var{n}.  The "
"@code{@@need} command has no effect on other output formats since they are "
"not paginated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10799
msgid ""
"This paragraph is preceded by a @code{@@need} command that starts a new page "
"in printed output if fewer than 800 mils (eight-tenths inch)  remain on the "
"page.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10804
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@need 800\n"
"This paragraph is preceded by @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10807
#, no-wrap
msgid "Orphans, preventing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10810
msgid ""
"The @code{@@need} command is useful for preventing orphans: single lines at "
"the bottoms of printed pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10812 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10814
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10814
msgid "@nodedescription Describing functions and the like uniformly."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10815
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10820
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deffn} command and the other @dfn{definition commands} enable "
"you to describe functions, variables, macros, commands, user options, "
"special forms and other entities in a uniform format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10825
msgid ""
"In the output, the name of the entity is shown with any arguments, along "
"with the entity category ---`Function', `Variable', or whatever.  "
"Underneath, the body of the definition is indented.  The name of the entity "
"is also entered into the appropriate index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10829
msgid ""
"All the definition commands follow a similar format.  This chapter starts by "
"describing @code{@@deffn} before detailing all the other definition commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10831
#, no-wrap
msgid "Def Cmd Template"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10833
msgid "@nodedescription Writing descriptions using definition commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10833
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Template for a Definition"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10834
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition template"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10835
#, no-wrap
msgid "Template for a definition"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10842
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deffn @var{category} @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}\n"
"@var{body-of-definition}\n"
"@@end deffn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10854
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deffn} command is used for definitions of entities that resemble "
"functions---entities that may take arguments.  Write the @code{@@deffn} "
"command at the beginning of a line and follow it on the same line by the "
"category of the entity, the name of the entity itself, and its arguments (if "
"any).  Then write the body of the definition on succeeding lines.  Finally, "
"end the definition with an @code{@@end deffn} command written on a line of "
"its own."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10866
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deffn Command forward-word count\n"
"This command moves point forward @@var@{count@} words\n"
"(or backward if @@var@{count@} is negative). @dots{}\n"
"@@end deffn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: deffn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10873
#, no-wrap
msgid "Command forward-word count"
msgstr ""

#. type: deffn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10876
msgid ""
"This command moves point forward @var{count} words (or backward if "
"@var{count} is negative). @dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10882
msgid ""
"Capitalize the category name like a title.  If the name of the category "
"contains spaces, as in the phrase `Interactive Command', enclose it in "
"braces.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10888
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deffn @{Interactive Command@} isearch-forward\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end deffn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10898
msgid ""
"Otherwise, the second word will be mistaken for the name of the entity.  As "
"a general rule, when any of the arguments in the heading line @emph{except} "
"the last one are more than one word, you need to enclose them in braces.  "
"This may also be necessary if the text contains commands, for example, "
"@samp{@{declaraci@@'on@}} if you are writing in Spanish."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10903
msgid ""
"The category is output in a different location for different output "
"formats.  For example, in the Info file, the category appears at the "
"beginning of the first line of the definition.  With @TeX{} output, the "
"category is printed next to the right margin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10905
msgid "@code{@@deffn} enters names into the index of functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10913
msgid ""
"Three predefined, specialized variations of @code{@@deffn} (@code{@@defun}, "
"@code{@@defmac}, and @code{@@defspec}) specify the category for you: "
"``Function'', ``Macro'', and ``Special Form'' respectively.  (In Lisp, a "
"special form is an entity much like a function.)  Similarly, the general "
"@code{@@defvr} command is accompanied by several specialized variations for "
"describing particular kinds of variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10915
#, no-wrap
msgid "Def Cmd Continuation Lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10917
msgid "@nodedescription Continuing the heading over source lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10917
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition Command Continuation Lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10918
#, no-wrap
msgid "Continuation lines in definition commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10919
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition command headings, continuing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10920
#, no-wrap
msgid "@sortas{@@} @samp{@@} as continuation in definition commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10926
msgid ""
"The heading line of a definition command can get very long.  Therefore, "
"Texinfo has a special syntax allowing them to be continued over multiple "
"lines of the source file: a lone @samp{@@} at the end of each line to be "
"continued.  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10932
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defun fn-name @@\n"
"  arg1 arg2 arg3\n"
"This is the basic continued defun.\n"
"@@end defun\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: defun
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10937
#, no-wrap
msgid "fn-name   arg1 arg2 arg3"
msgstr ""

#. type: defun
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10939
msgid "This is the basic continued defun."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10944
msgid ""
"As you can see, the continued lines are combined, as if they had been typed "
"on one source line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10948
msgid ""
"Although this example only shows a one-line continuation, continuations may "
"extend over any number of lines, in the same way; put an @code{@@} at the "
"end of each line to be continued."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10949
#, no-wrap
msgid "Whitespace, collapsed around continuations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10950
#, no-wrap
msgid "Collapsing whitespace around continuations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10955
msgid ""
"In general, any number of spaces or tabs before the @code{@@} continuation "
"character are collapsed into a single space.  There is one exception: the "
"Texinfo processors will not fully collapse whitespace around a continuation "
"inside braces.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10959
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deffn @{Category @@\n"
"  Name@} @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10965
msgid ""
"The output (not shown) has excess space between `Category' and `Name'.  To "
"avoid this, elide the unwanted whitespace in your input, or put the "
"continuation @code{@@} outside braces."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10970
msgid ""
"@code{@@} does not function as a continuation character in @emph{any} other "
"context.  Ordinarily, @samp{@@} followed by a whitespace character (space, "
"tab, newline) produces a normal interword space (@pxref{Multiple Spaces})."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10972
#, no-wrap
msgid "Optional Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10974
msgid "@nodedescription Handling optional and repeated arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10974
#, no-wrap
msgid "Optional and Repeated Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10975
#, no-wrap
msgid "Optional and repeated arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10976
#, no-wrap
msgid "Repeated and optional arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10977
#, no-wrap
msgid "Arguments, repeated and optional"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10978
#, no-wrap
msgid "Syntax, optional & repeated arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10979
#, no-wrap
msgid "Meta-syntactic chars for arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10986
msgid ""
"Some entities take optional or repeated arguments.  One convention for "
"indicating these uses square brackets and ellipses: an argument enclosed "
"within square brackets is optional, and an argument followed by an ellipsis "
"is optional and may be repeated more than once."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10991
msgid ""
"Thus, [@var{optional-arg}] means that @var{optional-arg} is optional and "
"@var{repeated-args}@code{@dots{}} stands for zero or more arguments.  "
"Parentheses are used when several arguments are grouped into additional "
"levels of list structure in Lisp."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10994
msgid ""
"Here is the definition line of an example of an imaginary (complicated) "
"special form:"
msgstr ""

#. type: defspec
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:10996
#, no-wrap
msgid "foobar (var [from to [inc]]) body@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11007
msgid ""
"In this example, the arguments @var{from} and @var{to} are optional, but "
"must both be present or both absent.  If they are present, @var{inc} may "
"optionally be specified as well.  These arguments are grouped with the "
"argument @var{var} into a list, to distinguish them from @var{body}, which "
"includes all remaining elements of the form."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11010
msgid ""
"In a Texinfo source file, this @code{@@defspec} line is written like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11013
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defspec foobar (var [from to [inc]]) body@@dots@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11016
#, no-wrap
msgid "No Space After Definition Name"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11018
msgid "@nodedescription How to omit space after name."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11018
#, no-wrap
msgid "Omitting the Space After a Definition Name"
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11020
#, no-wrap
msgid "txidefnamenospace"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11025
msgid ""
"As a matter of style or as demanded by the syntax of a programming language, "
"you may wish to omit any space after a name in a definition, occurring "
"before an opening bracket.  To do this, set the @samp{txidefnamenospace} "
"flag (@pxref{@code{@@set @@value}}).  For example, this input"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11031
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@set txidefnamenospace\n"
"@@deffn Builtin m4_index (string, substring)\n"
"@@dots@{@}\n"
"@@end deffn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: deffn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11037
#, no-wrap
msgid "Builtin m4_index (string, substring)"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypeivar
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11039 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11232
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11481 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11578
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11602 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11626
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11650
msgid "@dots{}"
msgstr ""

