#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11103
#, no-wrap
msgid "Def Cmds in Detail"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11105
msgid "@nodedescription Reference for all the definition commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11105
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Definition Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11108
msgid "This section describes all the definition commands of Texinfo."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11110
#, no-wrap
msgid "Functions Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11112
msgid "@nodedescription Commands for functions and similar entities."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11112
#, no-wrap
msgid "Functions and Similar Entities"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11114
#, no-wrap
msgid "Function definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11115
#, no-wrap
msgid "Command definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11116
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro definitions, programming-language"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11120
msgid ""
"This section describes the commands for describing functions and similar "
"entities with simple arguments:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11122
#, no-wrap
msgid "deffn"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11123 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20148
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deffn @var{category} @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11133
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deffn} command is the general definition command for functions, "
"interactive commands, and similar entities that may take simple arguments.  "
"You must choose a term to describe the category of entity being defined; for "
"example, ``Function'' could be used if the entity is a function.  The "
"@code{@@deffn} command is written at the beginning of a line and is followed "
"on the same line by the category of entity being described, the name of this "
"particular entity, and its arguments, if any.  Terminate the definition with "
"@code{@@end deffn} on a line of its own."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11136
msgid "For example, here is a definition:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11142
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deffn Command forward-char nchars\n"
"Move point forward @@var@{nchars@} characters.\n"
"@@end deffn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11148
msgid ""
"This shows a rather terse definition for a ``command'' named @code{forward-"
"char} with one argument, @var{nchars}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11160
msgid ""
"Within the text of the description, write an argument name explicitly with "
"@code{@@var} to refer to the value of the argument.  In the example above, "
"we used @samp{@@var@{nchars@}} in this way."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11167
msgid ""
"In the extremely unusual case when an argument name contains @samp{--}, or "
"another character sequence which is treated specially (@pxref{Conventions}), "
"use @code{@@code} around the special characters.  This avoids the conversion "
"to typographic en-dashes and em-dashes."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11168
#, no-wrap
msgid "defun"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11169
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defun @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11174
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defun} command is the definition command for functions with "
"simple arguments.  @code{@@defun} is equivalent to @samp{@@deffn Function "
"@dots{}}.  Terminate the definition with @code{@@end defun} on a line of its "
"own."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11175
#, no-wrap
msgid "defmac"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11176
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defmac @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11180
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defmac} command is the definition command for macros.  "
"@code{@@defmac} is equivalent to @samp{@@deffn Macro @dots{}} and works like "
"@code{@@defun}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11181
#, no-wrap
msgid "defspec"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11182
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defspec @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11188
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defspec} command is the definition command for special forms.  "
"(In Lisp, a special form is an entity much like a function; @pxref{Special "
"Forms,,, elisp, GNU Emacs Lisp Reference Manual}.)  @code{@@defspec} is "
"equivalent to @samp{@@deffn @{Special Form@} @dots{}} and works like "
"@code{@@defun}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11191
msgid "All these commands create entries in the index of functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11193
#, no-wrap
msgid "Typed Functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11195
msgid "@nodedescription Commands for functions in typed languages."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11195
#, no-wrap
msgid "Functions in Typed Languages"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11197
#, no-wrap
msgid "Typed functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11198
#, no-wrap
msgid "Functions, in typed languages"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11203
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypefn} command and its variants are generic commands for "
"describing functions.  They are particularly suitable for languages in which "
"you must declare types of variables and functions, such as C and C++."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11205
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypefn"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11206 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20217
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftypefn @var{category} @var{data-type} @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11213
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypefn} command is the general definition command for "
"functions and similar entities that may take arguments and that could be "
"typed.  The @code{@@deftypefn} command is written at the beginning of a line "
"and is followed on the same line by the category of entity being described, "
"information on the returned value, the name of this particular entity, and "
"its arguments, if any."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11224
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftypefn @{Library Function@} int foobar @@\n"
"  (int @@var@{foo@}, float @@var@{bar@})\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end deftypefn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11230
#, no-wrap
msgid "{Library Function} int foobar  (int @var{foo}, float @var{bar})"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11239
msgid ""
"This means that @code{foobar} is a ``library function'' that returns an "
"@code{int}, and its arguments are @var{foo} (an @code{int}) and @var{bar} (a "
"@code{float}).  @samp{Library Function} has to be enclosed in braces to make "
"it a single argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11244
msgid ""
"When using @code{@@deftypefn} command and variations, you should mark "
"parameter names with @code{@@var} to distinguish these from data type names, "
"keywords, and other parts of the literal syntax of the programming language."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11255
msgid ""
"If you are describing a procedure in a language that has packages, such as "
"Ada, you might consider using @code{@@deftypefn} in the following manner:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11263
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftypefn stacks private push @@\n"
"       (@@var@{s@}:in out stack; @@\n"
"       @@var@{n@}:in integer)\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end deftypefn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11270
msgid ""
"(In these examples the @code{@@deftypefn} arguments are shown using "
"continuations (@pxref{Def Cmd Continuation Lines}), but could be on a single "
"line.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11275
msgid ""
"In this instance, the procedure is classified as belonging to the package "
"@code{stacks} rather than classified as a `procedure' and its data type is "
"described as @code{private}.  (The name of the procedure is @code{push}, and "
"its arguments are @var{s} and @var{n}.)  Output:"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11276
#, no-wrap
msgid "stacks private push @"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11280
msgid "(@var{s}:in out stack; @ @var{n}:in integer)  @dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11287
msgid ""
"@code{@@deftypefn} and variants could be used when it is convenient to "
"interpret the arguments list as literal computer code, marking argument "
"names with @code{@@var}.  It is not necessary for any type names to appear, "
"and you can give an empty return type as @samp{@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11301
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftypefn stats @{@} t.test (@@var@{x@}, @@var@{y@} = NULL,@@\n"
"  @@w@{@@var@{alternative@} = c(\"two.sided\", \"less\", \"greater\")@},@@\n"
"  @@w@{@@var@{mu@} = 0@}, @@w@{@@var@{paired@} = FALSE@},@@\n"
"  @@w@{@@var@{var.equal@} = FALSE@},@@\n"
"  @@w@{@@var@{conf.level@} = 0.95@}, @@dots@{@})\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end deftypefn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11306
#, no-wrap
msgid "stats {} t.test (@var{x}, @var{y} = NULL,@"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11312
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  @w{@var{alternative} = c(\"two.sided\", \"less\", \"greater\")},@\n"
"  @w{@var{mu} = 0}, @w{@var{paired} = FALSE},@\n"
"  @w{@var{var.equal} = FALSE},@\n"
"  @w{@var{conf.level} = 0.95}, @dots{})\n"
"@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11322
msgid ""
"The information on return values may be used to name the return variables. "
"For example, in Perl, returned scalars may be given the @var{$result} and "
"@var{$status} name in the following manner:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11328
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftypefn @{Sub@} @{(@@var@{$result@}, @@var@{$status@}) =@} @@\n"
"                     process (@@var@{$input@})\n"
"@dots{} Set @@var@{$status@} to 0 in case of failure @dots{}\n"
"@@end deftypefn\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11331
msgid "to get output like"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11332
#, no-wrap
msgid "{Sub} {(@var{$result}, @var{$status}) =} process (@var{$input})"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypefn
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11334
msgid "@dots{} Set @var{$status} to 0 in case of failure @dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11338
msgid ""
"@code{@@deftypefn} creates an entry in the index of functions for @var{name}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11339
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypefun"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11340
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftypefun @var{data-type} @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11344
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypefun} command is the specialized definition command for "
"functions.  The command is equivalent to @samp{@@deftypefn Function @dots{}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11347
msgid ""
"@code{@@deftypefun} creates an entry in the index of functions for "
"@var{name}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11350
#, no-wrap
msgid "Return type, own line for"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11351
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypefnnewline"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11356
msgid ""
"Ordinarily, the return type is printed on the same line as the function name "
"and arguments, as shown above.  In source code, GNU style for typed "
"functions is to put the return type on a line by itself.  So Texinfo "
"provides an option to do that: @code{@@deftypefnnewline on}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11363
msgid ""
"This affects the generic functions only---not untyped functions with simple "
"arguments such as @code{@@deffn}, not typed variables, etc.  Specifically, "
"it affects the commands in this section, and the analogous commands for "
"object-oriented languages, namely @code{@@deftypeop} and "
"@code{@@deftypemethod} (@pxref{Object-Oriented Methods})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11365
msgid "Specifying @code{@@deftypefnnewline off} reverts to the default."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11367
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variables Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11369
msgid "@nodedescription Commands for variables and similar entities."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11369
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variables and Similar Entities"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11373
msgid "Here are the commands for defining variables and similar entities:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11375
#, no-wrap
msgid "defvr"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11376 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20278
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defvr @var{category} @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11384
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defvr} command is a general definition command for something "
"like a variable---an entity that records a value.  You must choose a term to "
"describe the category of entity being defined; for example, ``Variable'' "
"could be used if the entity is a variable.  Write the @code{@@defvr} command "
"at the beginning of a line and follow it on the same line by the category of "
"the entity and the name of the entity."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11389
msgid ""
"We recommend capitalizing the category name like a title.  If the name of "
"the category contains spaces, as in the name ``User Option'', enclose it in "
"braces.  Otherwise, the second word will be mistaken for the name of the "
"entity.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11397
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defvr @{User Option@} fill-column\n"
"This buffer-local variable specifies\n"
"the maximum width of filled lines.\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end defvr\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11402
msgid "Terminate the definition with @code{@@end defvr} on a line of its own."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11404
msgid ""
"@code{@@defvr} creates an entry in the index of variables for @var{name}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11405
#, no-wrap
msgid "defvar"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11406
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defvar @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11410
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defvar} command is the definition command for variables.  "
"@code{@@defvar} is equivalent to @samp{@@defvr Variable @dots{}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11419
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defvar kill-ring\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end defvar\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11424
msgid ""
"@code{@@defvar} creates an entry in the index of variables for @var{name}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11425
#, no-wrap
msgid "defopt"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11426
#, no-wrap
msgid "User options, marking"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11427
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defopt @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11433
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defopt} command is the definition command for @dfn{user "
"options}, i.e., variables intended for users to change according to taste; "
"Emacs has many such (@pxref{Variables,,, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}).  "
"@code{@@defopt} is equivalent to @samp{@@defvr @{User Option@} @dots{}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11436
#, no-wrap
msgid "Typed Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11438
msgid "@nodedescription Commands for variables in typed languages."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11438
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variables in Typed Languages"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11440
#, no-wrap
msgid "Typed variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11441
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variables, in typed languages"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11448
msgid ""
"Variables in typed languages are handled in a manner similar to functions in "
"typed languages.  @xref{Typed Functions}.  The general definition command "
"@code{@@deftypevr} corresponds to @code{@@deftypefn} and the specialized "
"definition command @code{@@deftypevar} corresponds to @code{@@deftypefun}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11450
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypevr"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11451 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20261
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftypevr @var{category} @var{data-type} @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11457
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypevr} command is the general definition command for "
"something like a variable in a typed language---an entity that records a "
"value.  You must choose a term to describe the category of the entity being "
"defined; for example, ``Variable'' could be used if the entity is a variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11462
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypevr} command is written at the beginning of a line and is "
"followed on the same line by the category of the entity being described, the "
"data type, and the name of this particular entity."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11472
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftypevr @{Global Flag@} int enable\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end deftypevr\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypevr
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11479
#, no-wrap
msgid "{Global Flag} int enable"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11484
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypevar"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11485
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftypevar @var{data-type} @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11489
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypevar} command is the specialized definition command for "
"variables in typed languages.  @code{@@deftypevar} is equivalent to "
"@samp{@@deftypevr Variable @dots{}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11492
msgid "These commands create entries in the index of variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11494 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11496
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data Types"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11496
msgid "@nodedescription The definition command for data types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11499
msgid "Here is the command for data types:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11501
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftp"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11502
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftp @var{category} @var{name} @var{attributes}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11512
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftp} command is the generic definition command for data "
"types.  The command is written at the beginning of a line and is followed on "
"the same line by the category, by the name of the type (which is a word like "
"@code{int} or @code{float}), and then by names of attributes of objects of "
"that type.  Thus, you could use this command for describing @code{int} or "
"@code{float}, in which case you could use @code{data type} as the category.  "
"(A data type is a category of certain objects for purposes of deciding which "
"operations can be performed on them.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11517
msgid ""
"In Lisp, for example, @dfn{pair} names a particular data type, and an object "
"of that type has two slots called the @sc{car} and the @sc{cdr}.  Here is "
"how you would write the first line of a definition of @code{pair}."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11523
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftp @{Data type@} pair car cdr\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end deftp\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11527
msgid "@code{@@deftp} creates an entry in the index of data types."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11530
#, no-wrap
msgid "Abstract Objects"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11532
msgid "@nodedescription Commands for object-oriented programming."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11532
#, no-wrap
msgid "Object-Oriented Programming"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11534
#, no-wrap
msgid "Object-oriented programming"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11542
msgid ""
"Here are the commands for formatting descriptions about abstract objects, "
"such as are used in object-oriented programming.  A class is a defined type "
"of abstract object.  An instance of a class is a particular object that has "
"the type of the class.  An instance variable is a variable that belongs to "
"the class but for which each instance has its own value."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11546
msgid "Variables: Object-Oriented Variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11546
msgid "Methods: Object-Oriented Methods"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11549 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11550
#, no-wrap
msgid "Object-Oriented Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11552
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variables, object-oriented"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11556
msgid ""
"These commands allow you to define different sorts of variables in object-"
"oriented programming languages."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11558
#, no-wrap
msgid "defcv"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11559 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20141
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defcv @var{category} @var{class} @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11565
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defcv} command is the general definition command for variables "
"associated with classes in object-oriented programming.  The @code{@@defcv} "
"command is followed by three arguments: the category of thing being defined, "
"the class to which it belongs, and its name.  For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11571
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defcv @{Class Option@} Window border-pattern\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end defcv\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: defcv
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11576
#, no-wrap
msgid "{Class Option} Window border-pattern"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11581
msgid "@code{@@defcv} creates an entry in the index of variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11582
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypecv"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11583 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20212
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftypecv @var{category} @var{class} @var{data-type} @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11588
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypecv} command is the definition command for typed class "
"variables in object-oriented programming.  It is analogous to @code{@@defcv} "
"with the addition of the @var{data-type} parameter to specify the type of "
"the instance variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11594
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftypecv @{Class Option@} Window int border-pattern\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end deftypecv\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypecv
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11600
#, no-wrap
msgid "{Class Option} Window int border-pattern"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11605
msgid "@code{@@deftypecv} creates an entry in the index of variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11606
#, no-wrap
msgid "defivar"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11607
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defivar @var{class} @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11612
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defivar} command is the definition command for instance "
"variables in object-oriented programming.  @code{@@defivar} is equivalent to "
"@samp{@@defcv @{Instance Variable@} @dots{}}.  For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11618
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defivar Window border-pattern\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end defivar\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: defivar
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11624
#, no-wrap
msgid "Window border-pattern"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11629
msgid "@code{@@defivar} creates an entry in the index of variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11630
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypeivar"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11631
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftypeivar @var{class} @var{data-type} @var{name}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11636
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypeivar} command is the definition command for typed "
"instance variables in object-oriented programming.  It is analogous to "
"@code{@@defivar} with the addition of the @var{data-type} parameter to "
"specify the type of the instance variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11642
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@deftypeivar Window int border-pattern\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end deftypeivar\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypeivar
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11648
#, no-wrap
msgid "Window @code{int} border-pattern"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11653
msgid "@code{@@deftypeivar} creates an entry in the index of variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11657 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11658
#, no-wrap
msgid "Object-Oriented Methods"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11660
#, no-wrap
msgid "Methods, object-oriented"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11666
msgid ""
"These commands allow you to define different sorts of function-like entities "
"resembling methods in object-oriented programming languages.  These entities "
"take arguments, as functions do, but are associated with particular classes "
"of objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11669
#, no-wrap
msgid "defop"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11670 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20187
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defop @var{category} @var{class} @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11673
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defop} command is the definition command for these method-like "
"entities with simple arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11678
msgid ""
"For example, some systems have constructs called @dfn{wrappers} that are "
"associated with classes as methods are, but that act more like macros than "
"like functions.  You could use @code{@@defop Wrapper} to describe one of "
"these."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11688
msgid ""
"Sometimes it is useful to distinguish methods and @dfn{operations}.  You can "
"think of an operation as the specification for a method.  Thus, a window "
"system might specify that all window classes have a method named "
"@code{expose}; we would say that this window system defines an @code{expose} "
"operation on windows in general.  Typically, the operation has a name and "
"also specifies the pattern of arguments; all methods that implement the "
"operation must accept the same arguments, since applications that use the "
"operation do so without knowing which method will implement it."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11694
msgid ""
"Often it makes more sense to document operations than methods.  For example, "
"window application developers need to know about the @code{expose} "
"operation, but need not be concerned with whether a given class of windows "
"has its own method to implement this operation.  To describe this operation, "
"you would write:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11697
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defop Operation windows expose\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11703
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defop} command is written at the beginning of a line and is "
"followed on the same line by the overall name of the category of operation, "
"the name of the class of the operation, the name of the operation, and its "
"arguments, if any."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11706
msgid ""
"@code{@@defop} creates an entry, such as `@code{expose} on @code{windows}', "
"in the index of functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11707
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypeop"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11708 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20250
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftypeop @var{category} @var{class} @var{data-type} @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11715
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypeop} command is the generic definition command for "
"operations in object-oriented programming.  It is particularly suitable for "
"typed object-oriented languages. It is similar to @code{@@defop} with the "
"addition of the @var{data-type} parameter to specify information on the "
"return value of the method, for example the return type."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11716
#, no-wrap
msgid "defmethod"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11717
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@defmethod @var{class} @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11722
msgid ""
"The @code{@@defmethod} command is the definition command for methods in "
"object-oriented programming.  A method is a kind of function that implements "
"an operation for a particular class of objects and its subclasses."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11733
msgid ""
"@code{@@defmethod} is equivalent to @samp{@@defop Method @dots{}}.  The "
"command is written at the beginning of a line and is followed by the name of "
"the class of the method, the name of the method, and its arguments, if any."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11741
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defmethod @code{bar-class} bar-method argument\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end defmethod\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11747
msgid ""
"illustrates the definition for a method called @code{bar-method} of the "
"class @code{bar-class}.  The method takes an argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11749
msgid "@code{@@defmethod} creates an entry in the index of functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11750
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypemethod"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11751
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@deftypemethod @var{class} @var{data-type} @var{name} @var{arguments}@dots{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11757
msgid ""
"The @code{@@deftypemethod} command is the definition command for methods in "
"object-oriented languages, in particular for typed languages such as C++ and "
"Java.  It is similar to the @code{@@defmethod} command with the addition of "
"the @var{data-type} parameter to specify information on the return value of "
"the method, for example the return type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11763
msgid ""
"The commands with information on return values are affected by the "
"@code{@@deftypefnnewline} option (@pxref{Typed Functions,, Functions in "
"Typed Languages})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11765 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11767
#, no-wrap
msgid "Generic Definition Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11767
msgid "@nodedescription Without automatic index entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11771
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides commands for definitions that do not produce automatic "
"index entries."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11772
#, no-wrap
msgid "defblock"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11773
#, no-wrap
msgid "defline"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11774
#, no-wrap
msgid "deftypeline"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11779
msgid ""
"You create a generic definition environment with @samp{@@defblock} paired "
"with @samp{@@end defblock}.  Within this environment, use a @code{@@defline} "
"or @code{@@deftypeline} line for each symbol you document.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11787
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defblock\n"
"@@defline Macro mac (arg1, arg2)\n"
"Description of mac.\n"
"@@deftypeline Builtin int foo (int @@var@{bar@})\n"
"Description of foo.\n"
"@@end defblock\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11791 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11821
msgid "This produces the output:"
msgstr ""

#. type: defline
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11793
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro mac (arg1, arg2)"
msgstr ""

#. type: defblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11795
msgid "Description of mac."
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypeline
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11795
#, no-wrap
msgid "Builtin int foo (int @var{bar})"
msgstr ""

#. type: defblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11797
msgid "Description of foo."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11803
msgid ""
"The syntax of @code{@@defline} is similar to that of @code{@@deffn}.  The "
"first argument gives a category for the definition.  Follow this with the "
"symbol name, followed by any parameters.  You should surround any arguments "
"containing spaces with braces."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11807
msgid ""
"You use @code{@@deftypeline} in a similar way to @code{@@deftypefn}, "
"following the category with a data type, and marking any parameters with "
"@code{@@var}. (@xref{Typed Functions}).)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11810
msgid ""
"To share the same description for multiple symbols, you can put several "
"@code{@@defline} lines together.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11817
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defblock\n"
"@@defline Function set-var (value)\n"
"@@defline @{Settable Variable@} var\n"
"Description of set-var and var.\n"
"@@end defblock\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: defline
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11823
#, no-wrap
msgid "Function set-var (value)"
msgstr ""

#. type: defline
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11824
#, no-wrap
msgid "{Settable Variable} var"
msgstr ""

#. type: defblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11826
msgid "Description of set-var and var."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11830
msgid ""
"It may be useful to define line macros (@pxref{Line Macros}) in combination "
"with these commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11833
#, no-wrap
msgid "Def Cmd Conventions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11835
msgid "@nodedescription Conventions for writing definitions."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11835
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conventions for Writing Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11836
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition conventions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11837
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conventions for writing definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11842
msgid ""
"A manual need not and should not contain more than one definition for a "
"given name.  An appendix containing a summary should use @code{@@table} "
"rather than the definition commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11849
msgid ""
"When you write a definition using @code{@@deffn}, @code{@@defun}, or one of "
"the other definition commands, please take care to use arguments that "
"indicate the meaning, as with the @var{count} argument to the @code{forward-"
"word} function.  Also, if the name of an argument contains the name of a "
"type, such as @var{integer}, take care that the argument actually is of that "
"type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11856
msgid ""
"@emph{Fonts.} As Texinfo is a semantic language, you should nearly never "
"need to specify font styles with explicit font commands in definitions "
"(@pxref{Fonts}).  However, you may be need to work around problems for "
"particular output formats and/or constructs.  Here are some possibilities:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11861
msgid ""
"Explicitly marking a Lisp keyword like @samp{&@var{keyword}} with "
"@code{@@r@{&keyword@}}, producing @r{&keyword}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11865
msgid ""
"Note such keywords in definition arguments are (at present)  rendered in "
"roman in @TeX{}, but this formatting is not done in any other output format."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11870
msgid ""
"@samp{@@r@{@@slanted@{argument@}@}}, producing @r{@slanted{argument}}, "
"similar to @code{@@var} but avoiding upper-casing its argument in Info "
"output."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11874
msgid ""
"In this and the previous point, @code{@@r} resets the font from that being "
"used in the definition line context (slanted or typewriter) to a roman, "
"upright style."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11882
msgid ""
"You could use @code{@@t} (or even @samp{@@r@{@@t@{@dots{}}, using @code{@@r} "
"to reset font styles) to mark literal syntax on a definition line where text "
"would otherwise be output in the slanted roman style.  @code{@@code} would "
"be inappropriate here as it adds quotation marks in Info output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11887
msgid ""
"Some degree of trial and error may be needed to get the result you want.  As "
"ever, how nested font commands combine depends on the output format, so "
"should be avoided where possible."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11891
msgid ""
"Hopefully, such usages are kept to a minimum.  One possibility is to wrap "
"these in @code{@@macro} (@pxref{Defining New Texinfo Commands}), allowing "
"these usages to be easily changed in the future."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11902
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro metasymbol @{argument@}\n"
"@@r@{@@t@{@@slanted@{\\argument\\@}@}@}\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11906
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro strongkeyword @{argument@}\n"
"@@r@{@@strong@{\\argument\\@}@}\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11910
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defun fset arg @@metasymbol@{[ +@} @@strongkeyword@{*keyword@}\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@end defun\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: macro
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11918
#, no-wrap
msgid "@r{@t{@slanted{\\argument\\}}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: macro
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11922
#, no-wrap
msgid "@r{@strong{\\argument\\}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: defun
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11924
#, no-wrap
msgid "fset arg @metasymbol{[ +} @strongkeyword{*keyword}"
msgstr ""

#. type: defun
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11926
#, no-wrap
msgid "@dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11930
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sample Function Definition"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11932
msgid "@nodedescription An example."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11932
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Sample Function Definition"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11933
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sample function definition"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11939
msgid ""
"Here is a definition from @ref{Calling Functions,,, elisp, The GNU Emacs "
"Lisp Reference Manual}, using the @code{@@defun} command.  The name of the "
"function, @code{apply}, follows immediately after the @code{@@defun} command "
"and it is followed, on the same line, by the parameter list."
msgstr ""

#. type: defun
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11941
#, no-wrap
msgid "apply function @r{&rest} arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: defun
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11947
msgid ""
"@code{apply} calls @var{function} with @var{arguments}, just like "
"@code{funcall} but with one difference: the last of @var{arguments} is a "
"list of arguments to give to @var{function}, rather than a single argument.  "
"We also say that this list is @dfn{appended} to the other arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: defun
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11952
msgid ""
"@code{apply} returns the result of calling @var{function}.  As with "
"@code{funcall}, @var{function} must either be a Lisp function or a primitive "
"function; special forms and macros do not make sense in @code{apply}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11962
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(setq f 'list)\n"
"    @result{} list\n"
"(apply f 'x 'y 'z)\n"
"@error{} Wrong type argument: listp, z\n"
"(apply '+ 1 2 '(3 4))\n"
"    @result{} 10\n"
"(apply '+ '(1 2 3 4))\n"
"    @result{} 10\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11965
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(apply 'append '((a b c) nil (x y z) nil))\n"
"    @result{} (a b c x y z)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: defun
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11969
msgid ""
"An interesting example of using @code{apply} is found in the description of "
"@code{mapcar}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11973
msgid "In the Texinfo source file, this example should look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11983
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defun apply function @@r@{&rest@} arguments\n"
"@@code@{apply@} calls @@var@{function@} with\n"
"@@var@{arguments@}, just like @@code@{funcall@} but with one\n"
"difference: the last of @@var@{arguments@} is a list of\n"
"arguments to give to @@var@{function@}, rather than a single\n"
"argument.  We also say that this list is @@dfn@{appended@}\n"
"to the other arguments.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:11991
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@code@{apply@} returns the result of calling\n"
"@@var@{function@}.  As with @@code@{funcall@},\n"
"@@var@{function@} must either be a Lisp function or a\n"
"primitive function; special forms and macros do not make\n"
"sense in @@code@{apply@}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12003
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@example\n"
"(setq f 'list)\n"
"    @@result@{@} list\n"
"(apply f 'x 'y 'z)\n"
"@@error@{@} Wrong type argument: listp, z\n"
"(apply '+ 1 2 '(3 4))\n"
"    @@result@{@} 10\n"
"(apply '+ '(1 2 3 4))\n"
"    @@result@{@} 10\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12007
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(apply 'append '((a b c) nil (x y z) nil))\n"
"    @@result@{@} (a b c x y z)\n"
"@@end example\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12013
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An interesting example of using @@code@{apply@} is found\n"
"in the description of @@code@{mapcar@}.\n"
"@@end defun\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12019
msgid ""
"In this manual, this function is listed in the Command Index under "
"@code{apply}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12021 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12023
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12025
#, no-wrap
msgid "Internationalization"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12023
msgid "@nodedescription Supporting languages other than English."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12030
msgid ""
"Texinfo has some support for writing in languages other than English, "
"although this area still needs considerable work.  (If you are the one "
"helping to translate the fixed strings written to documents, "
"@pxref{Internationalization of Document Strings}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12033
msgid ""
"For a list of the various accented and special characters Texinfo supports, "
"see @ref{Inserting Accents}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12035
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@documentlanguage}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12037
msgid "@nodedescription Declaring the current language."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12037
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@documentlanguage @var{ll}[_@var{cc}]}: Set the Document Language"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12039 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12040
#, no-wrap
msgid "documentlanguage"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12041
#, no-wrap
msgid "Language, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12042
#, no-wrap
msgid "Locale, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12043
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document language, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12047
msgid ""
"The @code{@@documentlanguage} command declares the current document locale.  "
"Write it on a line by itself, near the beginning of the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12050
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@documentlanguage @var{ll}[_@var{cc}]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12058
msgid ""
"Include a two-letter (or three-letter) ISO@tie{}639-2 language code "
"(@var{ll}) following the command name, optionally followed by an underscore "
"and two-letter ISO@tie{}3166 two-letter country code (@var{cc}).  If you "
"have a multilingual document, the intent is to be able to use this command "
"multiple times, to declare each language change."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12063
msgid ""
"As with GNU Gettext (@pxref{Top,,, gettext, Gettext}), if the country code "
"is omitted, the main dialect is assumed where possible.  For example, "
"@code{de} is equivalent to @code{de_DE} (German as spoken in Germany)."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12064
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document strings, translation of"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12073
msgid ""
"This command changes the translation of various @dfn{document strings} such "
"as ``see'' in cross-references (@pxref{Cross References}), ``Function'' in "
"defuns (@pxref{Definition Commands}), and so on.  Optionally, this command "
"may also affect how @command{texi2any} sorts indices (@pxref{Global "
"Customization Variables}, under @code{DOCUMENTLANGUAGE_COLLATION}).  If the "
"document language is not specified, the default English document strings are "
"output as they are."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12076
msgid ""
"Note that in Info output, some strings, such as ``Node:'', ``Next:'', "
"``Menu:'', etc., are keywords and so are not translated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12079
msgid ""
"In DocBook output @code{@@documentlanguage} sets the language for following "
"sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12082
msgid ""
"For @LaTeX{}, this command causes code to load the @samp{babel} package to "
"be output in the preamble, and @code{\\selectlanguage} to be output."
msgstr ""

#. type: file{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12083
#, no-wrap
msgid "txi-@var{cc}.tex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12089
msgid ""
"For @TeX{}, this command causes a file @file{txi-@var{locale}.tex} to be "
"read (if it exists).  If @code{@@documentlanguage} argument contains the "
"optional @samp{_@var{cc}} suffix, this is tried first.  For example, with "
"@code{@@documentlanguage de_DE}, @TeX{} first looks for @file{txi-"
"de_DE.tex}, then @file{txi-de.tex}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12096
msgid ""
"Such a @file{txi-*} file is intended to redefine the various English words "
"used in @TeX{} output, such as `Chapter', `See', and so on.  We are aware "
"that individual words like these cannot always be translated in isolation, "
"and that a very different strategy would be required for ideographic (among "
"other) scripts.  Help in improving Texinfo's language support is welcome."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12097
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hyphenation patterns, language-dependent"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12106
msgid ""
"@code{@@documentlanguage} also changes @TeX{}'s current hyphenation "
"patterns, if the @TeX{} program being run has the necessary support "
"included.  This will generally not be the case for @command{tex} itself, but "
"will usually be the case for up-to-date distributions of the extended @TeX{} "
"programs @command{etex} (DVI output) and @command{pdftex} (PDF output).  "
"@command{texi2dvi} will use the extended @TeX{}s if they are available "
"(@pxref{Format with @command{texi2dvi}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12107
#, no-wrap
msgid "ISO 639-2 language codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12108
#, no-wrap
msgid "ISO 3166 country codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12109
#, no-wrap
msgid "Language codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12110
#, no-wrap
msgid "Country codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12117
msgid ""
"Since the lists of language codes and country codes are updated relatively "
"frequently, we don't attempt to list them here.  The valid language codes "
"are on the official home page for ISO@tie{}639, @url{http://www.loc.gov/"
"standards/iso639-2/}.  The country codes and the official web site for "
"ISO@tie{}3166 can be found via @url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_3166}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12119
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@documentencoding}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12121
msgid "@nodedescription Declaring the input encoding."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12121
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@documentencoding @var{enc}}: Set Input Encoding"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12124
#, no-wrap
msgid "documentencoding"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12125
#, no-wrap
msgid "Encoding, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12126
#, no-wrap
msgid "Input encoding, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12127
#, no-wrap
msgid "Character set, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12128
#, no-wrap
msgid "Document input encoding"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12134
msgid ""
"By default, the input and output document encoding are assumed to be UTF-8, "
"the vast global character encoding, expressed in 8-bit bytes.  UTF-8 is "
"compatible with 7-bit ASCII.  It is recommended to use UTF-8 encoding for "
"Texinfo manuals."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12139
msgid ""
"The @code{@@documentencoding} command declares the input document encoding, "
"and also affects the encoding of the output.  Write it on a line by itself, "
"with a valid encoding specification following, near the beginning of the "
"file if your document encoding is not the default encoding."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12142
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@documentencoding @var{enc}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12151
msgid ""
"UTF-8 should always be the best choice for the encoding.  Texinfo still "
"supports additional encodings, mainly for compatibility with older "
"manuals@footnote{@command{texi2any} supports more encodings for Texinfo "
"manuals, potentially all the encodings supported by both Perl and iconv "
"(@pxref{Generic Charset Conversion,,, libc, The GNU C Library}).  The "
"support in output formats may be lacking, however, especially for @LaTeX{} "
"output.}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12153
#, no-wrap
msgid "US-ASCII"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12155
msgid "Character encoding based on the English alphabet."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12156
#, no-wrap
msgid "Euro symbol, and encodings"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12157
#, no-wrap
msgid "ISO-8859-1"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12158
#, no-wrap
msgid "ISO-8859-15"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12159
#, no-wrap
msgid "ISO-8859-2"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12165
msgid ""
"These specify the pre-UTF-8 standard encodings for Western European (the "
"first two) and Eastern European languages (the third), respectively.  ISO "
"8859-15 replaces some little-used characters from 8859-1 (e.g., precomposed "
"fractions) with more commonly needed ones, such as the Euro symbol (@euro{})."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12168
msgid ""
"A full description of the encodings is beyond our scope here; one useful "
"reference is @uref{http://czyborra.com/charsets/iso8859.html}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12169
#, no-wrap
msgid "koi8-r"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12171
msgid ""
"This was a commonly used encoding for the Russian language before UTF-8."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12172
#, no-wrap
msgid "koi8-u"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12174
msgid ""
"This was a commonly used encoding for the Ukrainian language before UTF-8."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12177
#, no-wrap
msgid "Local Variables section, for encoding"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12178
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info output, and encoding"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12183
msgid ""
"In Info output, a so-called `Local Variables' section (@pxref{File "
"Variables,,, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}) is output including the output "
"encoding.  This allows Info readers to set the encoding appropriately.  It "
"looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12188
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Local Variables:\n"
"coding: UTF-8\n"
"End:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12196
msgid ""
"By default, for Info and plain text output, @command{texi2any} outputs "
"accent constructs and special characters (such as @code{@@'e})  as the "
"actual UTF-8 sequence or 8-bit character in the output encoding where "
"possible.  If this is not possible, or if the option @option{--disable-"
"encoding} is given, an ASCII transliteration is used instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12197
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML output @subentry encodings"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12198
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{http-equiv}, and charset specification"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12199
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{<meta>} HTML tag, and charset specification"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12205
msgid ""
"In HTML output, a @samp{<meta>} tag is output, in the @samp{<head>} section "
"of the HTML, that specifies the output encoding.  Web servers and browsers "
"cooperate to use this information so the correct encoding is used to display "
"the page, if supported by the system.  That looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12209
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<meta http-equiv=\"Content-Type\" content=\"text/html;\n"
"     charset=utf-8\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12217
msgid ""
"In HTML and @LaTeX{} output, if @code{OUTPUT_CHARACTERS} is set "
"(@pxref{Customization Variables for Multiple Formats}), accent constructs "
"and special characters, such as @code{@@'e} or @code{``}, are output as the "
"actual UTF-8 sequence or 8-bit character in the output encoding where "
"possible.  Otherwise, HTML entities are used for those characters in HTML, "
"and @LaTeX{} macros are used in @LaTeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12224
msgid ""
"In DocBook output, if the encoding is different from UTF-8, an "
"@code{encoding} attribute is added to the XML declaration.  If "
"@code{OUTPUT_CHARACTERS} is set (@pxref{Customization Variables for Multiple "
"Formats}), accent constructs such as @code{@@'e} are output as the actual 8-"
"bit or UTF-8 character in the output encoding where possible.  Otherwise XML "
"entities are used for those constructs."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12225
#, no-wrap
msgid "Computer Modern fonts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12231
msgid ""
"In @TeX{} output, the characters which are supported in the standard "
"Computer Modern fonts are output accordingly.  For example, this means using "
"constructed accents rather than precomposed glyphs.  Using a missing "
"character generates a warning message, as does specifying an unimplemented "
"encoding."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12236
msgid ""
"Although modern @TeX{} systems support nearly every script in use in the "
"world, this wide-ranging support is not available in @file{texinfo.tex}, and "
"it's not feasible to duplicate or incorporate all that effort."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12244
msgid ""
"In @LaTeX{} output, code loading the @samp{inputenc} package is output based "
"on the encoding.  This, by itself, does not ensures that all the characters "
"from the input document can be subsequently output.  The fonts used in the "
"default case should cover the specific Texinfo glyphs, but not all the "
"possible encoded characters.  You may need to load different fonts in the "
"preamble and use @code{\\DeclareUnicodeCharacter} with a UTF-8 encoding.  "
"For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12248
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@latex\n"
"\\DeclareUnicodeCharacter@{017B@}@{\\.Z@}\n"
"@@end latex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12254
msgid ""
"Cross-references between Info files in different character encodings with "
"non-ASCII characters in node names fail.  We strongly recommend using UTF-8 "
"only as the encoding for manuals with non-ASCII characters in the "
"destinations of cross-references."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12256
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditionals"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12258
msgid "@nodedescription Specifying text for only some output cases."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12258
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditionally Visible Text"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12259
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditionally visible text"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12260
#, no-wrap
msgid "Text, conditionally visible"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12261
#, no-wrap
msgid "Visibility of conditional text"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12262
#, no-wrap
msgid "If text conditionally visible"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12268
msgid ""
"The @dfn{conditional commands} allow you to use different text for different "
"output formats, or for general conditions that you define.  For example, you "
"can use them to specify different text for the printed manual and the Info "
"output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12270
msgid "The conditional commands comprise the following categories."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12274
msgid "Commands specific to an output format (Info, @TeX{}, HTML, @dots{})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12278
msgid ""
"Commands specific to any output format @emph{excluding} a given one (e.g., "
"not Info, not @TeX{}, @dots{})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12282
msgid ""
"`Raw' formatter text for any output format, passed straight through with "
"minimal (but not zero) interpretation of @@-commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12286
msgid ""
"Format-independent variable substitutions, and testing if a variable is set "
"or clear."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12290 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12292
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditional Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12292
msgid "@nodedescription Text for a given format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12297
msgid ""
"Texinfo has an @code{@@if@var{format}} environment for each output format, "
"to allow conditional inclusion of text for a particular output format."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12298
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12303
msgid ""
"@code{@@ifinfo} begins segments of text that should be ignored when not "
"producing Info output, in particular in printed output.  The segment of text "
"appears only in the Info file and, for historical compatibility, the plain "
"text output."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12304
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifdocbook"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12305
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifhtml"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12306
#, no-wrap
msgid "iflatex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12307
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifplaintext"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12308
#, no-wrap
msgid "iftex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12309
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifxml"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12311
msgid "The environments for the other formats are analogous:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12313
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@ifdocbook @dots{} @@end ifdocbook"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12315
msgid "Text to appear only in the DocBook output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12316
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@ifhtml @dots{} @@end ifhtml"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12318
msgid "Text to appear only in the HTML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12319
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@iflatex @dots{} @@end iflatex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12321
msgid "Text to appear only in the @LaTeX{} output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12322
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@ifplaintext @dots{} @@end ifplaintext"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12324
msgid "Text to appear only in the plain text output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12325
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@iftex @dots{} @@end iftex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12327
msgid "Text to appear only in the printed manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12328
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@ifxml @dots{} @@end ifxml"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12330
msgid "Text to appear only in the XML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12336
msgid ""
"The @code{@@if@dots{}} and @code{@@end if@dots{}} commands must appear on "
"lines by themselves in your source file.  The newlines following the "
"commands are (more or less) treated as whitespace, so that the conditional "
"text is flowed normally into a surrounding paragraph."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12340
msgid ""
"The @code{@@if@dots{}} constructs are intended to conditionalize normal "
"Texinfo source; @pxref{Raw Formatter Commands}, for using underlying format "
"commands directly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12342
msgid "Here is an example showing all these conditionals:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12365
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@iftex\n"
"This text will appear only in the printed manual.\n"
"@@end iftex\n"
"@@ifinfo\n"
"However, this text will appear only in Info and plain text.\n"
"@@end ifinfo\n"
"@@ifhtml\n"
"And this text will only appear in HTML.\n"
"@@end ifhtml\n"
"@@iflatex\n"
"Moreover, this text will only appear in @@LaTeX@{@}.\n"
"@@end iflatex\n"
"@@ifplaintext\n"
"Whereas this text will only appear in plain text.\n"
"@@end ifplaintext\n"
"@@ifxml\n"
"Notwithstanding that this will only appear in XML@.\n"
"@@end ifxml\n"
"@@ifdocbook\n"
"Nevertheless, this will only appear in DocBook.\n"
"@@end ifdocbook\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12369
msgid "The preceding example produces the following line:"
msgstr ""

#. type: iftex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12372
msgid "This text will appear only in the printed manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifinfo
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12375
msgid "However, this text will appear only in Info and plain text."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifhtml
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12378
msgid "And this text will only appear in HTML."
msgstr ""

#. type: iflatex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12381
msgid "Moreover, this text will only appear in @LaTeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifplaintext
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12384
msgid "Whereas this text will only appear in plain text."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifxml
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12387
msgid "Notwithstanding that this will only appear in XML@."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifdocbook
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12390
msgid "Nevertheless, this will only appear in DocBook."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12395
msgid ""
"Notice that you only see one of the input lines, depending on which version "
"of the manual you are reading."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12396
#, no-wrap
msgid "errormsg"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12401
msgid ""
"In complex documents, you may want Texinfo to issue an error message in some "
"conditionals that should not ever be processed.  The "
"@code{@@errormsg@{@var{text}@}} command will do this; it takes one argument, "
"the text of the error message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12407
msgid ""
"We mention @code{@@errormsg@{@}} here even though it is not strictly related "
"to conditionals, since in practice it is most likely to be useful in that "
"context.  Technically, it can be used anywhere.  @xref{External Macro "
"Processors}, for a caveat regarding the line numbers which @code{@@errormsg} "
"emits in @TeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12409 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12411
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditional Not Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12411
msgid "@nodedescription Text for any format other than a given one."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12412
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifnotdocbook"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12413
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifnothtml"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12414
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifnotinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12415
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifnotlatex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12416
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifnotplaintext"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12417
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifnottex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12418
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifnotxml"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12422
msgid ""
"You can specify text to be included in any output format @emph{other} than a "
"given one with the @code{@@ifnot@dots{}} environments:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12431
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifnotdocbook @dots{} @@end ifnotdocbook\n"
"@@ifnothtml @dots{} @@end ifnothtml\n"
"@@ifnotinfo @dots{} @@end ifnotinfo\n"
"@@ifnotlatex @dots{} @@end ifnotlatex\n"
"@@ifnotplaintext @dots{} @@end ifnotplaintext\n"
"@@ifnottex @dots{} @@end ifnottex\n"
"@@ifnotxml @dots{} @@end ifnotxml\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12436
msgid ""
"The @code{@@ifnot@dots{}} command and the @code{@@end} command must appear "
"on lines by themselves in your actual source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12439
msgid ""
"If the output file is being made in the given format, the region is "
"@emph{ignored}.  Otherwise, it is included."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12444
msgid ""
"There is one exception (for historical compatibility): @code{@@ifnotinfo} "
"text is omitted for both Info and plain text output, not just Info.  To "
"specify text which appears only in Info and not in plain text, use "
"@code{@@ifnotplaintext}, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12451
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifinfo\n"
"@@ifnotplaintext\n"
"This will be in Info, but not plain text.\n"
"@@end ifnotplaintext\n"
"@@end ifinfo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12456
msgid ""
"The regions delimited by these commands are ordinary Texinfo source as with "
"@code{@@iftex}, not raw formatter source as with @code{@@tex} (@pxref{Raw "
"Formatter Commands})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12458 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12460
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raw Formatter Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12460
msgid "@nodedescription Using raw formatter commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12461
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raw formatter commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12463
#, no-wrap
msgid "@TeX{} commands, using ordinary"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12464
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ordinary @TeX{} commands, using"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12465
#, no-wrap
msgid "Commands using raw @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12466
#, no-wrap
msgid "Plain @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12476
msgid ""
"The @code{@@if@dots{}} conditionals just described must be used only with "
"normal Texinfo source.  For instance, most features of plain @TeX{} will not "
"work within @code{@@iftex}.  The purpose of @code{@@if@dots{}} is to provide "
"conditional processing for Texinfo source, not provide access to underlying "
"formatting features.  For that, Texinfo provides so-called @dfn{raw "
"formatter commands}.  They should only be used when truly required (most "
"documents do not need them)."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12478
#, no-wrap
msgid "Category codes, of plain @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12487
msgid ""
"The first raw formatter command is @code{@@tex}.  You can enter plain @TeX{} "
"completely, and use @samp{\\} in the @TeX{} commands, by delineating a "
"region with the @code{@@tex} and @code{@@end tex} commands.  All plain "
"@TeX{} commands and category codes are restored within a @code{@@tex} "
"region.  The sole exception is that the @code{@@} character still introduces "
"a command, so that @code{@@end tex} can be recognized.  Texinfo processors "
"will not output material in such a region unless @TeX{} output is being "
"produced."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12488
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\gdef @r{within @code{@@tex}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12489
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\globaldefs @r{within @code{@@tex}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12497
msgid ""
"In complex cases, you may wish to define new @TeX{} macros within "
"@code{@@tex}.  You must use @code{\\gdef} to do this, not @code{\\def}, "
"because @code{@@tex} regions are processed in a @TeX{} group.  If you need "
"to make several definitions, you may wish to set @code{\\globaldefs=1} (its "
"value will be restored to zero as usual when the group ends at @code{@@end "
"tex}, so it won't cause problems with the rest of the document)."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12498
#, no-wrap
msgid "Equation, displayed, in plain @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12499
#, no-wrap
msgid "Displayed equation, in plain @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12501
msgid "As an example, here is a displayed equation written in plain @TeX{}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12508
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@tex\n"
"$$ \\chi^2 = \\sum_@{i=1@}^N\n"
"         \\left (y_i - (a + b x_i)\n"
"         \\over \\sigma_i\\right)^2 $$\n"
"@@end tex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12514
msgid ""
"The output of this example will appear only in a printed manual.  If you are "
"reading this in a format not generated by @TeX{}, you will not see the "
"equation that appears in the printed manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: tex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12519
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$ \\chi^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^N\n"
"         \\left(y_i - (a + b x_i)\n"
"         \\over \\sigma_i\\right)^2 $$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12521
#, no-wrap
msgid "HTML, including raw"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12522 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:15874
#, no-wrap
msgid "html"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12523
#, no-wrap
msgid "DocBook, including raw"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12524 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:15870
#, no-wrap
msgid "docbook"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12525
#, no-wrap
msgid "@LaTeX{}, including raw"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12526
#, no-wrap
msgid "latex"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12527
#, no-wrap
msgid "XML, including raw"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12528 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:15879
#, no-wrap
msgid "xml"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12534
msgid ""
"Analogously, you can use @code{@@html @dots{} @@end html} for a region of "
"raw HTML, @code{@@docbook @dots{} @@end docbook} for a region of raw "
"DocBook, @code{@@latex @dots{} @@end latex} for a region of raw @LaTeX{}, "
"and @code{@@xml @dots{} @@end xml} for a region of raw XML@."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12536
msgid "The behavior of newlines in raw regions is unspecified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12545
msgid ""
"In all cases, in raw processing, @code{@@} retains the same meaning as in "
"the remainder of the document.  Thus, the Texinfo processors must recognize "
"and even execute, to some extent, the contents of the raw regions, "
"regardless of the final output format.  Therefore, specifying changes that "
"globally affect the document inside a raw region leads to unpredictable and "
"generally undesirable behavior.  For example, using the "
"@code{@@kbdinputstyle} command inside a raw region is undefined.  The remedy "
"is simple: don't do that."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12547
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inline Conditionals"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12549
msgid "@nodedescription Brace-delimited conditional text."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12549
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inline Conditionals: @code{@@inline}, @code{@@inlineifelse}, @code{@@inlineraw}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12550
#, no-wrap
msgid "inlinefmt"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12551
#, no-wrap
msgid "inlinefmtifelse"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12552
#, no-wrap
msgid "inlineraw"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12553
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inline conditionals"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12554
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditional commands, inline"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12555
#, no-wrap
msgid "Brace-delimited conditional text"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12556
#, no-wrap
msgid "Newlines, avoiding in conditionals"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12557
#, no-wrap
msgid "Whitespace, controlling in conditionals"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12561
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides a set of conditional commands with arguments given within "
"braces:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12563
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inlinefmt@{@var{format}, @var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12566
msgid ""
"Process the Texinfo @var{text} if @var{format} output is being generated."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12567
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inlinefmtifelse@{@var{format}, @var{then-text}, @var{else-text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12570
msgid ""
"Process the Texinfo @var{then-text} if @var{format} output is being "
"generated; otherwise, process @var{else-text}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12571
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inlineraw@{@var{format}, @var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12573
msgid "Similar, but for raw @var{text} (@pxref{Raw Formatter Commands})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12576
msgid "The supported @var{format} names are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12579
#, no-wrap
msgid "docbook  html  info  latex  plaintext  tex  xml\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12585
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inlinefmt@{html, @@emph@{HTML-only text@}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12589
msgid "is nearly equivalent to"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12594
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifhtml\n"
"@@emph@{HTML-only text@}\n"
"@@end ifhtml\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12599
msgid ""
"except that no whitespace is added, as happens in the latter (environment) "
"case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12603
msgid ""
"In these commands, whitespace is ignored after the comma separating the "
"arguments, as usual, but is @emph{not} ignored at the end of @var{text}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12609
msgid ""
"To insert a literal at sign, left brace, or right brace in one of the "
"arguments, you must use the alphabetic commands @code{@@atchar@{@}} "
"(@pxref{Inserting an Atsign}), and @code{@@lbracechar@{@}} or "
"@code{@@rbracechar@{@}} (@pxref{Inserting Braces}), or the parsing will "
"become confused."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12615
msgid ""
"With @code{@@inlinefmtifelse}, it is also necessary to use "
"@code{@@comma@{@}} to avoid mistaking a @samp{,} in the text for the "
"delimiter.  With @code{@@inlinefmt} and @code{@@inlineraw}, "
"@code{@@comma@{@}} is not required (though it's fine to use it), since these "
"commands always have exactly two arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12618
msgid ""
"For @TeX{}, the processed @var{text} cannot contain newline-delimited "
"commands.  Text to be ignored (i.e., for non-@TeX{}) can, though."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12621
msgid ""
"Two other @code{@@inline...} conditionals complement the @code{@@ifset} and "
"@code{@@ifclear} commands; see the next section."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12623
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@set @@clear @@value}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12625
msgid "@nodedescription Variable tests and substitutions."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12625
#, no-wrap
msgid "Flags: @code{@@set}, @code{@@clear}, conditionals, and @code{@@value}"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12631
msgid "set clear value"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12631
msgid ""
"You can direct the Texinfo formatting commands to format or ignore parts of "
"a Texinfo file with the @code{@@set}, @code{@@clear}, @code{@@ifset}, and "
"@code{@@ifclear} commands.  Here is a summary:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12633
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@set @var{flag} [@var{value}]"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12635
msgid "Set the variable @var{flag}, to the optional @var{value} if specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12636 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20079
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@clear @var{flag}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12638
msgid ""
"Undefine the variable @var{flag}, whether or not it was previously defined."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12639
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@ifset @var{flag}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12643
msgid ""
"If @var{flag} is set, text through the next @code{@@end ifset} command is "
"formatted.  If @var{flag} is clear, text through the following @code{@@end "
"ifset} command is ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12644
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inlineifset@{@var{flag}, @var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12646
msgid "Brace-delimited version of @code{@@ifset}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12647
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@ifclear @var{flag}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12651
msgid ""
"If @var{flag} is set, text through the next @code{@@end ifclear} command is "
"ignored.  If @var{flag} is clear, text through the following @code{@@end "
"ifclear} command is formatted."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12652
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inlineifclear@{@var{flag}, @var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12654
msgid "Brace-delimited version of @code{@@ifclear}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12658
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@set @@value}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12660
msgid "@nodedescription Expand a flag variable to a string."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12660
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@set} and @code{@@value}"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12663
msgid "set value"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12663
#, no-wrap
msgid "set"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12664
#, no-wrap
msgid "value"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12665
#, no-wrap
msgid "clear"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12669
msgid ""
"You use the @code{@@set} command to specify a value for a flag, which is "
"later expanded by the @code{@@value} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12677
msgid ""
"A @dfn{flag} (aka @dfn{variable}) name is an identifier starting with an "
"alphanumeric, @samp{-}, or @samp{_}.  Subsequent characters, if any, may not "
"be whitespace, @samp{@@}, braces, angle brackets, or any of @samp{~`^+|}; "
"other characters, such as @samp{%}, may work.  However, it is best to use "
"only letters and numerals in a flag name, not @samp{-} or @samp{_} or "
"others---they will work in some contexts, but not all, due to limitations in "
"@TeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12679
msgid "The value is the remainder of the input line, and can contain anything."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12686
msgid "Write the @code{@@set} command like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12689
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@set foo This is a string.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12693
msgid "This sets the value of the flag @code{foo} to ``This is a string.''."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12697
msgid ""
"The Texinfo processors then replace a @code{@@value@{@var{flag}@}} command "
"with the string to which @var{flag} is set.  Thus, when @code{foo} is set as "
"shown above, the Texinfo processors convert this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12701
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@value@{foo@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: exdent
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12701
#, no-wrap
msgid "@r{to this:}"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12703
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is a string.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12708
msgid ""
"You can write a @code{@@value} command within a paragraph; but you must "
"write a @code{@@set} command on a line of its own."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12710
msgid "If you write the @code{@@set} command like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12713
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@set foo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12717
msgid ""
"without specifying a string, the value of @code{foo} is the empty string."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12720
msgid ""
"If you clear a previously set flag with @code{@@clear @var{flag}}, a "
"subsequent @code{@@value@{flag@}} command will report an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12722
msgid "For example, if you set @code{foo} as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12725
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@set howmuch very, very, very\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12729 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12746
msgid "then the processors transform"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12733 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12750
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is a @@value@{howmuch@} wet day.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: exdent
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12733 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12750
#, no-wrap
msgid "@r{into}"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12735
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is a very, very, very wet day.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12739
msgid "If you write"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12742
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@clear howmuch\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12752
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is a @{No value for \"howmuch\"@} wet day.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12757
msgid ""
"@code{@@value} cannot be reliably used as the argument to an accent command "
"(@pxref{Inserting Accents}).  For example, this fails:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12762
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@set myletter a\n"
"@@'@@value@{myletter@}    @c fails!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12765
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@ifset @@ifclear}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12767
msgid "@nodedescription Format a region if a flag is set."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12767
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@ifset} and @code{@@ifclear}"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12770
msgid "ifset ifclear"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12770
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifset"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12777
msgid ""
"When a @var{flag} is set, the Texinfo formatting commands format text "
"between subsequent pairs of @code{@@ifset @var{flag}} and @code{@@end ifset} "
"commands.  When the @var{flag} is cleared, the Texinfo formatting commands "
"do @emph{not} format the text.  @code{@@ifclear} operates analogously."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12780
msgid ""
"Write the conditionally formatted text between @code{@@ifset @var{flag}} and "
"@code{@@end ifset} commands, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12786
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifset @var{flag}\n"
"@var{conditional-text}\n"
"@@end ifset\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12791
msgid ""
"For example, you can create one document that has two variants, such as a "
"manual for a `large' and `small' model:"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12792
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shrubbery"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12796
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can use this machine to dig up shrubs\n"
"without hurting them.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12798
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@set large\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12802
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifset large\n"
"It can also dig up fully grown trees.\n"
"@@end ifset\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12804
#, no-wrap
msgid "Remember to replant promptly @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12810
msgid ""
"In the example, the formatting commands will format the text between "
"@code{@@ifset large} and @code{@@end ifset} because the @code{large} flag is "
"set."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12814
msgid ""
"When @var{flag} is cleared, the Texinfo formatting commands do @emph{not} "
"format the text between @code{@@ifset @var{flag}} and @code{@@end ifset}; "
"that text is ignored and does not appear in the output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12823
msgid ""
"For example, if you clear the flag of the preceding example by writing an "
"@code{@@clear large} command after the @code{@@set large} command (but "
"before the conditional text), then the Texinfo formatting commands ignore "
"the text between the @code{@@ifset large} and @code{@@end ifset} commands.  "
"In the formatted output, that text does not appear; you see only the lines "
"that say, ``You can use this machine to dig up shrubs without hurting them.  "
"Remember to replant promptly @dots{}''."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12824
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifclear"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12832
msgid ""
"If a flag is cleared with a @code{@@clear @var{flag}} command, then the "
"formatting commands format text between subsequent pairs of @code{@@ifclear} "
"and @code{@@end ifclear} commands.  But if the flag is set with @code{@@set "
"@var{flag}}, then the formatting commands do @emph{not} format text between "
"an @code{@@ifclear} and an @code{@@end ifclear} command; rather, they ignore "
"that text.  An @code{@@ifclear} command looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12835
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@ifclear @var{flag}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12838
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@inlineifset @@inlineifclear}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12840
msgid "@nodedescription Brace-delimited flag conditionals."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12840
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@inlineifset} and @code{@@inlineifclear}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12842
#, no-wrap
msgid "inlineifset"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12843
#, no-wrap
msgid "inlineifclear"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12844
#, no-wrap
msgid "Flag conditionals, brace-delimited"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12845
#, no-wrap
msgid "Brace-delimited flag conditionals"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12852
msgid ""
"@code{@@inlineifset} and @code{@@inlineifclear} provide brace-delimited "
"alternatives to the @code{@@ifset} and @code{@@ifclear} forms, similar to "
"the other @code{@@inline...} Commands (@pxref{Inline Conditionals}).  The "
"same caveats about argument parsing given there apply here too."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12854 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20609
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inlineifset@{@var{var}, @var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12856
msgid "Process the Texinfo @var{text} if the flag @var{var} is defined."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12857 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:20608
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@inlineifclear@{@var{var}, @var{text}@}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12859
msgid "Process the Texinfo @var{text} if the flag @var{var} is not defined."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12864
msgid ""
"Except for the syntax, their general behavior and purposes is the same as "
"with @code{@@ifset} and @code{@@ifclear}, described in the previous section."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12866 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12868
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@value} Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12868
msgid "@nodedescription An easy way to update edition information."
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12871
msgid "value Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12876
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{@@value} command to minimize the number of places you "
"need to change when you record an update to a manual.  @xref{GNU Sample "
"Texts}, for the full text of an example of using this to work with Automake "
"distributions."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12878
msgid "This example is adapted from @ref{Top,,, make, The GNU Make Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12882
msgid "Set the flags:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12889
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@set EDITION 0.35 Beta\n"
"@@set VERSION 3.63 Beta\n"
"@@set UPDATED 14 August 1992\n"
"@@set UPDATE-MONTH August 1992\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12894
msgid "Write text for the @code{@@copying} section (@pxref{@code{@@copying}}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12902
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@copying\n"
"This is Edition @@value@{EDITION@},\n"
"last updated @@value@{UPDATED@},\n"
"of @@cite@{The GNU Make Manual@},\n"
"for @@code@{make@}, version @@value@{VERSION@}.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12904
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Copyright @dots{}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12907
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Permission is granted @dots{}\n"
"@@end copying\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12912
msgid "Write text for the title page, for people reading the printed manual:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12921
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@documentinfo\n"
"@@title GNU Make\n"
"@@subtitle A Program for Directing Recompilation\n"
"@@subtitle Edition @@value@{EDITION@}, @dots{}\n"
"@@subtitle @@value@{UPDATE-MONTH@}\n"
"@@end documentinfo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12927
msgid ""
"(On a printed cover, a date listing the month and the year looks less fussy "
"than a date listing the day as well as the month and year.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12930
msgid "Write text for the Top node, for people reading the Info file:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12936
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifnottex\n"
"@@node Top\n"
"@@top Make\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12942
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is Edition @@value@{EDITION@},\n"
"last updated @@value@{UPDATED@},\n"
"of @@cite@{The GNU Make Manual@},\n"
"for @@code@{make@}, version @@value@{VERSION@}.\n"
"@@end ifnottex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12947
msgid ""
"After you format the manual, the @code{@@value} constructs have been "
"expanded, so the output contains text like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12952
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is Edition 0.35 Beta, last updated 14 August 1992,\n"
"of `The GNU Make Manual', for `make', Version 3.63 Beta.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12958
msgid ""
"When you update the manual, you change only the values of the flags; you do "
"not need to edit the three sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12960
#, no-wrap
msgid "Testing for Texinfo Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12962
msgid "@nodedescription Testing if a Texinfo command is available."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12962
#, no-wrap
msgid "Testing for Texinfo Commands: @code{@@ifcommanddefined}, @code{@@ifcommandnotdefined}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12964
#, no-wrap
msgid "Testing for Texinfo commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12965
#, no-wrap
msgid "Checking for Texinfo commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12966
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo commands, testing for"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12967
#, no-wrap
msgid "Commands, testing for Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12968
#, no-wrap
msgid "Versions of Texinfo, adapting to"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12969
#, no-wrap
msgid "Features of Texinfo, adapting to"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12970
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifcommanddefined"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12971
#, no-wrap
msgid "ifcommandnotdefined"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12978
msgid ""
"Occasionally, you may want to arrange for your manual to test if a given "
"Texinfo command is available and (presumably) do some sort of fallback "
"formatting if not.  There are conditionals @code{@@ifcommanddefined} and "
"@code{@@ifcommandnotdefined} to do this.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12983
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifcommanddefined node\n"
"Good, @@samp@{@@@@node@} is defined.\n"
"@@end ifcommanddefined\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12987
msgid "will output the expected `Good, @samp{@@node} is defined.'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12995
msgid ""
"This conditional will also consider any new commands defined by the document "
"via @code{@@macro}, @code{@@alias}, @code{@@definfoenclose}, and "
"@code{@@def@r{(}code@r{)}index} (@pxref{Defining New Texinfo Commands}) to "
"be true.  Caveat: the @TeX{} implementation reports internal @TeX{} "
"commands, in addition to all the Texinfo commands, as being ``defined''; the "
"@code{texi2any} implementation is reliable in this regard, however."
msgstr ""

#. type: pindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:12996
#, no-wrap
msgid "@file{NEWS} file for Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13001
msgid ""
"You can check the @file{NEWS} file in the Texinfo source distribution and "
"linked from the Texinfo home page (@url{https://www.gnu.org/software/"
"texinfo}) to see when a particular command was added."
msgstr ""

#. type: vindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13002
#, no-wrap
msgid "txicommandconditionals"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13008
msgid ""
"These command-checking conditionals themselves were added in Texinfo@tie{}"
"5.0, released in 2013---decades after Texinfo's inception.  In order to test "
"if they themselves are available, the predefined flag "
"@code{txicommandconditionals} can be tested, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13015
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifset txicommandconditionals\n"
"@@ifcommandnotdefined foobarnode\n"
"(Good, @@samp@{@@@@foobarnode@} is not defined.)\n"
"@@end ifcommandnotdefined\n"
"@@end ifset\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13020
msgid ""
"Since flags (see the previous section) were added early in the existence of "
"Texinfo, there is no problem with assuming they are available."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13027
msgid ""
"We recommend avoiding these tests whenever possible---which is usually the "
"case.  For many software packages, it is reasonable for all developers to "
"have a given version of Texinfo (or newer) installed, and thus no reason to "
"worry about older versions.  (It is straightforward for anyone to download "
"and install the Texinfo source; it does not have any problematic "
"dependencies.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13034
msgid ""
"The issue of Texinfo versions does not generally arise for end users.  With "
"properly distributed packages, users need not process the Texinfo manual "
"simply to build and install the package; they can use preformatted Info (or "
"other) output files.  This is desirable in general, to avoid unnecessary "
"dependencies between packages (@pxref{Releases,,, standards, GNU Coding "
"Standards})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13036 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13038
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditional Nesting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13038
msgid "@nodedescription Using conditionals inside conditionals."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13039
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditionals, nested"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13040
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nesting conditionals"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13045
msgid ""
"Texinfo requires that for a failing conditional, the ignored text must be "
"properly nested with respect to that failing conditional.  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13055
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifset somevar\n"
"@@ifset anothervar\n"
"Both somevar and anothervar are set.\n"
"@@end ifset\n"
"@@ifclear anothervar\n"
"Somevar is set, anothervar is not.\n"
"@@end ifclear\n"
"@@end ifset\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13058
msgid "If @samp{somevar} is not set, the whole block is skipped."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13062
msgid ""
"To allow the processors to reliably determine which commands to consider for "
"nesting purposes, all conditional commands must be on lines by themselves, "
"with no text (even spaces) before or after."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13079
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifset a\n"
"@@ifset b\n"
"@@ifclear ok  - ok, ignored\n"
"@@end junky   - ok, ignored\n"
"@@end ifset\n"
"@@c WRONG - missing @@end ifset.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13085 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13087
#, no-wrap
msgid "Defining New Texinfo Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13087
msgid "@nodedescription User-defined macros and aliases."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13089
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13090
#, no-wrap
msgid "Defining new Texinfo commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13091
#, no-wrap
msgid "New Texinfo commands, defining"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13092
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo commands, defining new"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13093
#, no-wrap
msgid "User-defined Texinfo commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13097
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides several ways to define new commands (in all cases, it's not "
"recommended to try redefining existing commands):"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13104
msgid ""
"A Texinfo @dfn{macro} allows you to define a new Texinfo command as any "
"sequence of text and/or existing commands (including other macros).  The "
"macro can have any number of @dfn{parameters}---text you supply each time "
"you use the macro."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13108
msgid ""
"Incidentally, these macros have nothing to do with the @code{@@defmac} "
"command, which is for documenting macros in the subject area of the manual "
"(@pxref{Def Cmd Template})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13112
msgid ""
"@samp{@@linemacro} allows you to define a macro whose arguments extend to "
"the end of the line and are separated by spaces."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13115
msgid "@samp{@@alias} defines a new name for an existing command."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13120
msgid ""
"@samp{@@definfoenclose} allows you to define new commands with customized "
"output for some non-printed output formats.  This command is deprecated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13126
msgid ""
"Most generally of all (not just for defining new commands), it is possible "
"to invoke any external macro processor and have Texinfo recognize so-called "
"@code{#line} directives for error reporting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13130
msgid ""
"If you want to do simple text substitution, @code{@@set} and @code{@@value} "
"is the simplest approach (@pxref{@code{@@set @@clear @@value}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13132 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13134
#, no-wrap
msgid "Defining Macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13134
msgid "@nodedescription Defining and undefining new commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13135
#, no-wrap
msgid "Defining macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13136
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro definitions, Texinfo"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13138
#, no-wrap
msgid "macro"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13140
msgid ""
"You use the Texinfo @code{@@macro} command to define a macro, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13145
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro @var{macroname}@{@var{param1}, @var{param2}, @dots{}@}\n"
"@var{text} @dots{} \\@var{param1}\\ @dots{}\n"
"@@end macro\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13150
msgid ""
"The @dfn{parameters} @var{param1}, @var{param2}, @dots{} correspond to "
"arguments supplied when the macro is subsequently used in the document "
"(described in the next section)."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13151
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro names, valid characters in"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13152
#, no-wrap
msgid "Names of macros, valid characters of"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13161
msgid ""
"In principle, @var{macroname} should consist of alphanumerics, and (except "
"as the first character) @samp{-}.  The @samp{_} character is excluded so "
"that macros can be called inside @code{@@math} without a following space "
"(@pxref{Inserting Math}).  However, for a macro to work consistently with "
"@TeX{}, @var{macroname} must consist entirely of letters: no digits, "
"hyphens, or other special characters.  So, we recommend using only letters.  "
"Texinfo commands should not be redefined as macros."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13165
msgid ""
"If a macro needs no parameters, you can define it either with an empty list "
"(@samp{@@macro foo @{@}}) or with no braces at all (@samp{@@macro foo})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13166
#, no-wrap
msgid "Body of a macro"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13171
msgid ""
"The definition or @dfn{body} of the macro can contain most Texinfo commands, "
"including macro invocations.  However, a macro definition that defines "
"another macro does not work in @TeX{} due to limitations in the design of "
"@code{@@macro}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13172
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parameters to macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13177
msgid ""
"In the macro body, instances of a parameter name surrounded by backslashes, "
"as in @samp{\\@var{param1}\\} in the example above, are replaced by the "
"corresponding argument from the macro invocation.  You can use parameter "
"names any number of times in the body, including zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13178
#, no-wrap
msgid "Backslash in macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13181
msgid ""
"To get a single @samp{\\} in the macro expansion, use @samp{\\\\}.  Any "
"other use of @samp{\\} in the body yields a warning."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13182
#, no-wrap
msgid "Spaces in macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13183
#, no-wrap
msgid "Whitespace in macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13188
msgid ""
"The newline characters after the @code{@@macro} line and before the "
"@code{@@end macro} line are ignored, that is, not included in the macro "
"body.  All other whitespace is treated according to the usual Texinfo rules."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13189
#, no-wrap
msgid "Recursive macro invocations"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13190
#, no-wrap
msgid "rmacro"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13193
msgid ""
"To allow a macro to be used recursively, that is, in an argument to a call "
"to itself, you must define it with @samp{@@rmacro}, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13200
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@rmacro rmac @{arg@}\n"
"a\\arg\\b\n"
"@@end rmacro\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"@@rmac@{1@@rmac@{text@}2@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13204
msgid ""
"This produces the output `a1atextb2b'.  With @samp{@@macro} instead of "
"@samp{@@rmacro}, an error message is given."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13205
#, no-wrap
msgid "unmacro"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13206
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macros, undefining"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13207
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undefining macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13211
msgid ""
"You can undefine a macro @var{foo} with @code{@@unmacro @var{foo}}.  It is "
"not an error to undefine a macro that is already undefined.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13214
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@unmacro foo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13217 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13219
#, no-wrap
msgid "Invoking Macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13219
msgid "@nodedescription Using a macro, once you've defined it."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13221
#, no-wrap
msgid "Invoking macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13222
#, no-wrap
msgid "Expanding macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13223
#, no-wrap
msgid "Running macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13224
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro invocation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13228
msgid ""
"After a macro is defined (see the previous section), you can @dfn{invoke} "
"(use) it in your document like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13231
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@@var{macroname} @{@var{arg1}, @var{arg2}, @dots{}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13236
msgid ""
"and the result will be more or less as if you typed the body of "
"@var{macroname} at that spot.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13242
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro foo @{p, q@}\n"
"Together: \\p\\ & \\q\\.\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@foo@{a, b@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13249
#, no-wrap
msgid "Together: a & b.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13251
#, no-wrap
msgid "Backslash, and macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13257
msgid ""
"Thus, the arguments and parameters are separated by commas and delimited by "
"braces; any whitespace after (but not before) a comma is ignored.  The "
"braces are required in the invocation even when the macro takes no "
"arguments, consistent with other Texinfo commands.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13263
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro argless @{@}\n"
"No arguments here.\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@argless@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13270
#, no-wrap
msgid "No arguments here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13272
#, no-wrap
msgid "Comma, in macro arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13279
msgid ""
"Passing macro arguments containing commas requires care, since commas also "
"separate the arguments.  To include a comma character in an argument, the "
"most reliable method is to use the @code{@@comma@{@}} command.  Another "
"method is to surround the argument with @samp{@@asis@{@dots{}@}}.  For "
"@code{texi2any}, you can also prepend a backslash character, as in "
"@samp{\\,}, but this does not work with @TeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13280
#, no-wrap
msgid "Automatic quoting of commas for some macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13281
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quoting, automatic for some macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13284
msgid ""
"It's not always necessary to worry about commas.  To facilitate use of "
"macros, two rules for @dfn{automatic quoting} are implemented:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13286
#, no-wrap
msgid "If a macro takes only one argument, all commas in its invocation"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13288
msgid "are quoted by default.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13294
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro TRYME@{text@}\n"
"@@strong@{TRYME: \\text\\@}\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13296
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@TRYME@{A nice feature, though it can be dangerous.@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13301
msgid "will produce the following output"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13304
#, no-wrap
msgid "@strong{TRYME: A nice feature, though it can be dangerous.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13309
msgid ""
"And indeed, it can.  Namely, there is no control on the number of arguments "
"passed to one-argument macros, so be careful when you invoke them."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13310
#, no-wrap
msgid "If a macro invocation includes another command (including a"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13313
msgid ""
"recursive invocation of itself), any commas in the nested command "
"invocation(s) are quoted by default.  For example, in"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13316
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@say@{@@strong@{Yes, I do@}, person one@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13320
msgid ""
"the comma after @samp{Yes} is implicitly quoted.  Here's another example, "
"with a recursive macro:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13326
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@rmacro cat@{a,b@}\n"
"\\a\\\\b\\\n"
"@@end rmacro\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13328
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@cat@{@@cat@{foo, bar@}, baz@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13333
msgid "will produce the string @samp{foobarbaz}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13334
#, no-wrap
msgid "Otherwise, a comma should be explicitly quoted, as above, for it"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13336
msgid "to be treated as a part of an argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13338
#, no-wrap
msgid "Backslash, in macro arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13339
#, no-wrap
msgid "Braces, in macro arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13342
msgid ""
"The backslash itself can be quoted in macro arguments with another "
"backslash.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13345
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@@var{macname} @{\\\\bleh@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13349
msgid "will pass the argument @samp{\\bleh} to @var{macname}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13354
msgid ""
"@command{texi2any} also recognizes @samp{\\@{} and @samp{\\@}} sequences for "
"curly braces, but these are not recognized by the implementation in @TeX{}.  "
"There should, however, rarely be a need for these, as they are only needed "
"when a macro argument contains unbalanced braces."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13358
msgid ""
"If a macro is defined to take exactly one argument, it can be invoked "
"without any braces, taking all of the line after the macro name as the "
"argument.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13364
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro bar @{p@}\n"
"Twice: \\p\\ & \\p\\.\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@bar aah\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13371
#, no-wrap
msgid "Twice: aah & aah.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13376
msgid ""
"In these arguments, there is no escaping of special characters, so each "
"@samp{\\} stands for itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13380
msgid ""
"If a macro is defined to take more than one argument, but is called with "
"only one (in braces), the remaining arguments are set to the empty string, "
"and no error is given.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13386
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro addtwo @{p, q@}\n"
"Both: \\p\\\\q\\.\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@addtwo@{a@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13390
msgid "produces simply:"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13393
#, no-wrap
msgid "Both: a.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13396
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro Details"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13398
msgid "@nodedescription Limitations of Texinfo macros."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13398
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro Details and Caveats"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13399
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro details"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13400
#, no-wrap
msgid "Details of macro usage"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13401
#, no-wrap
msgid "Caveats for macro usage"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13403
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro expansion, contexts for"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13404
#, no-wrap
msgid "Expansion of macros, contexts for"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13406
msgid "By design, macro expansion should not happen in the following contexts:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13408
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@macro} and @code{@@unmacro} lines;"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13410
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@if...} lines, including @code{@@ifset} and similar;"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13412
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@set}, @code{@@clear}, @code{@@value};"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13414
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@clickstyle} lines;"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13416
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@end} lines when there is no macro expansion in the block"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13418
msgid "command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13423
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, @TeX{} may do some expansion in these situations, possibly "
"yielding errors."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13432
msgid ""
"Also, quite a few macro-related constructs cause problems with @TeX{}; some "
"of the caveats are listed below.  Thus, if you get macro-related errors when "
"producing the printed version of a manual, you might try expanding the "
"macros with @command{texi2any} by invoking @command{texi2dvi} with the "
"@samp{-E} option (@pxref{Format with @command{texi2dvi}}).  Or, more "
"reliably, eschew Texinfo macros altogether and use a language designed for "
"macro processing, such as M4 (@pxref{External Macro Processors})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13436
msgid "As mentioned earlier, macro names must consist entirely of letters."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13442
msgid ""
"It is not advisable to redefine any @TeX{} primitive, plain, or Texinfo "
"command name as a macro. Unfortunately, this is a large and open-ended set "
"of names, and the possible resulting errors are unpredictable."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13445
msgid "Arguments to macros taking more than one argument cannot cross lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13451
msgid ""
"Macros containing a command which must be on a line by itself, such as a "
"conditional, cannot be invoked in the middle of a line.  Similarly, macros "
"containing line-oriented commands or text, such as @code{@@example} "
"environments, may behave unpredictably in @TeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13456
msgid ""
"Texinfo commands in the expansion of a macro in the text of an index entry "
"may end up being typeset as literal text (including an ``@@'' sign), instead "
"of being interpreted with their intended meaning."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13459
msgid "White space is ignored at the beginnings of lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13463
msgid ""
"Macros can't be reliably used in the argument to accent commands "
"(@pxref{Inserting Accents})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13466
msgid "Macros should not be used in node names.  @xref{Node Names}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13470
msgid ""
"The backslash escape for commas in macro arguments does not work; "
"@code{@@comma@{@}} must be used."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13478
msgid ""
"Ending a macro body with @samp{@@c} may cause text following the macro "
"invocation to be ignored as a comment in @command{texi2any}.  This is not "
"the case when processing with @TeX{}.  This was often done to ``comment "
"out'' an unwanted newline at the end of a macro body, but this is not "
"necessary any more, as the final newline before @samp{@@end macro} is not "
"included in the macro body anyway."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13485
msgid ""
"In general, you can't arbitrarily substitute a macro (or @code{@@value}) "
"call for Texinfo command arguments, even when the text is the same.  Texinfo "
"is not M4 (or even plain @TeX{}).  It might work with some commands, it "
"fails with others.  Best not to do it at all.  For instance, this fails:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13491
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro offmacro\n"
"off\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@headings @@offmacro\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13497
msgid ""
"This looks equivalent to @code{@@headings off}, but for @TeX{}nical reasons, "
"it fails with a mysterious error message (namely, @samp{Paragraph ended "
"before @@headings was complete})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13501
msgid ""
"Expanding macros in a math context may lead to poor spacing in @TeX{} "
"output.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13508
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@iftex\n"
"@@macro atan\n"
"\\\\mathop@{\\\\rm atan@}\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@end iftex\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13510
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@math@{@@atan@{@}(x)@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13515
msgid ""
"The braces following @samp{@@atan} are treated by @TeX{} as a subformula, "
"leading to extra space before the opening parenthesis.  You can work around "
"this by defining a raw @TeX{} macro instead:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13520
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@tex\n"
"\\gdef\\atan#1@{\\mathop@{\\rm atan@}@}\n"
"@@end tex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13524
msgid ""
"The @samp{#1} in this definition absorbs the braces, so that the spacing is "
"as expected for the @code{\\mathop} object."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13529
msgid ""
"If you have problems using conditionals within a macro, an alternative is to "
"use separate macro definitions inside conditional blocks.  For example, "
"instead of"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13540
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro Mac\n"
"@@iftex\n"
"text for TeX output\n"
"@@end iftex\n"
"@@ifnottex\n"
"text for not TeX output\n"
"@@end ifnottex\n"
"@@end macro\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13545
msgid "you can do the following instead:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13553
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@iftex\n"
"@@macro Mac\n"
"text for TeX output\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@end iftex\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13559
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifnottex\n"
"@@macro Mac\n"
"text for not TeX output\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@end ifnottex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13567
msgid ""
"Macros cannot define macros in the natural way.  To do this, you must use "
"conditionals and raw @TeX{}.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13580
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@ifnottex\n"
"@@macro ctor @{name, arg@}\n"
"@@macro \\name\\\n"
"something involving \\arg\\ somehow\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@end ifnottex\n"
"@@tex\n"
"\\gdef\\ctor#1@{\\ctorx#1,@}\n"
"\\gdef\\ctorx#1,#2,@{\\def#1@{something involving #2 somehow@}@}\n"
"@@end tex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13584
msgid "The following limitations are by design:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13593
msgid ""
"If you want to pass an argument with the Texinfo command @code{@@,} (to "
"produce a cedilla, see @ref{Inserting Accents}), you have to use "
"@code{@@value} or another workaround.  Otherwise, the comma may be taken as "
"separating the arguments.  With @command{texi2any}, the comma can be escaped "
"by a backslash.  With @TeX{} another workaround need to be used, therefore "
"we recommend using such a workaround."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro mactwo@{argfirst, argsecond@}\n"
"\\argfirst\\+\\argsecond\\.\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@set fc Fran@@,cois\n"
"@@mactwo@{@@value@{fc@},@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: display
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13609
#, no-wrap
msgid "Fran@,cois+.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13620
msgid ""
"@code{@@verbatim} and macros do not mix; for instance, you can't start a "
"verbatim block inside a macro and end it outside "
"(@pxref{@code{@@verbatim}}).  Starting any environment inside a macro and "
"ending it outside may or may not work, for that matter."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13627
msgid ""
"Macros that completely define macros are ok, but it's not possible to have "
"incompletely nested macro definitions.  That is, @code{@@macro} and "
"@code{@@end macro} (likewise for @code{@@rmacro}) must be correctly paired.  "
"For example, you cannot start a macro definition within a macro, and then "
"end that nested definition outside the macro."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13634
msgid ""
"In the @command{makeinfo} implementation before Texinfo 5.0, ends of lines "
"from expansion of a @code{@@macro} definition did not end an @@-command line-"
"delimited argument (@code{@@chapter}, @code{@@center}, etc.).  This is no "
"longer the case.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13641
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@macro twolines@{@}\n"
"aaa\n"
"bbb\n"
"@@end macro\n"
"@@center @@twolines@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13644
msgid "In the current @code{texi2any}, this is equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13648
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@center aaa\n"
"bbb\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13653
msgid ""
"with just @samp{aaa} as the argument to @code{@@center}.  In the earlier "
"implementation, it would have been parsed as this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13656
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@center aaa bbb\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13659
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@alias}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13661
msgid "@nodedescription Command aliases."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13661 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:19983
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@alias @var{new}=@var{existing}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13664 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13666
#, no-wrap
msgid "alias"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13664
#, no-wrap
msgid "Aliases, command"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13665
#, no-wrap
msgid "Command aliases"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13672
msgid ""
"The @samp{@@alias} command defines a new command to be just like an existing "
"one.  This is useful for defining additional markup names, thus preserving "
"additional semantic information in the input even though the output result "
"may be the same."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13677
msgid ""
"Write the @samp{@@alias} command on a line by itself, followed by the new "
"command name, an equals sign, and the existing command name.  Whitespace "
"around the equals sign is optional and ignored if present.  Thus:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13680
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@alias @var{new} = @var{existing}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13687
msgid ""
"For example, if your document contains citations for both books and some "
"other media (movies, for example), you might like to define a macro "
"@code{@@moviecite@{@}} that does the same thing as an ordinary "
"@code{@@cite@{@}} but conveys the extra semantic information as well.  You'd "
"do this as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13690
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@alias moviecite = cite\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13695
msgid ""
"Macros do not always have the same effect as aliases, due to vagaries of "
"argument parsing.  Also, aliases are much simpler to define than macros.  So "
"the command is not redundant."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13698
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, it's not possible to alias Texinfo environments; for example, "
"@code{@@alias lang=example} is an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13700
msgid "Aliases must not be recursive, directly or indirectly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13705
msgid ""
"It is not advisable to redefine any @TeX{} primitive, plain @TeX{}, or "
"Texinfo command name as an alias.  Unfortunately this is a very large set of "
"names, and the possible resulting errors from @TeX{} are unpredictable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13710
msgid ""
"Alias identifiers should be the same as for macro names, that is "
"alphanumerics and (except as the first character) @samp{-}.  However, with "
"@TeX{}, letters only should be used.  So, we recommend using only letters."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13712 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13714
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line Macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13714
msgid "@nodedescription Macros using whole line for arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13716
#, no-wrap
msgid "linemacro"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13717
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line macros"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13718
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macros taking whole line as an argument"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13722
msgid ""
"You can also define a macro using the @samp{@@linemacro} command.  The "
"syntax for line macro definitions is similar to that for @code{@@macro}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13728
msgid ""
"However, the syntax for using a macro defined this way is different.  The "
"macro call uses the rest of the line, with arguments separated by spaces.  "
"The line macro facility is mainly intended to allow you to define commands "
"that operate similarly to the builtin commands for providing definitions "
"(@pxref{Definition Commands})."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13735
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@linemacro defbuiltin @{name, args@}\n"
"@@defline @{Builtin@} \\name\\ \\args\\\n"
"@@end linemacro\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13738
msgid "The above macro may be used as"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13744
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defblock\n"
"@@defbuiltin foo (bar)\n"
"Explanation\n"
"@@end defblock\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13747
msgid "This produces the following result:"
msgstr ""

#. type: defline
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13749
#, no-wrap
msgid "{Builtin} \\name\\ \\args\\"
msgstr ""

#. type: defblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13755
msgid "@defbuiltin foo (bar)  Explanation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13760
msgid ""
"Here the first argument is @samp{foo}, and is substituted where "
"@samp{\\name\\} appears in the macro body.  Likewise, @samp{(bar)} is "
"substituted for @samp{\\args\\}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13767
msgid ""
"You can include spaces in a macro argument by surrounding the argument by "
"braces.  Any pair of braces enclosing an argument is removed before "
"substitution.  Any empty arguments (including the last one)  must be given "
"as @samp{@{@}}.  Additionally, non-initial spaces may appear in the final "
"argument in the argument list without surrounding braces (as in the example "
"below)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13774
msgid ""
"If an argument line for a line macro ends in a @samp{@@} character, then "
"this character together with the following newline are included in a macro "
"argument, and the following line in the input file is also used to get the "
"arguments for the macro.  Note that any substitution of the "
"@samp{@@@var{<newline>}} sequence must be in a valid context, such as a "
"definition line (@pxref{Def Cmd Continuation Lines})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13776
msgid "Another example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13782
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@linemacro deffunc @{type, name, args@}\n"
"@@findex \\name\\\n"
"@@deftypeline @{Func@} @{\\type\\@} \\name\\ \\args\\\n"
"@@end linemacro\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13788
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@defblock\n"
"@@deffunc @{long int@} F (int @@var@{one@}, int @@var@{two@}, @@\n"
"int @@var@{three@}, int @@var@{four@}, int @@var@{five@})\n"
"Explanation\n"
"@@end defblock\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13794
msgid ""
"Note how braces are needed around @samp{\\type\\} in the macro body in case "
"the @code{type} argument contains a space. This example produces the output "
"below and enters an index entry for @code{F}."
msgstr ""

#. type: deftypeline
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13797
#, no-wrap
msgid "{Func} {\\type\\} \\name\\ \\args\\"
msgstr ""

#. type: defblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13804
msgid ""
"@deffunc {long int} F (int @var{one}, int @var{two}, @ int @var{three}, int "
"@var{four}, int @var{five})  Explanation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13810
msgid ""
"Line macros produce a whole number of lines of output in their expansion.  "
"There may be better compatibility between the Texinfo processors "
"(@command{texi2any} and @TeX{} with @file{texinfo.tex}) for macros defined "
"with @code{@@linemacro} than for those defined with @code{@@macro}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13812
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@definfoenclose}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13814
msgid "@nodedescription Customized highlighting."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13814
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@definfoenclose}: Customized Highlighting"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13817 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13818
#, no-wrap
msgid "definfoenclose"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13817
#, no-wrap
msgid "Highlighting, customized"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13824
msgid ""
"An @code{@@definfoenclose} command may be used to define a highlighting "
"command for online output formats.  A command defined using "
"@code{@@definfoenclose} marks text by enclosing it in strings that precede "
"and follow the text."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13828
msgid ""
"In practice, there is little use for this command, and we do not recommend "
"you use it.  Support for @code{@@definfoenclose} may be removed in future "
"releases of Texinfo."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13835
msgid ""
"Write a @code{@@definfoenclose} command at the beginning of a line followed "
"by three comma-separated arguments.  The first argument to "
"@code{@@definfoenclose} is the @@-command name (without the @code{@@}); the "
"second argument is the start delimiter string; and the third argument is the "
"end delimiter string.  The latter two arguments enclose the highlighted text "
"in the output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13841
msgid ""
"A delimiter string may contain spaces.  Neither the start nor end delimiter "
"is required.  If you do not want a start delimiter but do want an end "
"delimiter, you must follow the command name with two commas in a row; "
"otherwise, the end delimiter string you intended will naturally be "
"(mis)interpreted as the start delimiter string."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13844
msgid ""
"An enclosure command defined this way takes one argument in braces, since it "
"is intended for new markup commands (@pxref{Marking Text})."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13845
#, no-wrap
msgid "phoo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13847
msgid "For example, you can write:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13850
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@definfoenclose phoo,//,\\\\\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13857
msgid ""
"near the beginning of a Texinfo file to define @code{@@phoo} as an Info and "
"HTML command that inserts `//' before and `\\\\' after the argument to "
"@code{@@phoo}.  You can then write @code{@@phoo@{bar@}} wherever you want `//"
"bar\\\\' highlighted in Info and HTML."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13859
msgid "For @TeX{} formatting, you could write"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13864
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@iftex\n"
"@@alias phoo = i\n"
"@@end iftex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13869
msgid ""
"to define @code{@@phoo} as a command that causes @TeX{} to typeset the "
"argument to @code{@@phoo} in italics."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13878
msgid ""
"Each definition applies to its own formatter: one for @TeX{}, the other for "
"online formats.  The @TeX{} definitions need to be in @samp{@@iftex}.  "
"@code{@@definfoenclose} command need not be within @samp{@@ifinfo} unless "
"you want to use different definitions for different online output formats.  "
"@code{@@definfoenclose} defined commands have no effect in DocBook and "
"@LaTeX{} output, their argument is output as-is.  An @code{@@alias} could "
"also be used for these formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13881
msgid ""
"@code{@@definfoenclose} definitions must not be recursive, directly or "
"indirectly."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13883
#, no-wrap
msgid "External Macro Processors"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13885
msgid "@nodedescription @code{#line} directives."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13885
#, no-wrap
msgid "External Macro Processors: Line Directives"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13886
#, no-wrap
msgid "External macro processors"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13887
#, no-wrap
msgid "Macro processors, external"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13895
msgid ""
"Texinfo macros (and its other text substitution facilities) work fine in "
"straightforward cases.  If your document needs unusually complex processing, "
"however, their fragility and limitations can be a problem.  In this case, "
"you may want to use a different macro processor altogether, such as M4 "
"(@pxref{Top,,, m4, M4}) or CPP (@pxref{Top,,, cpp, The C Preprocessor})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13903
msgid ""
"With one exception, Texinfo does not need to know whether its input is "
"``original'' source or preprocessed from some other source file.  Therefore, "
"you can arrange your build system to invoke whatever programs you like to "
"handle macro expansion or other preprocessing needs.  Texinfo does not offer "
"built-in support for any particular preprocessor, since no one program "
"seemed likely to suffice for the requirements of all documents."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13904
#, no-wrap
msgid "Line numbers, in error messages"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13905
#, no-wrap
msgid "Error messages, line numbers in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13910
msgid ""
"The one exception is line numbers in error messages.  In that case, the line "
"number should refer to the original source file, whatever it may be.  "
"There's a well-known mechanism for this: the so-called @samp{#line} "
"directive.  Texinfo supports this."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13912 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13913
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{#line} Directive"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13915
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{#line} directive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13918
msgid "An input line such as this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13921
#, no-wrap
msgid "@hashchar{}line 100 \"foo.ptexi\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13930
msgid ""
"indicates that the next line was line 100 of the file @file{foo.ptexi}, and "
"so that's what an error message should refer to. Both M4 "
"(@pxref{Preprocessor features,,, m4, GNU M4}) and CPP (@pxref{Line "
"Control,,, cpp, The C Preprocessor}, and @ref{Preprocessor Output,,, cpp, "
"The C Preprocessor}) can generate such lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13936
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} program recognizes these lines by default, except "
"within @code{@@verbatim} blocks (@pxref{@code{@@verbatim}}).  Their "
"recognition can be turned off completely with @code{CPP_LINE_DIRECTIVES} "
"(@pxref{Global Customization Variables}), though there is normally no reason "
"to do so."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13942
msgid ""
"For those few programs (M4, CPP, Texinfo) which need to document "
"@samp{#line} directives and therefore have examples which would otherwise "
"match the pattern, the command @code{@@hashchar@{@}} can be used "
"(@pxref{Inserting a Hashsign}).  The example line above looks like this in "
"the source for this manual:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13945
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@hashchar@{@}line 100 \"foo.ptexi\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13950
msgid ""
"The @code{@@hashchar} command was added to Texinfo in 2013.  If you don't "
"want to rely on it, you can also use @code{@@set} and @code{@@value} to "
"insert the literal @samp{#}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13954
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@set hash #\n"
"@@value@{hash@}line 1 \"example.c\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13959
msgid ""
"Or, if suitable, a @code{@@verbatim} environment can be used instead of "
"@code{@@example}.  As mentioned above, @code{#line}-recognition is disabled "
"inside verbatim blocks."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13961 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13962
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{#line} and @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13964
#, no-wrap
msgid "@TeX{} and @samp{#line} directives"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13965
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{#line} directive @subentry not processing with @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13973
msgid ""
"As mentioned, @command{texi2any} recognizes the @samp{#line} directives "
"described in the previous section.  However, @file{texinfo.tex} does not and "
"cannot.  Therefore, such a line will be incorrectly typeset verbatim if "
"@TeX{} sees it.  The solution is to use @command{texi2any}'s macro expansion "
"options before running @TeX{}.  There are three approaches:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13980
msgid ""
"If you run @command{texi2dvi} or its variants (@pxref{Format with "
"@command{texi2dvi}}), you can pass @option{-E} and @command{texi2dvi} will "
"run @command{texi2any} first to expand macros and eliminate @samp{#line}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13985
msgid ""
"If you run @command{texi2any}, you can specify @option{--no-ifinfo --iftex "
"-E somefile.out}, and then give @file{somefile.out} to @command{texi2dvi} in "
"a separate command."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13990
msgid ""
"Or you can run @option{texi2any --dvi --Xopt -E}.  (Or @option{--pdf} "
"instead of @option{--dvi}.)  @command{texi2any} will then call "
"@command{texi2dvi -E}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13992
#, no-wrap
msgid "errormsg@r{, and line numbers in @TeX{}}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:13999
msgid ""
"One last caveat regarding use with @TeX{}: since the @code{#line} directives "
"are not recognized, the line numbers emitted by the @code{@@errormsg@{@}} "
"command (@pxref{Conditional Commands}), or by @TeX{} itself, are the "
"(incorrect) line numbers from the derived file which @TeX{} is reading, "
"rather than the preprocessor-specified line numbers."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14001 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14002
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{#line} Syntax Details"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14004
#, no-wrap
msgid "@samp{#line} directive @subentry syntax details"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14005
#, no-wrap
msgid "Syntax details, @samp{#line}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14006
#, no-wrap
msgid "Regular expression, for @samp{#line}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14014
msgid ""
"Syntax details for the @samp{#line} directive: the @samp{#} character can be "
"preceded or followed by whitespace, the word @samp{line} is optional, and "
"the file name can be followed by a whitespace-separated list of integers "
"(these are so-called ``flags'' output by CPP in some cases).  For those who "
"like to know the gory details, the actual (Perl) regular expression which is "
"matched is this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14017
#, no-wrap
msgid "/^\\s*#\\s*(line)? (\\d+)(( \"([^\"]+)\")(\\s+\\d+)*)?\\s*$/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14022
msgid ""
"As far as we've been able to tell, the trailing integer flags only occur in "
"conjunction with a file name, so that is reflected in the regular expression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14025
msgid ""
"As an example, the following is a syntactically valid @samp{#line} "
"directive, meaning line 1 of @file{/usr/include/stdio.h}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14028
#, no-wrap
msgid "@hashchar{} 1 \"/usr/include/stdio.h\" 2 3 4\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14034
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, the quoted file name (@samp{\"...\"}) has to be optional, "
"because M4 (especially) can often generate @samp{#line} directives within a "
"single file.  Since the @samp{line} is also optional, the result is that "
"lines might match which you wouldn't expect, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14037
#, no-wrap
msgid "@hashchar{} 1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14040
msgid ""
"The possible solutions are described above (@pxref{@samp{#line} Directive})."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14042 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14044
#, no-wrap
msgid "Include Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14044
msgid "@nodedescription How to incorporate other Texinfo files."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14046
#, no-wrap
msgid "Include files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14053
msgid ""
"When a Texinfo processor sees an @code{@@include} command in a Texinfo file, "
"it processes the contents of the file named by the @code{@@include} and "
"incorporates them into the output files being created.  Include files thus "
"let you keep a single large document as a collection of conveniently small "
"parts."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14055
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using Include Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14057
msgid "@nodedescription How to use the @code{@@include} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14057
#, no-wrap
msgid "How to Use Include Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14059
#, no-wrap
msgid "include"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14064
msgid ""
"To include another file within a Texinfo file, write the @code{@@include} "
"command at the beginning of a line and follow it on the same line by the "
"name of a file to be included.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14067
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@include buffers.texi\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14073
msgid ""
"@code{@@value@{@var{var}@}} references are expanded on the @code{@@include} "
"line.  Other than that, the only @@-commands allowed are @code{@@@@}, "
"@code{@@@{}, @code{@@@}} and associated @@-commands such as "
"@code{@@atchar@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14082
msgid ""
"An included file should simply be a segment of text that you expect to be "
"included as is into the overall or @dfn{outer} Texinfo file; it should not "
"contain the standard beginning and end parts of a Texinfo file.  In "
"particular, you should not start an included file with a line saying "
"@samp{\\input texinfo}; if you do, that text is inserted into the output "
"file literally.  Likewise, you should not end an included file with a "
"@code{@@bye} command; nothing after @code{@@bye} is formatted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14087
msgid ""
"In the long-ago past, you were required to write an @code{@@setfilename} "
"line at the beginning of an included file, but no longer.  Now, it does not "
"matter whether you write such a line.  If an @code{@@setfilename} line "
"exists in an included file, it is ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14091
msgid ""
"GNU Emacs Texinfo mode provides @code{texinfo-multiple-files-update} to "
"update node pointers and master menu with multiple include files.  "
"@xref{Update Multiple Files}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14093
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sample Include File"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14095
msgid ""
"@nodedescription A sample outer file with included files within it; and a "
"sample included file."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14095
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sample File with @code{@@include}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14096
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sample @code{@@include} file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14097
#, no-wrap
msgid "Include file sample"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14098
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@include} file sample"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14102
msgid ""
"Here is an example of an outer Texinfo file with @code{@@include} files "
"within it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14108
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@settitle Include Example\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14114
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node Top\n"
"@@top Include Example\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14121
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@include foo.texi\n"
"@@include bar.texi\n"
"@@include concept-index.texi\n"
"@@bye\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14125
msgid "An included file, such as @file{foo.texi}, might look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14130
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@node First\n"
"@@chapter First Chapter\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14132
#, no-wrap
msgid "Contents of first chapter @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14136
msgid ""
"The full contents of @file{concept-index.texi} might be as simple as this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14151
msgid ""
"The outer Texinfo source file for @cite{The GNU Emacs Lisp Reference Manual} "
"is named @file{elisp.texi}.  This outer file has already contained a master "
"menu with 417 entries and a list of 41 @code{@@include} files."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14153
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@verbatiminclude}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14155
msgid "@nodedescription Including a file verbatim."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14155
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@verbatiminclude} @var{file}: Include a File Verbatim"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14158
#, no-wrap
msgid "verbatiminclude"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14159
#, no-wrap
msgid "Verbatim, include file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14160
#, no-wrap
msgid "Including a file verbatim"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14164
msgid ""
"You can include the exact contents of a file in the document with the "
"@code{@@verbatiminclude} command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14167
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@verbatiminclude @var{filename}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14175
msgid ""
"The contents of @var{filename} is printed in a verbatim environment "
"(@pxref{@code{@@verbatim}}).  Generally, the file is printed exactly as it "
"is, with all special characters and white space retained.  No indentation is "
"added; if you want indentation, enclose the @code{@@verbatiminclude} within "
"@code{@@example} (@pxref{@code{@@example}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14180
msgid ""
"@code{@@value@{@var{var}@}} references are expanded on the "
"@code{@@verbatiminclude} line.  This makes it possible to include files in "
"other directories within a distribution, for instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14183
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@verbatiminclude @@value@{top_srcdir@}/NEWS\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14187
msgid "(You still have to get @code{top_srcdir} defined in the first place)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14191
msgid ""
"Other than that, the only @@-commands allowed are @code{@@@@}, @code{@@@{}, "
"@code{@@@}} and associated @@-commands such as @code{@@atchar@{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:14194
msgid ""
"For a method on printing the file contents in a smaller font size, see the "
"end of the section on @code{@@verbatim}."
msgstr ""

