msgid "Shell, running @command{texi2any} in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22772
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} program provides better error messages than either of "
"the Emacs formatting commands.  We recommend it.  The @command{texi2any} "
"program is independent of Emacs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22777
msgid ""
"You can run @command{texi2any} (or @command{makeinfo}) in GNU Emacs Texinfo "
"mode by using either the @code{makeinfo-region} or the @code{makeinfo-"
"buffer} commands.  In Texinfo mode, the commands are bound to @kbd{C-c C-m C-"
"r} and @kbd{C-c C-m C-b} by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22779
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-m C-r"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22780
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x makeinfo-region"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22782 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22891
msgid "Format the current region for Info."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22782
#, no-wrap
msgid "makeinfo-region"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22784
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-m C-b"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22785
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x makeinfo-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22787 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22896
msgid "Format the current buffer for Info."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22787
#, no-wrap
msgid "makeinfo-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22794
msgid ""
"When you invoke @code{makeinfo-region} the output goes to a temporary "
"buffer.  When you invoke @code{makeinfo-buffer} output goes to the file set "
"with @code{@@setfilename} (@pxref{@code{@@setfilename}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22799
msgid ""
"The Emacs @code{makeinfo-region} and @code{makeinfo-buffer} commands run the "
"@command{texi2any} program in a temporary shell buffer.  If "
"@command{texi2any} finds any errors, Emacs displays the error messages in "
"the temporary buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22800
#, no-wrap
msgid "Errors, parsing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22801
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parsing errors"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22802
#, no-wrap
msgid "next-error"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22809
msgid ""
"You can parse the error messages by typing @kbd{C-x `} (@code{next-error}).  "
"This causes Emacs to go to and position the cursor on the line in the "
"Texinfo source that @command{texi2any} thinks caused the error.  "
"@xref{Compilation, , Running @code{make} or Compilers Generally, emacs, The "
"GNU Emacs Manual}, for more information about using the @code{next-error} "
"command."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22813
msgid ""
"In addition, you can kill the shell in which the @command{texi2any} command "
"is running or make the shell buffer display its most recent output."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22815
#, no-wrap
msgid "makeinfo-kill-job"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22816
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-m C-k"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22817
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x makeinfo-kill-job"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22820
msgid ""
"Kill the current running @command{texi2any} (or @command{makeinfo}) job "
"(from @code{makeinfo-region} or @code{makeinfo-buffer})."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22821
#, no-wrap
msgid "makeinfo-recenter-output-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22822
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-m C-l"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22823
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x makeinfo-recenter-output-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22826
msgid ""
"Redisplay the @command{texi2any} shell buffer to display its most recent "
"output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22832
msgid ""
"(Note that the parallel commands for killing and recentering a @TeX{} job "
"are @kbd{C-c C-t C-k} and @kbd{C-c C-t C-l}.  @xref{Texinfo Mode Printing}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22837
msgid ""
"You can specify options for @command{texi2any} by setting the @code{makeinfo-"
"options} variable with either the @kbd{M-x customize} or the @kbd{M-x set-"
"variable} command, or by setting the variable in your @file{.emacs} "
"initialization file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22839
msgid "For example, you could write the following in your @file{.emacs} file:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22845
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(setq makeinfo-options\n"
"     \"--paragraph-indent=0 --no-split\n"
"      --fill-column=70 --verbose\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: iftex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22855
msgid ""
"For more information, see @ref{@command{texi2any} Options}, as well as "
"``Easy Customization Interface,'' ``Examining and Setting Variables,'' and "
"``Init File'' in @cite{The GNU Emacs Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifnottex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22862
msgid ""
"For more information, see@* @ref{Easy Customization, , Easy Customization "
"Interface, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual},@* @ref{Examining, , Examining and "
"Setting Variables, emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual},@* @ref{Init File, , , "
"emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}, and@* @ref{@command{texi2any} Options}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22865
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{texinfo-format} commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: nodedescriptionblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22869
msgid ""
"Two Info formatting commands written in Emacs Lisp are an alternative to "
"@command{texi2any}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22870
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @code{texinfo-format@dots{}} Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22878
msgid ""
"In GNU Emacs in Texinfo mode, you can format part or all of a Texinfo file "
"with the @code{texinfo-format-region} command.  This formats the current "
"region and displays the formatted text in a temporary buffer called "
"@samp{*Info Region*}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22885
msgid ""
"Similarly, you can format a buffer with the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} "
"command.  This command creates a new buffer and generates the Info file in "
"it.  Typing @kbd{C-x C-s} will save the Info file under the name specified "
"by the @code{@@setfilename} line which must be near the beginning of the "
"Texinfo file."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22887 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22889
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-format-region"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22888
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-e C-r"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22892 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22894
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-format-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22893
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-e C-b"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22904
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-format-region} and @code{texinfo-format-buffer} commands "
"provide you with some error checking, and other functions can provide you "
"with further help in finding formatting errors.  These procedures are "
"described in an appendix; see @ref{Catching Mistakes}.  However, the "
"@command{texi2any} program provides better error checking "
"(@pxref{@command{texi2any} in Emacs})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22908
msgid ""
"A peculiarity of the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} and @code{texinfo-format-"
"region} commands is that they do not indent (nor fill) paragraphs that "
"contain @code{@@w} or @code{@@*} commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22910
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing with Emacs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22912
msgid "@nodedescription How to format with @TeX{} and print with Emacs."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22912
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting and Printing with Emacs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22917
msgid ""
"GNU Emacs can be used for formatting and printing with @TeX{}, from an Emacs "
"Shell.  Texinfo mode also provides predefined key commands for formatting "
"and printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22919
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo Mode Printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22921
msgid "@nodedescription How to format and print part or all of a file."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22921
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting and Printing in Texinfo Mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22922
#, no-wrap
msgid "Region printing in Texinfo mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22923
#, no-wrap
msgid "Format and print in Texinfo mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22924
#, no-wrap
msgid "Print and format in Texinfo mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22931
msgid ""
"Texinfo mode provides several predefined key commands for @TeX{} formatting "
"and printing.  These include commands for sorting indices, looking at the "
"printer queue, killing the formatting job, and recentering the display of "
"the buffer in which the operations occur."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22932
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing a region or buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22933
#, no-wrap
msgid "Region formatting and printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22934
#, no-wrap
msgid "Buffer formatting and printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22935
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part of file formatting and printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22941
msgid ""
"Often, when you are writing a document, you want to typeset and print only "
"part of a file to see what it will look like.  You can use the @code{texinfo-"
"tex-region} and related commands for this purpose.  Use the @code{texinfo-"
"tex-buffer} command to format all of a buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22948
msgid ""
"For @code{texinfo-tex-region} or @code{texinfo-tex-buffer} to work, the file "
"@emph{must} start with a @samp{\\input texinfo} line and must include a "
"@code{@@settitle} line.  The file must end with @code{@@bye} on a line by "
"itself.  (When you use @code{texinfo-tex-region}, you must surround the "
"@code{@@settitle} line with start-of-header and end-of-header lines.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22950
#, no-wrap
msgid "texinfo-tex-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22951
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-b"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22952
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-tex-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22956
msgid ""
"Run @command{texi2dvi} on the buffer.  In addition to running @TeX{} on the "
"buffer, this command automatically creates or updates indices as needed."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22957
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-r"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22958
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-tex-region"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22960
msgid "Run @TeX{} on the current region."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22967
msgid ""
"If @@-commands related to printed output are between the start-of-header and "
"end-of-header lines, then @code{texinfo-tex-region} will format the region "
"accordingly.  For example, if you write the @code{@@smallbook} command "
"between the start-of-header and end-of-header lines, @code{texinfo-tex-"
"region}, will format the region in ``small'' book size."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22968
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-i"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22969
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-texindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22978
msgid ""
"Run @command{texindex} to sort the indices of a Texinfo file formatted with "
"@code{texinfo-tex-region}.  The @code{texinfo-tex-region} command does not "
"run @command{texindex} automatically; it only runs the @command{tex} "
"typesetting command.  You must run the @code{texinfo-tex-region} command a "
"second time after sorting the raw index files with the @command{texindex} "
"command.  (Usually, you do not format an index when you format a region, "
"only when you format a buffer.  Now that the @command{texi2dvi} command "
"exists, there is little or no need for this command.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22979
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-p"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22980
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-tex-print"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22983
msgid ""
"Print a DVI file that was made with @code{texinfo-tex-region} or "
"@code{texinfo-tex-buffer}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22984
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-q"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22985
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x tex-show-print-queue"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22987
msgid "Show the print queue."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22988
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-d"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22989
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-delete-from-print-queue"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22993
msgid ""
"Delete a job from the print queue; you will be prompted for the job number "
"shown by a preceding @kbd{C-c C-t C-q} command (@code{texinfo-show-tex-print-"
"queue})."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22994
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-k"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22995
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x tex-kill-job"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:22999
msgid ""
"Kill the currently running @TeX{} job started by either @code{texinfo-tex-"
"region} or @code{texinfo-tex-buffer}, or any other process running in the "
"Texinfo shell buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23000
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-x"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23001
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x texinfo-quit-job"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23005
msgid ""
"Quit a @TeX{} formatting job that has stopped because of an error by sending "
"an @key{x} to it.  When you do this, @TeX{} preserves a record of what it "
"did in a @file{.log} file."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23006
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-t C-l"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23007
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x tex-recenter-output-buffer"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23010
msgid ""
"Redisplay the shell buffer in which the @TeX{} printing and formatting "
"commands are run to show its most recent output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23015
msgid ""
"Thus, the usual sequence of commands for formatting a buffer is as follows "
"(with comments to the right):"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23021
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-t C-b             @r{Run @command{texi2dvi} on the buffer.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-p             @r{Print the DVI file.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-q             @r{Display the printer queue.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23028
msgid ""
"The Texinfo mode @TeX{} formatting commands start a subshell in Emacs called "
"the @file{*tex-shell*}.  The @code{texinfo-tex-command}, @code{texinfo-"
"texindex-command}, and @code{tex-dvi-print-command} commands are all run in "
"this shell."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23032
msgid ""
"You can watch the commands operate in the @samp{*tex-shell*} buffer, and you "
"can switch to and from and use the @samp{*tex-shell*} buffer as you would "
"any other shell buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23036
msgid ""
"The formatting and print commands depend on the values of several "
"variables.  The default values are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23040
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    @r{Variable}                              @r{Default value}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23050
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"texinfo-texi2dvi-command                  \"texi2dvi\"\n"
"texinfo-tex-command                       \"tex\"\n"
"texinfo-texindex-command                  \"texindex\"\n"
"texinfo-delete-from-print-queue-command   \"lprm\"\n"
"texinfo-tex-trailer                       \"@@bye\"\n"
"tex-start-of-header                       \"%**start\"\n"
"tex-end-of-header                         \"%**end\"\n"
"tex-dvi-print-command                     \"lpr -d\"\n"
"tex-show-queue-command                    \"lpq\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23058
msgid ""
"You can change the values of these variables with the @kbd{M-x set-variable} "
"command (@pxref{Examining, , Examining and Setting Variables, emacs, The GNU "
"Emacs Manual}), or with your @file{.emacs} initialization file (@pxref{Init "
"File, , , emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23059
#, no-wrap
msgid "Customize Emacs package (@t{Development/Docs/Texinfo})"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23066
msgid ""
"Beginning with version 20, GNU Emacs offers a user-friendly interface, "
"called @dfn{Customize}, for changing values of user-definable variables.  "
"@xref{Easy Customization, , Easy Customization Interface, emacs, The GNU "
"Emacs Manual}, for more details about this.  The Texinfo variables can be "
"found in the @samp{Development/Docs/Texinfo} group, once you invoke the "
"@kbd{M-x customize} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23068
#, no-wrap
msgid "Compile-Command"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23070
msgid "@nodedescription How to print using Emacs's compile command."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23070
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using the Local Variables List"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23071
#, no-wrap
msgid "Local variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23072
#, no-wrap
msgid "Compile command for formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23073
#, no-wrap
msgid "Format with the compile command"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23083
msgid ""
"Yet another way to apply the @TeX{} formatting command to a Texinfo file is "
"to put that command in a @dfn{local variables list} at the end of the "
"Texinfo file.  You can then specify the @command{tex} or @command{texi2dvi} "
"commands as a @code{compile-command} and have Emacs run it by typing @kbd{M-"
"x compile}.  This creates a special shell called the @file{*compilation*} "
"buffer in which Emacs runs the compile command.  For example, at the end of "
"the @file{gdb.texi} file, after the @code{@@bye}, you could put the "
"following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23089
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Local Variables:\n"
"compile-command: \"texi2dvi gdb.texi\"\n"
"End:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23095
msgid ""
"This technique is most often used by programmers who also compile programs "
"this way; see @ref{Compilation, , , emacs, The GNU Emacs Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23099 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23101
#, no-wrap
msgid "Texinfo Mode Summary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23101
msgid "@nodedescription Summary of all the Texinfo mode commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23107
msgid ""
"In Texinfo mode, each set of commands has default keybindings that begin "
"with the same keys.  All the commands that are custom-created for Texinfo "
"mode begin with @kbd{C-c}.  The keys are somewhat mnemonic."
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23108
#, no-wrap
msgid "Insert Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23114
msgid ""
"The insert commands are invoked by typing @kbd{C-c} twice and then the first "
"letter of the @@-command to be inserted.  (It might make more sense "
"mnemonically to use @kbd{C-c C-i}, for `custom insert', but @kbd{C-c C-c} is "
"quick to type.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23126
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-c c       @r{Insert} @samp{@@code}.\n"
"C-c C-c d       @r{Insert} @samp{@@dfn}.\n"
"C-c C-c e       @r{Insert} @samp{@@end}.\n"
"C-c C-c i       @r{Insert} @samp{@@item}.\n"
"C-c C-c n       @r{Insert} @samp{@@node}.\n"
"C-c C-c s       @r{Insert} @samp{@@samp}.\n"
"C-c C-c v       @r{Insert} @samp{@@var}.\n"
"C-c @{       @r{Insert braces.}\n"
"C-c ]\n"
"C-c @}       @r{Move out of enclosing braces.}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-c C-d     @r{Insert a node's section title}\n"
"               @r{in the space for the description}\n"
"               @r{in a menu entry line.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23134
#, no-wrap
msgid "Show Structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23138
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-show-structure} command is often used within a narrowed "
"region."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23141
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-c C-s         @r{List all the headings.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23143
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Master Update Command"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23147
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-master-menu} command creates a master menu; and can be "
"used to update every node and menu in a file as well."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23154
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-u m\n"
"M-x texinfo-master-menu\n"
"               @r{Create or update a master menu.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23160
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-u C-c C-u m   @r{With @kbd{C-u} as a prefix argument, first}\n"
"               @r{create or update all nodes and regular}\n"
"               @r{menus, and then create a master menu.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23163
#, no-wrap
msgid "Update Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23168
msgid ""
"The update pointer commands are invoked by typing @kbd{C-c C-u} and then "
"either @kbd{C-n} for @code{texinfo-update-node} or @kbd{C-e} for "
"@code{texinfo-every-node-update}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23172
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-u C-n     @r{Update a node.}\n"
"C-c C-u C-e     @r{Update every node in the buffer.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23174
#, no-wrap
msgid "Update Menus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23181
msgid ""
"Invoke the update menu commands by typing @kbd{C-c C-u} and then either "
"@kbd{C-m} for @code{texinfo-make-menu} or @kbd{C-a} for @code{texinfo-all-"
"menus-update}.  To update both nodes and menus at the same time, precede "
"@kbd{C-c C-u C-a} with @kbd{C-u}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23184
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-u C-m     @r{Make or update a menu.}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23188
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-u C-a     @r{Make or update all}\n"
"               @r{menus in a buffer.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23194
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-u C-c C-u C-a @r{With @kbd{C-u} as a prefix argument,}\n"
"               @r{first create or update all nodes and}\n"
"               @r{then create or update all menus.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23197
#, no-wrap
msgid "Format for Info"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23202
msgid ""
"The Info formatting commands that are written in Emacs Lisp are invoked by "
"typing @kbd{C-c C-e} and then either @kbd{C-r} for a region or @kbd{C-b} for "
"the whole buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23207
msgid ""
"The Info formatting commands that are based on the @command{texi2any}/"
"@command{makeinfo} program are invoked by typing @kbd{C-c C-m} and then "
"either @kbd{C-r} for a region or @kbd{C-b} for the whole buffer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23211
msgid "Use the @code{texinfo-format@dots{}} commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23216
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-e C-r     @r{Format the region.}\n"
"C-c C-e C-b     @r{Format the buffer.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23222
msgid "Use @command{texi2any}/@command{makeinfo}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23228
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-m C-r     @r{Format the region.}\n"
"C-c C-m C-b     @r{Format the buffer.}\n"
"C-c C-m C-l     @r{Recenter the @command{texi2any} output buffer.}\n"
"C-c C-m C-k     @r{Kill the @command{texi2any} formatting job.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subheading
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23230
#, no-wrap
msgid "Typeset and Print"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23236
msgid ""
"The @TeX{} typesetting and printing commands are invoked by typing @kbd{C-c "
"C-t} and then another control command: @kbd{C-r} for @code{texinfo-tex-"
"region}, @kbd{C-b} for @code{texinfo-tex-buffer}, and so on."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23247
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-c C-t C-r     @r{Run @TeX{} on the region.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-b     @r{Run} @command{texi2dvi} @r{on the buffer.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-i     @r{Run} @command{texindex}.\n"
"C-c C-t C-p     @r{Print the DVI file.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-q     @r{Show the print queue.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-d     @r{Delete a job from the print queue.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-k     @r{Kill the current @TeX{} formatting job.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-x     @r{Quit a currently stopped @TeX{} formatting job.}\n"
"C-c C-t C-l     @r{Recenter the output buffer.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23253
msgid ""
"The remaining updating commands do not have standard keybindings because "
"they are rarely used."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23260
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x texinfo-insert-node-lines\n"
"               @r{Insert missing @code{@@node} lines in region.}\n"
"               @r{With @kbd{C-u} as a prefix argument,}\n"
"               @r{use section titles as node names.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23268
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x texinfo-multiple-files-update\n"
"               @r{Update a multi-file document.}\n"
"               @r{With @kbd{C-u 2} as a prefix argument,}\n"
"               @r{create or update all nodes and menus}\n"
"               @r{in all included files first.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23273
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x texinfo-indent-menu-description\n"
"               @r{Indent descriptions.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23278
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x texinfo-sequential-node-update\n"
"               @r{Insert node pointers in strict sequence.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23282
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23284
msgid "@nodedescription Processing directly Info files."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23284
#, no-wrap
msgid "Direct Formatting of Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23286
#, no-wrap
msgid "Direct formatting of Info files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23294
msgid ""
"In general, there is no need to edit or process Info files.  Most of the "
"content of Info file is plain text, but the tag tables require computing "
"offsets for nodes and indirect files (@pxref{Tag and Split Files}), which is "
"impractical.  The Texinfo processors output well-formatted Info from Texinfo "
"input.  Also, lot of information from the Texinfo source is lost in the Info "
"file.  The Texinfo source is therefore more suitable to analyse a manual, be "
"it for error reporting, for statistics, or to set up translations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23302
msgid ""
"Still, especially with manually written Info files, there are some "
"situations where it may be interesting to process Info files directly, in "
"particular to compute Info files node offsets and to compute split Info "
"files file offsets.  Two Emacs commands do that.  @code{Info-tagify} adds a "
"tag table for a nonsplit file lacking one.  This function is also useful for "
"Info file validation (@pxref{Running @code{Info-validate}}).  It is also "
"possible to split a nonsplit Info file with @code{Info-split}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23304
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tagifying"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23306
msgid "@nodedescription How to tagify a file."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23306
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tagifying a File"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23307
#, no-wrap
msgid "Making a tag table manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23308
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tag table, making manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23309
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info files @subentry making a tag table"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23317
msgid ""
"Texinfo processors create tag tables automatically.  The only exception "
"arise when @code{texinfo-format-buffer} is called with a prefix, as @kbd{C-u "
"M-x texinfo-format-buffer}, to create an non split Info file without a tag "
"table (which can be useful in some cases for Info file validation, "
"@pxref{Unsplit and Tagify}).  Adding a tag table is therefore more generally "
"useful for manually written Info files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23320
msgid ""
"To create a tag table for an nonsplit file, visit the Info file you wish to "
"tagify and type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23323 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23795
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x Info-tagify\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23330
msgid ""
"(Note the uppercase @samp{I} in @code{Info-tagify}.)  This creates an Info "
"file with a tag table.  A tag table is part of a well formed Info file "
"(@pxref{Tag and Split Files}).  A tag table is also needed to validate or "
"split the Info file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23332
#, no-wrap
msgid "Splitting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23334
msgid "@nodedescription How to split a file manually."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23334
#, no-wrap
msgid "Splitting a File Manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23335
#, no-wrap
msgid "Splitting an Info file manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23336
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info files @subentry splitting manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23342
msgid ""
"You should split a large file or else let the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} "
"or @code{makeinfo-buffer} command do it for you automatically.  (Generally "
"you will let one of the formatting commands do this job for you.  @xref{Info "
"Formatting}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23344
msgid "The split-off files are called the indirect subfiles."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23349
msgid ""
"Tag tables are created automatically by the formatting command; you only "
"need to create a tag table yourself if you are doing the job manually.  "
"@xref{Tagifying}, for information about creating a tag table."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23352
msgid ""
"Visit the Info file you wish to tagify and split manually and type the two "
"commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23356
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"M-x Info-tagify\n"
"M-x Info-split\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23360
msgid "(Note that the @samp{I} in @samp{Info} is uppercase.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23366
msgid ""
"When you use the @code{Info-split} command, the buffer is modified into a "
"(small) Info file which lists the indirect subfiles.  This file should be "
"saved in place of the original visited file.  The indirect subfiles are "
"written in the same directory the original file is in, with names generated "
"by appending @samp{-} and a number to the original file name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23369
msgid ""
"The primary file still functions as an Info file, but it contains just the "
"tag table and a directory of subfiles.  @xref{Tag and Split Files}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23371 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23373
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching Mistakes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23373
msgid "@nodedescription How to find mistakes in formatting."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23374
#, no-wrap
msgid "Structure, catching mistakes in"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23375
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes, catching mistakes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23376
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching mistakes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23377
#, no-wrap
msgid "Correcting mistakes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23378
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mistakes, catching"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23379
#, no-wrap
msgid "Problems, catching"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23380
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging the Texinfo structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23386
msgid ""
"Besides mistakes in the content of your documentation, there are two kinds "
"of mistake you can make with Texinfo: you can make mistakes with @@-"
"commands, and you can make mistakes with the structure of the nodes and "
"chapters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23390
msgid ""
"For finding problems with @@-commands, you can run @TeX{} or a region "
"formatting command on the region that has a problem; indeed, you can run "
"these commands on each region as you write it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23394
msgid ""
"For finding problems with the structure of nodes and chapters, you can use "
"@kbd{C-c C-s} (@code{texinfo-show-structure}) and the related @code{occur} "
"command and you can use the @kbd{M-x Info-validate} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23396 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23398
#, no-wrap
msgid "@command{texi2any} Preferred"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23398
msgid "@nodedescription @command{texi2any} finds errors."
msgstr ""

#.  prev name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23402
msgid "@code{makeinfo} Preferred"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23408
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} program does an excellent job of catching errors and "
"reporting them---far better than @code{texinfo-format-region} or "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer}.  In addition, the various functions for "
"automatically creating and updating node pointers and menus remove many "
"opportunities for human error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23415
msgid ""
"Use @command{texi2any} (or its Texinfo mode manifestations, @code{makeinfo-"
"region} and @code{makeinfo-buffer}) to format your file and check for other "
"errors.  This is the best way to work with Texinfo.  But if you cannot use "
"@command{texi2any}, or your problem is very puzzling, then you may want to "
"use the tools described in this section."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23417
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging with Info"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23419
msgid "@nodedescription How to catch errors with Info formatting."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23419
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching Errors with Info Formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23420
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching errors with Info formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23421
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging with Info formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23426
msgid ""
"After you have written part of a Texinfo file, you can use the @code{texinfo-"
"format-region} or the @code{makeinfo-region} command to see whether the "
"region formats properly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23431
msgid ""
"Most likely, however, you are reading this section because for some reason "
"you cannot use the @code{makeinfo-region} command; therefore, the rest of "
"this section presumes that you are using @code{texinfo-format-region}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23439
msgid ""
"If you have made a mistake with an @@-command, @code{texinfo-format-region} "
"will stop processing at or after the error and display an error message.  To "
"see where in the buffer the error occurred, switch to the @samp{*Info "
"Region*} buffer; the cursor will be in a position that is after the location "
"of the error.  Also, the text will not be formatted after the place where "
"the error occurred (or more precisely, where it was detected)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23443
msgid ""
"For example, if you accidentally end a menu with the command @code{@@end "
"menus} with an `s' on the end, instead of with @code{@@end menu}, you will "
"see an error message that says:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23446
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@end menus is not handled by texinfo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23451
msgid ""
"The cursor will stop at the point in the buffer where the error occurs, or "
"not long after it.  The buffer will look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23456
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"---------- Buffer: *Info Region* ----------\n"
"* Menu:\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23465
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* Using texinfo-show-structure::  How to use\n"
"                                 `texinfo-show-structure'\n"
"                                 to catch mistakes.\n"
"* Running Info-validate::         How to check for\n"
"                                 unreferenced nodes.\n"
"@@end menus\n"
"@point{}\n"
"---------- Buffer: *Info Region* ----------\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23471
msgid ""
"The @code{texinfo-format-region} command sometimes provides slightly odd "
"error messages.  For example, the following cross-reference fails to format:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23474
#, no-wrap
msgid "(@@xref@{Catching Mistakes, for more info.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23482
msgid ""
"In this case, @code{texinfo-format-region} detects the missing closing brace "
"but displays a message that says @samp{Unbalanced parentheses} rather than "
"@samp{Unbalanced braces}.  This is because the formatting command looks for "
"mismatches between braces as if they were parentheses."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23486
msgid ""
"Sometimes @code{texinfo-format-region} fails to detect mistakes.  For "
"example, in the following, the closing brace is swapped with the closing "
"parenthesis:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23489
#, no-wrap
msgid "(@@xref@{Catching Mistakes), for more info.@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23493
msgid "Formatting produces:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23495
#, no-wrap
msgid "(*Note for more info.: Catching Mistakes)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23499
msgid ""
"The only way for you to detect this error is to realize that the reference "
"should have looked like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23502
#, no-wrap
msgid "(*Note Catching Mistakes::, for more info.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23507
msgid ""
"Incidentally, if you are reading this node in Info and type @kbd{f "
"@key{RET}} (@code{Info-follow-reference}), you will generate an error "
"message that says:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23510
#, no-wrap
msgid "No such node: \"Catching Mistakes) The only way @dots{}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23521
msgid ""
"This is because Info perceives the example of the error as the first cross-"
"reference in this node and if you type a @key{RET} immediately after typing "
"the Info @kbd{f} command, Info will attempt to go to the referenced node.  "
"If you type @kbd{f catch @key{TAB} @key{RET}}, Info will complete the node "
"name of the correctly written example and take you to the `Catching "
"Mistakes' node.  (If you try this, you can return from the `Catching "
"Mistakes' node by typing @kbd{l} (@code{Info-last}).)"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23523 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23525
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging with @TeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23525
msgid "@nodedescription How to catch errors with @TeX{} formatting."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23526
#, no-wrap
msgid "Catching errors with @TeX{} formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23527
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging with @TeX{} formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23530
msgid "You can also catch mistakes when you format a file with @TeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23536
msgid ""
"Usually, you will want to do this after you have run @code{texinfo-format-"
"buffer} (or, better, @code{makeinfo-buffer}) on the same file, because "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer} sometimes displays error messages that make "
"more sense than @TeX{}.  (@xref{Debugging with Info}, for more information.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23539
msgid ""
"For example, @TeX{} was run on a Texinfo file, part of which is shown here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23548
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"---------- Buffer: texinfo.texi ----------\n"
"name of the Texinfo file as an extension.  The\n"
"@@samp@{??@} are `wildcards' that cause the shell to\n"
"substitute all the raw index files.  (@@xref@{sorting\n"
"indices, for more information about sorting\n"
"indices.)\n"
"---------- Buffer: texinfo.texi ----------\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23553
msgid ""
"(The cross-reference lacks a closing brace.)  @TeX{} produced the following "
"output, after which it stopped:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23563
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"---------- Buffer: *tex-shell* ----------\n"
"Runaway argument?\n"
"@{sorting indices, for more information about sorting\n"
"indices.) @@ETC.\n"
"! Paragraph ended before @@xref was complete.\n"
"<to be read again>\n"
"                  @@par\n"
"l.27\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23566
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"?\n"
"---------- Buffer: *tex-shell* ----------\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23570
msgid ""
"In this case, @TeX{} produced an accurate and understandable error message:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23573
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paragraph ended before @@xref was complete.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23580
msgid ""
"@samp{@@par} is an internal @TeX{} command of no relevance to Texinfo.  "
"@samp{l.27} means that @TeX{} detected the problem on line 27 of the Texinfo "
"file.  The @samp{?} is the prompt @TeX{} uses in this circumstance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23583
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, @TeX{} is not always so helpful, and sometimes you must truly "
"be a Sherlock Holmes to discover what went wrong."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23586
msgid ""
"In any case, if you run into a problem like this, you can do one of three "
"things."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23591
msgid ""
"You can tell @TeX{} to continue running and ignore just this error by typing "
"@key{RET} at the @samp{?} prompt."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23595
msgid ""
"You can tell @TeX{} to continue running and to ignore all errors as best it "
"can by typing @kbd{r @key{RET}} at the @samp{?} prompt."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23601
msgid ""
"This is often the best thing to do.  However, beware: the one error may "
"produce a cascade of additional error messages as its consequences are felt "
"through the rest of the file.  To stop @TeX{} when it is producing such an "
"avalanche of error messages, type @kbd{C-c} (or @kbd{C-c C-c}, if you are "
"running a shell inside Emacs)."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23605
msgid ""
"You can tell @TeX{} to stop this run by typing @kbd{x @key{RET}} at the "
"@samp{?} prompt."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23609
msgid ""
"If you are running @TeX{} inside Emacs, you need to switch to the shell "
"buffer and line at which @TeX{} offers the @samp{?} prompt."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23616
msgid ""
"Sometimes @TeX{} will format a file without producing error messages even "
"though there is a problem.  This usually occurs if a command is not ended "
"but @TeX{} is able to continue processing anyhow.  For example, if you fail "
"to end an itemized list with the @code{@@end itemize} command, @TeX{} will "
"write a DVI file that you can print out.  The only error message that @TeX{} "
"will give you is the somewhat mysterious comment:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23619
#, no-wrap
msgid "(@@end occurred inside a group at level 1)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23628
msgid ""
"However, if you print the DVI file, you will find that the text of the file "
"that follows the itemized list is entirely indented as if it were part of "
"the last item in the itemized list.  The error message is the way @TeX{} "
"says that it expected to find an @code{@@end} command somewhere in the file; "
"but that it could not determine where it was needed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23633
msgid ""
"Another source of notoriously hard-to-find errors is a missing @code{@@end "
"group} command.  If you ever are stumped by incomprehensible errors, look "
"for a missing @code{@@end group} command first."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23638
msgid ""
"If the Texinfo file lacks header lines, @TeX{} may stop in the beginning of "
"its run and display output that looks like the following.  The @samp{*} "
"indicates that @TeX{} is waiting for input."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23643
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is TeX, Version 3.14159 (Web2c 7.0)\n"
"(test.texinfo [1])\n"
"*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23651
msgid ""
"In this case, simply type @kbd{\\end @key{RET}} after the asterisk.  Then "
"write the header lines in the Texinfo file and run the @TeX{} command again. "
"(Note the use of the backslash, @samp{\\}.  @TeX{} uses @samp{\\} instead of "
"@samp{@@}; and in this circumstance, you are working directly with @TeX{}, "
"not with Texinfo.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23653
#, no-wrap
msgid "Running @code{Info-validate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23655
msgid "@nodedescription How to find badly referenced nodes."
msgstr ""

