#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23658
msgid "Running Info-Validate"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23658
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info-validate"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23659
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes, checking for badly referenced"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23660
#, no-wrap
msgid "Checking for badly referenced nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23661
#, no-wrap
msgid "Looking for badly referenced nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23662
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finding badly referenced nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23663
#, no-wrap
msgid "Badly referenced nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23670
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{Info-validate} command to check whether any of the "
"`Next', `Previous', `Up' or other node pointers fail to point to a node.  "
"This command checks that every node pointer points to an existing node.  The "
"@code{Info-validate} command works only on Info files, not on Texinfo files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23680
msgid ""
"The @command{texi2any} program validates pointers automatically, so you do "
"not need to use the @code{Info-validate} command if you are using "
"@command{texi2any}.  With the customization variable "
"@code{CHECK_NORMAL_MENU_STRUCTURE} set, @command{texi2any} will also warn if "
"the nodes pointers (either explicitly or automatically set)  are not "
"consistent with the order of node menu entries.  @command{texi2any} does not "
"check that every `Next' pointer is matched by a `Previous' (in the node "
"where the `Next' points) which points back, since it may be correct for a "
"non standard document structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23685
msgid ""
"You only may need to use @code{Info-validate} if you are unable to run "
"@command{texi2any} and instead must create an Info file using @code{texinfo-"
"format-region} or @code{texinfo-format-buffer}, or if you write an Info file "
"from scratch."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23687 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23689
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23691
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using @code{Info-validate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23689
msgid "@nodedescription How to run @code{Info-validate}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23692
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info validating a large file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23693
#, no-wrap
msgid "Validating a large file"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23697
msgid ""
"To use @code{Info-validate}, visit the Info file you wish to check and type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23700 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23801
#, no-wrap
msgid "M-x Info-validate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23706
msgid ""
"Note that the @code{Info-validate} command requires an uppercase `I'@.  You "
"may also need to create a tag table before running @code{Info-validate}.  "
"@xref{Tagifying}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23711
msgid ""
"If your file is valid, you will receive a message that says ``File appears "
"valid''.  However, if you have a pointer that does not point to a node, "
"error messages will be displayed in a buffer called @samp{*problems in info "
"file*}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23714
msgid ""
"For example, @code{Info-validate} was run on a test file that contained only "
"the first node of this manual.  One of the messages said:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23717
#, no-wrap
msgid "In node \"Overview\", invalid Next: Texinfo Mode\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23723
msgid ""
"This meant that the node called @samp{Overview} had a `Next' pointer that "
"did not point to anything (which was true in this case, since the test file "
"had only one node in it)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23727
msgid ""
"Now suppose we add a node named @samp{Texinfo Mode} to our test case but we "
"do not specify a `Previous' for this node.  Then we will get the following "
"error message:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23730
#, no-wrap
msgid "In node \"Texinfo Mode\", should have Previous: Overview\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23736
msgid ""
"This is because, with a standard document structure, every `Next' pointer "
"should be matched by a `Previous' (in the node where the `Next' points)  "
"which points back."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23739
msgid ""
"@code{Info-validate} also checks that all menu entries and cross-references "
"point to actual nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23746
msgid ""
"@code{Info-validate} requires a tag table and does not work with files that "
"have been split.  (The @code{texinfo-format-buffer} command automatically "
"splits large files.)  In order to use @code{Info-validate} on a large file, "
"you must run @code{texinfo-format-buffer} with an argument so that it does "
"not split the Info file; and you must create a tag table for the unsplit "
"file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23748
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unsplit and Tagify"
msgstr ""

#. type: nodedescriptionblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23751
msgid "How to create an unsplit file and add a tag table for validation."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23752
#, no-wrap
msgid "Creating an Unsplit File and Adding a Tag Table"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23754
msgid "Unsplit"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23755
#, no-wrap
msgid "Creating an unsplit file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23756
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unsplit file creation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23768
msgid ""
"You can run @code{Info-validate} only on a single Info file that has a tag "
"table.  The command will not work on the indirect subfiles that are "
"generated when a master file is split.  If you have a large file (longer "
"than 300,000 bytes or so), you need to run the @code{texinfo-format-buffer} "
"or @code{makeinfo-buffer} command in such a way that it does not create "
"indirect subfiles.  You will also need to create a tag table for the Info "
"file.  After you have done this, you can run @code{Info-validate} and look "
"for badly referenced nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23773
msgid ""
"The first step is to create an unsplit Info file.  To prevent @code{texinfo-"
"format-buffer} from splitting a Texinfo file into smaller Info files, give a "
"prefix to the @kbd{M-x texinfo-format-buffer} command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23776
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u M-x texinfo-format-buffer\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23780
msgid "or else"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23783
#, no-wrap
msgid "C-u C-c C-e C-b\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23788
msgid ""
"When you do this, @code{texinfo-format-buffer} will not split the file and "
"will not create a tag table for it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23792
msgid ""
"After creating an unsplit Info file, you must create a tag table for it "
"(@pxref{Tagifying}).  Visit the unsplit Info file created by @code{texinfo-"
"format-buffer} and type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23798
msgid "The third step is to validate the Info file:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23806
msgid ""
"(Note the uppercase @samp{I} in @code{Info-validate}.)  In brief, the steps "
"are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23812
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"C-u M-x texinfo-format-buffer\n"
"M-x Info-tagify\n"
"M-x Info-validate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23819
msgid ""
"After you have validated the node structure, you can rerun @code{texinfo-"
"format-buffer} in the normal way so it will construct a tag table and split "
"the file automatically, or you can make the tag table and split the file "
"manually (@pxref{Splitting})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23821 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23823
#, no-wrap
msgid "Batch Formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23823
msgid "@nodedescription How to format for Info in Emacs batch mode."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23824
#, no-wrap
msgid "Batch formatting for Info"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23825
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info batch formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23831
msgid ""
"You can format Texinfo files for Info using @code{batch-texinfo-format} and "
"Emacs batch mode.  You can run Emacs in batch mode from any shell, including "
"a shell inside of Emacs.  (@xref{Initial Options,,, emacs, The GNU Emacs "
"Manual}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23834
msgid ""
"Here is a shell command to format all the files that end in @file{.texinfo} "
"in the current directory:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23837
#, no-wrap
msgid "emacs -batch -funcall batch-texinfo-format *.texinfo\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23842
msgid ""
"Emacs processes all the files listed on the command line, even if an error "
"occurs while attempting to format some of them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23845
msgid ""
"Run @code{batch-texinfo-format} only with Emacs in batch mode as shown; it "
"is not interactive.  It kills the batch mode Emacs on completion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23852
msgid ""
"@code{batch-texinfo-format} is convenient if you lack @command{texi2any} and "
"want to format several Texinfo files at once.  When you use Batch mode, you "
"create a new Emacs process.  This frees your current Emacs, so you can "
"continue working in it.  (When you run @code{texinfo-format-region} or "
"@code{texinfo-format-buffer}, you cannot use that Emacs for anything else "
"until the command finishes.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23854 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23856
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23857
#, no-wrap
msgid "Global Document Commands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23856
msgid "@nodedescription Affecting formatting throughout."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23862
msgid ""
"Here are additional commands which affect the document as a whole.  Most of "
"these commands are for customizing the appearance of the printed output.  "
"They are generally all given before the Top node, if they are given at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23864
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@setchapternewpage}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23866
msgid "@nodedescription Start chapters on right-hand pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23866
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@setchapternewpage}: Blank Pages Before Chapters"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23869
#, no-wrap
msgid "setchapternewpage"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23870
#, no-wrap
msgid "Starting chapters"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23871
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pages, starting odd"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23879
msgid ""
"In an officially bound book, text is usually printed on both sides of the "
"paper, chapters start on right-hand pages, and right-hand pages have odd "
"numbers.  But in short reports, text often is printed only on one side of "
"the paper.  Also in short reports, chapters sometimes do not start on new "
"pages, but are printed on the same page as the end of the preceding chapter, "
"after a small amount of vertical whitespace."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23884
msgid ""
"You can use the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command with various arguments to "
"specify how chapters should be started in printed output and whether headers "
"should be formatted for printing on one or both sides of the paper (single-"
"sided or double-sided printing)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23888
msgid ""
"Write the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command at the beginning of a line "
"followed by its argument.  For example, you would write the following to "
"cause each chapter to start on a fresh odd-numbered page:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23891
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setchapternewpage odd\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23895
msgid ""
"You can specify one of three alternatives with the "
"@code{@@setchapternewpage} command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23898
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setchapternewpage off"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23902
msgid ""
"Typeset a new chapter on the same page as the last chapter, after skipping "
"some vertical whitespace.  Also, format page headers for single-sided "
"printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23903
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setchapternewpage on"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23907
msgid ""
"Start new chapters on new pages and format page headers for single-sided "
"printing.  This is the form most often used for short reports or personal "
"printing. This is the default."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23908
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@setchapternewpage odd"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23912
msgid ""
"Start new chapters on new, odd-numbered pages (right-handed pages)  and "
"typeset for double-sided printing.  This is the form most often used for "
"books and manuals."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23917
msgid ""
"Texinfo does not have a @code{@@setchapternewpage even} command, because "
"there is no printing tradition of starting chapters or books on an even-"
"numbered page."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23921
msgid ""
"If you don't like the default headers that @code{@@setchapternewpage} sets, "
"you can explicit control them with the @code{@@headings} command.  "
"@xref{@code{@@headings}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23926
msgid ""
"At the beginning of a manual or book, pages are not numbered---for example, "
"the title and copyright pages of a book are not numbered.  By convention, "
"table of contents and front matter pages are numbered with roman numerals "
"and not in sequence with the rest of the document."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23929
msgid ""
"The @code{@@setchapternewpage} has no effect in output formats that do not "
"have pages, such as Info and HTML."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23936
msgid ""
"We recommend not including any @code{@@setchapternewpage} command in your "
"document source at all, since such desired pagination is not intrinsic to "
"the document.  For a particular hard copy run, if you don't want the default "
"output (no blank pages, same headers on all pages) use the @option{--"
"texinfo} option to @command{texi2dvi} to specify the output you want."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23938 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23942
#, no-wrap
msgid "Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23939
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page Headings"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23942
msgid "@nodedescription Page headings."
msgstr ""

#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23942
msgid "Headings Introduced"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23943
#, no-wrap
msgid "Footings"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23944
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page numbering"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23945
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23946
#, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting headings and footings"
msgstr ""

#.  HTML output also supports something like these, but in a
#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23954
msgid ""
"Most printed manuals contain headings along the top of every page except the "
"title and copyright pages.  Some manuals also contain footings. Headings and "
"footings have no meaning in Info or the other output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23960
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides two standard heading formats, one for manuals printed on "
"one side of each sheet of paper, and the other for manuals printed on both "
"sides of the paper.  By default, nothing is specified for the footing of a "
"Texinfo file, so the footing remains blank."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23963
msgid ""
"Texinfo also has several heading and footing commands that you can use to "
"generate your own heading and footing formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23971
msgid ""
"In Texinfo, headings and footings are single lines at the tops and bottoms "
"of pages; you cannot create multiline headings or footings.  Each header or "
"footer line is divided into three parts: a left part, a middle part, and a "
"right part.  Any part, or a whole line, may be left blank.  Text for the "
"left part of a header or footer line is set flushleft; text for the middle "
"part is centered; and text for the right part is set flushright."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23973
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@headings}"
msgstr ""

#. type: nodedescriptionblock
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23976
msgid ""
"An option for turning headings on and off and double or single sided "
"printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23977
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @code{@@headings} Command"
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23980
msgid "headings on off"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23980
#, no-wrap
msgid "headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23987
msgid ""
"The @code{@@headings} command is rarely used.  It specifies what kinds of "
"page headings and footings to print on each page.  Usually, this is "
"controlled by the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command.  You need the "
"@code{@@headings} command only if the @code{@@setchapternewpage} command "
"does not do what you want."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23989
msgid "You can use @code{@@headings} as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23991
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings off"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23993
msgid "Turn off printing of page headings."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23994
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings single"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23996
msgid "Turn on page headings appropriate for single-sided printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23997
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings double"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:23999
msgid "Turn on page headings appropriate for double-sided printing."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24000
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings singleafter"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24001
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings doubleafter"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24004
msgid ""
"Turn on @code{single} or @code{double} headings, respectively, after the "
"current page is output."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24005
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings on"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24008
msgid ""
"Turn on page headings: @code{single} if @samp{@@setchapternewpage on}, "
"@code{double} otherwise."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24016
msgid ""
"For example, suppose you write @code{@@setchapternewpage off} before the "
"@code{@@titlepage} command to start a new chapter on the same page as the "
"end of the last chapter.  This command also causes page headers to be "
"typeset for single-sided printing.  To cause page headers to be typeset for "
"double-sided printing, write @code{@@headings double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24019
msgid ""
"You can stop any page headings at all from being generated by writing "
"@code{@@headings off} on a line of its own, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24022
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@headings off\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24025
#, no-wrap
msgid "Heading Format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24027
msgid "@nodedescription Standard page heading formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24027
#, no-wrap
msgid "Standard Heading Formats"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24032
msgid ""
"Texinfo provides two standard heading formats, one for manuals printed on "
"one side of each sheet of paper, and the other for manuals printed on both "
"sides of the paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24037
msgid ""
"The standard format for single-sided printing consists of a header line in "
"which the left-hand part contains the name of the chapter, the central part "
"is blank, and the right-hand part contains the page number.  A single-sided "
"page looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24047
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  _______________________\n"
" |                       |\n"
" | chapter   page number |\n"
" |                       |\n"
" | Start of text ...     |\n"
" | ...                   |\n"
" |                       |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24058
msgid ""
"In the standard double-sided format, the left part of the left-hand (even-"
"numbered) page contains the page number, the central part is blank, and the "
"right part contains the title (specified by the @code{@@settitle} command).  "
"The left part of the right-hand (odd-numbered) page contains the name of the "
"chapter, the central part is blank, and the right part contains the page "
"number.  An even-numbered page and an odd-numbered page, side by side as in "
"an open book, look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24068
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  _______________________     _______________________\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
" | page number     title |   | chapter   page number |\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
" | Start of text ...     |   | More  text ...        |\n"
" | ...                   |   | ...                   |\n"
" |                       |   |                       |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24075
msgid ""
"The chapter name is preceded by the word ``Chapter'', the chapter number and "
"a colon.  This makes it easier to keep track of where you are in the manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24078
msgid ""
"Note that on pages where a new chapter starts, some text may be omitted from "
"the heading line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24092
msgid ""
"By default, nothing is specified for the footing of a Texinfo file, so the "
"footing remains blank."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24095
#, no-wrap
msgid "Custom Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24097
msgid "@nodedescription How to create your own headings and footings."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24097
#, no-wrap
msgid "How to Make Your Own Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24102
msgid ""
"You can use the standard headings provided with Texinfo or specify your "
"own.  By default, Texinfo has no footers, so if you specify them, the "
"available page size for the main text will be slightly reduced."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24105
msgid "Texinfo provides six commands for specifying headings and footings:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24109
msgid ""
"@code{@@everyheading} and @code{@@everyfooting} generate page headers and "
"footers that are the same for both even- and odd-numbered pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24112
msgid ""
"@code{@@evenheading} and @code{@@evenfooting} commands generate headers and "
"footers for even-numbered (left-hand) pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24115
msgid ""
"@code{@@oddheading} and @code{@@oddfooting} generate headers and footers for "
"odd-numbered (right-hand) pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24119
msgid ""
"You must cancel the predefined heading commands with the @code{@@headings "
"off} command before defining your own specifications."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24124
msgid ""
"Here is how to place the chapter name at the left, the page number in the "
"center, and the date at the right of every header for both even- and odd-"
"numbered pages:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24129
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@headings off\n"
"@@everyheading @@thischapter @@| @@thispage @@| @@today@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24137
msgid ""
"You need to divide the left part from the central part and the central part "
"from the right part by inserting @samp{@@|} between parts.  Otherwise, the "
"specification command will not be able to tell where the text for one part "
"ends and the next part begins."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24142
msgid ""
"Each part can contain text or @@-commands.  The text is printed as if the "
"part were within an ordinary paragraph in the body of the page.  The @@-"
"commands replace themselves with the page number, date, chapter name, or "
"whatever."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24145
msgid "Here are the six heading and footing commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24147
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24148
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24149
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyheading @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24150
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyfooting @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24155
msgid ""
"The `every' commands specify the format for both even- and odd-numbered "
"pages.  These commands are for documents that are printed on one side of "
"each sheet of paper, or for documents in which you want symmetrical headers "
"or footers."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24156
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24157
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24158
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddheading"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24159
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddfooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24160
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenheading @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24161
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddheading  @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24162
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenfooting @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24163
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddfooting  @var{left} @@| @var{center} @@| @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24167
msgid ""
"The `even' and `odd' commands specify the format for even-numbered pages and "
"odd-numbered pages.  These commands are for books and manuals that are "
"printed on both sides of each sheet of paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24174
msgid ""
"Use the @samp{@@this@dots{}} series of @@-commands to provide the names of "
"chapters and sections and the page number.  You can use the "
"@samp{@@this@dots{}} commands in the left, center, or right portions of "
"headers and footers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24177
msgid "Here are the @samp{@@this@dots{}} commands:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24179
#, no-wrap
msgid "thispage"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24182
msgid "Expands to the current page number."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24183
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissection"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24187
msgid ""
"Expands to the number and name of the current section, in the format "
"`Section 1.1: Title'."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24188
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissectionname"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24191
msgid "Expands to the name of the current section."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24192
#, no-wrap
msgid "thissectionnum"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24195
msgid "Expands to the number of the current section, in the format `1.1'."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24196
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischapter"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24200
msgid ""
"Expands to the number and name of the current chapter, in the format "
"`Chapter 1: Title'."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24201
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischaptername"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24204
msgid "Expands to the name of the current chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24205
#, no-wrap
msgid "thischapternum"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24209
msgid ""
"Expands to the number of the current chapter, or letter of the current "
"appendix."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24210
#, no-wrap
msgid "thispart"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24214
msgid ""
"Expands to the name of the current part, as specified by the last "
"@code{@@part} command."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24215
#, no-wrap
msgid "thistitle"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24219
msgid ""
"Expands to the name of the document, as specified by the @code{@@settitle} "
"command."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24220
#, no-wrap
msgid "thisfile"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24228
msgid ""
"For @code{@@include} files only: expands to the name of the current "
"@code{@@include} file.  If the current Texinfo source file is not an "
"@code{@@include} file, this command has no effect.  This command does "
"@emph{not} provide the name of the current Texinfo source file unless it is "
"an @code{@@include} file.  (@xref{Include Files}, for more information about "
"@code{@@include} files.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24233
msgid ""
"You can also use the @code{@@today@{@}} command, which expands to the "
"current date, in `1 Jan 1900' format."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24233
#, no-wrap
msgid "today"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24238
msgid ""
"Other @@-commands and text are printed in a header or footer just as if they "
"were in the body of a page.  It is useful to incorporate text, particularly "
"when you are writing drafts:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24244
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@headings off\n"
"@@everyheading @@emph@{Draft!@} @@| @@thispage @@| @@thischapter\n"
"@@everyfooting @@| @@| Version: 0.27: @@today@{@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24249
msgid ""
"Beware of overlong titles: they may overlap another part of the header or "
"footer and blot it out."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24254
msgid ""
"If you have very short chapters and/or sections, several of them can appear "
"on a single page.  You can specify which chapters and sections you want "
"@code{@@thischapter}, @code{@@thissection} and other such macros to refer to "
"on such pages as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24256
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24257
#, no-wrap
msgid "everyfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24258
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24259
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@everyfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24265
msgid ""
"The @var{ref} argument can be either @code{top} (the @code{@@this...} "
"commands will refer to the chapter/section at the top of a page) or "
"@code{bottom} (the commands will reflect the situation at the bottom of a "
"page).  These @samp{@@every...} commands specify what to do on both even- "
"and odd-numbered pages."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24266
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24267
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddheadingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24268
#, no-wrap
msgid "evenfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24269
#, no-wrap
msgid "oddfootingmarks"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24270
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24271
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddheadingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24272
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@evenfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24273
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@oddfootingmarks @var{ref}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24277
msgid ""
"These @samp{@@even...} and @samp{@@odd...} commands specify what to do on "
"only even- or odd-numbered pages, respectively.  The @var{ref} argument is "
"the same as with the @samp{@@every...} commands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24283
msgid ""
"Write these commands immediately after the @code{@@...contents} commands, or "
"after the @code{@@end titlepage} command if you don't have a table of "
"contents or if it is printed at the end of your manual.  These commands have "
"no effect in @LaTeX{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24286
msgid ""
"By default, for @TeX{}, the @code{@@this...} commands reflect the situation "
"at the bottom of a page both in headings and in footings."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24288
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@paragraphindent}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24290
msgid "@nodedescription Specify paragraph indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24290
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@paragraphindent}: Controlling Paragraph Indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24294
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indenting paragraphs, control of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24295
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paragraph indentation control"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24302
msgid ""
"The Texinfo processors may insert whitespace at the beginning of the first "
"line of each paragraph, thereby indenting that paragraph.  You can use the "
"@code{@@paragraphindent} command to specify this indentation.  Write a "
"@code{@@paragraphindent} command at the beginning of a line followed by "
"either @samp{asis} or a number:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24305
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@paragraphindent @var{indent}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24308 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24398
msgid "The indentation is according to the value of @var{indent}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24313 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24403
msgid ""
"Do not change the existing indentation (not implemented in printed output)."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24315 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24404
#, no-wrap
msgid "0"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24317 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24406
msgid "Omit all indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: var{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24318 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24407
#, no-wrap
msgid "n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24321
msgid ""
"Indent by @var{n} space characters in Info output, by @var{n} ems in printed "
"output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24326
msgid ""
"The default value of @var{indent} is 3.  @code{@@paragraphindent} is ignored "
"for HTML output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24331
msgid ""
"It is best to write the @code{@@paragraphindent} command before the end-of-"
"header line at the beginning of a Texinfo file, so the region formatting "
"commands indent paragraphs as specified.  @xref{Start of Header}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24333
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@firstparagraphindent}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24335
msgid "@nodedescription Suppressing first paragraph indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24335
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@firstparagraphindent}: Indenting After Headings"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24338
#, no-wrap
msgid "firstparagraphindent"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24339
#, no-wrap
msgid "First paragraph, suppressing indentation of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24340
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppressing first paragraph indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24341
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preventing first paragraph indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24342
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indenting, suppressing of first paragraph"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24343
#, no-wrap
msgid "Headings, indentation after"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24350
msgid ""
"As you can see in the present manual, the first paragraph in any section is "
"not indented by default.  Typographically, indentation is a paragraph "
"separator, which means that it is unnecessary when a new section begins.  "
"This indentation is controlled with the @code{@@firstparagraphindent} "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24353
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@firstparagraphindent @var{word}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24357
msgid ""
"The first paragraph after a heading is indented according to the value of "
"@var{word}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24363
msgid ""
"Prevents the first paragraph from being indented (default).  This option is "
"ignored by @command{makeinfo} if @code{@@paragraphindent asis} is in effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24364
#, no-wrap
msgid "insert"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24368
msgid ""
"Include normal paragraph indentation.  This respects the paragraph "
"indentation set by a @code{@@paragraphindent} command "
"(@pxref{@code{@@paragraphindent}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24371
msgid "@code{@@firstparagraphindent} is ignored for HTML and DocBook output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24376
msgid ""
"It is best to write the @code{@@firstparagraphindent} command before the end-"
"of-header line at the beginning of a Texinfo file, so the region formatting "
"commands indent paragraphs as specified.  @xref{Start of Header}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24378
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@exampleindent}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24380
msgid "@nodedescription Specify environment indentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24380
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@exampleindent}: Environment Indenting"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24383
#, no-wrap
msgid "exampleindent"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24384
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indenting environments"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24385
#, no-wrap
msgid "Environment indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24386
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example indentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24392
msgid ""
"The Texinfo processors indent each line of @code{@@example} and similar "
"environments.  You can use the @code{@@exampleindent} command to specify "
"this indentation.  Write an @code{@@exampleindent} command at the beginning "
"of a line followed by either @samp{asis} or a number:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24395
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@exampleindent @var{indent}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24410
msgid ""
"Indent environments by @var{n} space characters in Info output, by @var{n} "
"ems in printed output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24416
msgid ""
"The default value of @var{indent} is 5 spaces in Info, and 0.4@dmn{in} in "
"printed output, which is somewhat less.  (The reduction is to help fit more "
"characters onto physical lines in printed manuals.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24421
msgid ""
"It is best to write the @code{@@exampleindent} command before the end-of-"
"header line at the beginning of a Texinfo file, so the region formatting "
"commands indent paragraphs as specified.  @xref{Start of Header}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24423
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@smallbook}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24425
msgid "@nodedescription How to print small format books and manuals."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24425
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@smallbook}: Printing ``Small'' Books"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24428
#, no-wrap
msgid "smallbook"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24429
#, no-wrap
msgid "Small book size"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24430
#, no-wrap
msgid "Book, printing small"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24431
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page sizes for books"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24432
#, no-wrap
msgid "Size of printed book"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24440
msgid ""
"By default, @TeX{} typesets pages for printing in an 8.5 by 11 inch format, "
"which is the ``letter'' size commonly used in the United States.  However, "
"you can direct @TeX{} or @LaTeX{} to typeset a document in a 7 by 9.25 inch "
"format that is suitable for bound books by inserting the following command "
"on a line by itself at the beginning of the Texinfo file, before the title "
"page:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24443
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@smallbook\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24450
msgid ""
"(Since many books are about 7 by 9.25 inches, this command might better have "
"been called the @code{@@regularbooksize} command, but it came to be called "
"the @code{@@smallbook} command by comparison to the 8.5 by 11 inch format.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24454
msgid ""
"@xref{Format with @command{texi2dvi} or @command{texi2pdf}}, and "
"@ref{Preparing for @TeX{}}, for other ways to format with @code{@@smallbook} "
"with @TeX{} that do not require changing the source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24456
#, no-wrap
msgid "A4 Paper"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24458
msgid "@nodedescription How to print on A4, A5 or B6 paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24458
#, no-wrap
msgid "Printing on A4 Paper"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24459
#, no-wrap
msgid "A4 paper, printing on"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24460
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paper size, A4"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24461
#, no-wrap
msgid "European A4 paper"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24462
#, no-wrap
msgid "afourpaper"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24466
msgid ""
"You can format a document for printing on ISO 216 A4 paper size with the "
"@code{@@afourpaper} command.  Write the command on a line by itself near the "
"beginning of the Texinfo file, before the title page."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24467
#, no-wrap
msgid "A5 paper, printing on"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24468
#, no-wrap
msgid "afourlatex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24469
#, no-wrap
msgid "afourwide"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24470
#, no-wrap
msgid "afivepaper"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24471
#, no-wrap
msgid "bsixpaper"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24476
msgid ""
"You may or may not prefer the formatting that results from the command "
"@code{@@afourlatex}.  There's also @code{@@afourwide} for A4 paper in wide "
"format, @code{@@afivepaper} for A5 paper, and @code{@@bsixpaper} for B6 "
"paper."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24480
msgid ""
"@xref{Format with @command{texi2dvi} or @command{texi2pdf}} and "
"@ref{Preparing for @TeX{}}, for other ways to format for different paper "
"sizes that do not require changing the source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24482
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@pagesizes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24484
msgid "@nodedescription How to print with customized page sizes."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24484
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{@@pagesizes} [@var{width}][, @var{height}]: Custom Page Sizes"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24486 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24487
#, no-wrap
msgid "pagesizes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24488
#, no-wrap
msgid "Custom page sizes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24489
#, no-wrap
msgid "Page sizes, customized"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24490
#, no-wrap
msgid "Text width and height"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24491
#, no-wrap
msgid "Width of text area"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24492
#, no-wrap
msgid "Height of text area"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24493
#, no-wrap
msgid "Depth of text area"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24500
msgid ""
"You can explicitly specify the height and (optionally) width of the main "
"text area on the page with the @code{@@pagesizes} command.  Write this on a "
"line by itself near the beginning of the Texinfo file, before the title "
"page.  The height comes first, then the width if desired, separated by a "
"comma.  Examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24503
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@pagesizes 200mm,150mm  @c for b5 paper\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24508
#, no-wrap
msgid "@@pagesizes 11.5in       @c for legal paper\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24510
#, no-wrap
msgid "B5 paper, printing on"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24511
#, no-wrap
msgid "Legal paper, printing on"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24516
msgid ""
"This would be reasonable for printing on B5-size paper.  To emphasize, this "
"command specifies the size of the @emph{text area}, not the size of the "
"paper (which is 250@dmn{mm} by 177@dmn{mm} for B5, 14@dmn{in} by 8.5@dmn{in} "
"for legal)."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24517
#, no-wrap
msgid "Margins on page, not controllable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24521
msgid ""
"To make more elaborate changes, such as changing any of the page margins, "
"you must define a new command in @file{texinfo.tex} or @file{texinfo.cnf}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24525
msgid ""
"@xref{Format with @command{texi2dvi} or @command{texi2pdf}}, and "
"@ref{Preparing for @TeX{}}, for other ways to specify @code{@@pagesizes} "
"that do not require changing the source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24527 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24529
#, no-wrap
msgid "Microtypography"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24529
msgid "@nodedescription Improving the appearance of paragraphs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24537
msgid ""
"Some versions of @TeX{}, namely pdf@TeX{} and Lua@TeX{}, have "
"@dfn{microtypography} features.  These involve stretching font glyphs "
"slightly, and allowing text to produce very slightly into the margins.  Use "
"of these gives @TeX{} more flexibility in breaking a paragraph into lines, "
"and can improve paragraph appearance by reducing hyphenation and producing a "
"more consistent typographic color."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24538
#, no-wrap
msgid "microtype"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24542
msgid ""
"@file{texinfo.tex} uses these features (`microtype' for short) when "
"available.  You can turn microtype off by specifying @samp{@@microtype off} "
"in your input file; likewise, @samp{@@microtype on} turns microtype back on."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24543
#, no-wrap
msgid "CM-Super fonts @subentry for @LaTeX{}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24551
msgid ""
"The fonts used in @LaTeX{} output may not be scalable.  If fonts are not "
"scalable, using microtypography could trigger an error when converting the "
"@LaTeX{} output.  Therefore microtypography is not turned on in the default "
"case in @LaTeX{} output.  It is possible to turn it on with "
"@samp{@@microtype on}.  A way to get scalable fonts for the font used in the "
"default @LaTeX{} output is to install the CM-Super font package (@url{http://"
"ctan.org/pkg/cm-super})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24553
msgid "The @code{@@microtype} command does nothing for other output formats."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24555 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24557
#, no-wrap
msgid "Magnification"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24557
msgid "@nodedescription How to print scaled up output."
msgstr ""

#.  old name
#. type: anchor{#1}
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24559
msgid "Cropmarks and Magnification"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24560
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\mag @r{(raw @TeX{} magnification)}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24561
#, no-wrap
msgid "Magnified printing"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24562
#, no-wrap
msgid "Larger or smaller pages"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24570
msgid ""
"You can attempt to direct @TeX{} to typeset pages larger or smaller than "
"usual with the @code{\\mag} @TeX{} command.  Everything that is typeset is "
"scaled proportionally larger or smaller.  (@code{\\mag} stands for "
"``magnification''.)  This is @emph{not} a Texinfo @@-command, but is a raw "
"@TeX{} command that is prefixed with a backslash.  You have to write this "
"command between @code{@@tex} and @code{@@end tex} (@pxref{Raw Formatter "
"Commands})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24575
msgid ""
"Follow the @code{\\mag} command with an @samp{=} and then a number that is "
"1000 times the magnification you desire.  For example, to print pages at 1.2 "
"normal size, write the following near the beginning of the Texinfo file, "
"before the title page:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24581
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@@tex\n"
"\\global\\mag=1200\n"
"@@end tex\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24588
msgid ""
"With some printing technologies, you can print normal-sized copies that look "
"better than usual by giving a larger-than-normal master to your print shop.  "
"They do the reduction, thus effectively increasing the resolution."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24592
msgid ""
"Depending on your system, DVI files prepared with a nonstandard-@code{\\mag} "
"may not print or may print only with certain magnifications.  Be prepared to "
"experiment."
msgstr ""

#. type: appendix
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24594 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24596
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Specification"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24596
msgid "@nodedescription Technical details of the Info file format."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24598
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info format specification"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24599
#, no-wrap
msgid "Specification of Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24600
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition of Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24603
msgid "Here we describe the technical details of the Info format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24612
msgid ""
"In this formal description, the characters @code{<>*()|=#} are used for the "
"language of the description itself.  Other characters are literal.  The "
"formal constructs used are typical: @code{<...>} indicates a metavariable "
"name, @samp{=} means definition, @samp{*} repetition, @samp{?} optional, "
"@samp{()} grouping, @samp{|} alternation, and @samp{#} comment."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24617
msgid ""
"In general, programs that read Info files should try to be case-insensitive "
"to keywords that occur in the file (for example, @samp{Tag Table} and "
"@samp{Tag table} should be equivalent) in order to support Info-generating "
"programs that use different capitalization."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24623
msgid ""
"We specify literal parentheses (those that are part of the Info format) with "
"@t{<lparen>} and @t{<rparen>}, meaning the single characters @samp{(} and "
"@samp{)} respectively.  Finally, the two-character sequence @samp{^@var{x}} "
"means the single character @samp{CTRL-@var{x}}, for any @var{x}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24631
msgid ""
"This format definition was written some 25 years after the Info format was "
"first devised.  So in the event of conflicts between this definition and "
"actual practice, practice wins.  It also assumes some general knowledge of "
"Texinfo; it is meant to be a guide for implementors rather than a rigid "
"technical standard.  We may refer back to other parts of this manual for "
"examples and definitions, rather than redundantly spelling out every detail."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Whole: Info Format Whole Manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Preamble: Info Format Preamble."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Indirect: Info Format Indirect Table."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Tag table: Info Format Tag Table."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Local variables: Info Format Local Variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Regular nodes: Info Format Regular Nodes."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Menu: Info Format Menu."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Image: Info Format Image."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Printindex: Info Format Printindex."
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24643
msgid "Cross-Reference: Info Format Cross Reference"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24646
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Whole Manual"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24648
msgid "@nodedescription Split vs.@: nonsplit manuals."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24648
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: A Whole Manual"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24650
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nonsplit manuals, Info format of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24651
#, no-wrap
msgid "Split manuals, Info format of"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24652
#, no-wrap
msgid "Whole manual, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24656
msgid ""
"To begin, an Info manual is either @dfn{nonsplit} (contained wholly within a "
"single file) or @dfn{split} (across several files)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24658
msgid "The syntax for a nonsplit manual is:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24665
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <nonsplit info file> =\n"
"<preamble>\n"
"<node>*\n"
"<tag table>?\n"
"<local variables>?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24670
msgid ""
"When split, there is a @dfn{main file}, which contains only pointers to the "
"nodes given in other @dfn{subfiles}.  The main file looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24677
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <split info main file> =\n"
"<preamble>\n"
"<indirect table>\n"
"<tag table>\n"
"<local variables>?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24680
msgid "The subfiles in a split manual have the following syntax:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24685
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <split info subfile> =\n"
"<preamble>\n"
"<node>*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24689
msgid ""
"Note that the tag table is not optional for split files, as it is used with "
"the indirect table to deduce which subfile a particular node is in."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24692
msgid ""
"Several of the sections in an Info file (such as nodes or tag tables) begin "
"with a sequence:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24695
#, no-wrap
msgid "    <separator> = (^L)?^_(^L)?^J\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24700
msgid ""
"That is, a @samp{CTRL-_} character followed by a newline, with optional "
"formfeed characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24703
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Preamble"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24704
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Preamble"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24706
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preamble, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24711
msgid ""
"The @t{<preamble>} is text at the beginning of all output files.  It is not "
"intended to be visible by default in an Info viewer, but may be displayed "
"upon user request."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24717
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <preamble> =\n"
"<identification>       # \"This is FILENAME, produced by ...\"\n"
"<copying text>         # Expansion of @@copying text.\n"
"<dir entries>          # Derived from @@dircategory and @@direntry.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24721
msgid "These pieces are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24723
#, no-wrap
msgid "<identification line>"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24726
msgid ""
"An arbitrary string beginning the output file, followed by a blank line."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24727
#, no-wrap
msgid "<copying text>"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24730
msgid ""
"The expansion of a @code{@@copying} environment, if the manual has one "
"(@pxref{@code{@@copying}})."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24731
#, no-wrap
msgid "<dir entries>"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24734
msgid ""
"The result of any @code{@@dircategory} and @code{@@direntry} commands "
"present in the manual (@pxref{Installing Dir Entries})."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24738
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Indirect Table"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24739
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Indirect Table"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24741
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indirect table, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24748
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <indirect table> =\n"
"<separator>\n"
"Indirect:\n"
"(<filename>: <bytepos>)*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24756
msgid ""
"The indirect table is written to the main file in the case of split output "
"only.  It specifies, as a decimal integer, the starting byte position (zero-"
"based) that the first node of each subfile would have if the subfiles were "
"concatenated together in order, not including the top-level file.  The first "
"node of actual content is pointed to by the first entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24759
msgid ""
"As an example, suppose split output is generated for the GDB manual.  The "
"top-level file @file{gdb.info} will contain something like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24766
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<separator>\n"
"Indirect:\n"
"gdb.info-1: 1878\n"
"gdb.info-2: 295733\n"
"...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24773
msgid ""
"This tells Info viewers that the first node of the manual occurs at byte "
"1878 of the file @file{gdb.info-1} (which would be after that file's "
"preamble.)  The first node in the @file{gdb.info-2} subfile would start at "
"byte 295733 if @file{gdb.info-2} were appended to @file{gdb.info-1}, "
"including any preamble sections in both files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24779
msgid ""
"Unfortunately, Info-creating programs such as @command{makeinfo} have not "
"always implemented these rules perfectly, due to various bugs and "
"oversights.  Therefore, robust Info viewers should fall back to searching "
"``nearby'' the given position for a node, instead of giving up immediately "
"if the position is not exactly at a node beginning."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24781
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Tag Table"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24782
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Tag Table"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24784
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tag table, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24794
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <tag table> =\n"
"<separator>\n"
"Tag Table:\n"
"(<lparen>Indirect<rparen>)?\n"
"(Node|Ref): <nodeid>^?<bytepos>\n"
"<separator>\n"
"End Tag Table\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24797
msgid "The @samp{(Indirect)} line appears in the case of split output only."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24801
msgid ""
"The tag table specifies the starting byte position of each node and anchor "
"in the file.  In the case of split output, it is only written in the main "
"output file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24807
msgid ""
"Each line defines an identifier as either an anchor or a node, as "
"specified.  For example, @samp{Node: Top^?1647} says that the node named "
"@samp{Top} starts at byte 1647 while @samp{Ref: Overview-Footnote-1^?30045} "
"says that the anchor named @samp{Overview-Footnote-1} starts at byte 30045.  "
"It is an error to define the same identifier both ways."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24812
msgid ""
"In the case of nonsplit output, the byte positions simply refer to the "
"location in the output file.  In the case of split output, the byte "
"positions refer to an imaginary file created by concatenating all the split "
"files (but not the top-level file).  See the previous section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24814 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25048
msgid "Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24822
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"^_\n"
"Tag Table:\n"
"Node: Top^?89\n"
"Node: Ch1^?292\n"
"^_\n"
"End Tag Table\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24828
msgid ""
"This specifies a manual with two nodes, `Top' and `Ch1', at byte positions "
"89 and 292 respectively.  Because the @samp{(Indirect)} line is not present, "
"the manual is not split."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24831
msgid ""
"Preamble sections or other non-node sections of files do not have a tag "
"table entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24833
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Local Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24834
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Local Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24836
#, no-wrap
msgid "Local variable section, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24840
msgid ""
"The local variables section is optional.  It is currently used to give the "
"encoding information and/or the document language code."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24848
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <local variables> =\n"
"<separator>\n"
"Local Variables:\n"
"(coding: <encoding>)?\n"
"(Info-documentlanguage: <language>)?\n"
"End:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24851
msgid ""
"@xref{@code{@@documentencoding}} for information about the document encoding."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24856
msgid ""
"@samp{<language>} should be of the form @var{ll} or @var{ll}_@var{cc}; see "
"@ref{@code{@@documentlanguage}}.  Only one language can be specified in the "
"local variables section even if @code{@@documentlanguage} occurs several "
"times in a Texinfo document."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24858
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Regular Nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24859
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Regular Nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24861
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info nodes, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24864
msgid "Regular nodes look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24869
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <node> =\n"
"<separator>\n"
"File: <fn>, Node: <id1>, (Next: <id2>, )? (Prev: <id3>, )? Up: <id4>\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24871
#, no-wrap
msgid "<general text, until the next ^_ or end-of-file>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24877
msgid ""
"At least one space or tab must be present after each colon and comma, but "
"any number of spaces are ignored.  The @t{<id>} node identifiers have "
"following format:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24882
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   <id> = (<lparen><infofile><rparen>)?<node-spec>?\n"
"   <node-spec> = <nodename> | <del><nodename><del>\n"
"   <del> = ^?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24887
msgid ""
"This @t{<node>} defines @t{<id1>} in file @t{<fn>}, which is typically "
"either @samp{manualname} or @samp{manualname.info}.  No parenthesized "
"@t{<infofile>} component may appear within @t{<id1>}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24894
msgid ""
"Each of the identifiers after @code{Next}, @code{Prev} and @code{Up} refer "
"to nodes or anchors within a file.  These pointers normally refer within the "
"same file, but @samp{(dir)} is often used to point to the top-level dir "
"file.  If an @t{<infofile>} component is used then the node name may be "
"omitted, in which case the node identifier refers to the @samp{Top} node "
"within the referenced file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24900
msgid ""
"The @code{Next} and @code{Prev} pointers are optional.  The @code{Up} "
"pointer is technically also optional, although most likely this indicates a "
"mistake in the node structuring.  Conventionally, the nodes are arranged to "
"form a tree, but this is not a requirement of the format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24907
msgid ""
"Node names containing periods, commas, colons or parentheses can confuse "
"Info readers.  If it is necessary to refer to a node whose name contains any "
"of these, the @t{<nodename>} should be surrounded by a pair of @code{DEL} "
"characters (@samp{CTRL-?}, character number 127).  @command{makeinfo} adds "
"these characters when needed in the default case.  Note that not all Info "
"readers recognize this syntax.  @xref{Info Node Names Constraints}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24910
msgid ""
"The @t{<general text>} of the node can include the special constructs "
"described next."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24912
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24913
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24915
#, no-wrap
msgid "Menus, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24919
msgid ""
"Conventionally menus appear at the end of nodes, but the Info format places "
"no restrictions on their location."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24924
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <menu> =\n"
"* Menu:\n"
"(<menu entry> | <menu comment>)*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24931
msgid ""
"The parts of a @t{<menu entry>} are also described in @ref{Menu Parts}.  "
"They have the same syntax as cross-references, with a leading @samp{*} "
"instead of @samp{* (N|n)ote} (@pxref{Info Format Cross Reference}).  Indices "
"extend the menu format to specify the destination line; @pxref{Info Format "
"Printindex}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24936
msgid ""
"A @t{<menu comment>} is any line not beginning with @samp{*} that appears "
"either at the beginning of the menu or is separated from a menu entry by one "
"or more blank lines.  These comments are intended to be displayed as part of "
"the menu, as-is (@pxref{Writing a Menu})."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24938
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Image"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24939
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Image"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24941
#, no-wrap
msgid "Images @subentry in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24945
msgid ""
"The @code{@@image} command results in the following special directive within "
"the Info file (@pxref{Images}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24954
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <image> =\n"
"^@@^H[image src=\"<image file>\"\n"
"            (dpi=<dpi>)?\n"
"            (depth=<depth>)?\n"
"            (text=\"<txt file contents>\")?\n"
"            (alt=\"<alt text>\")?\n"
"^@@^H]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24959
msgid ""
"The line breaks and indentation in this description are editorial; the "
"whitespace between the different parts of the directive in Info files is "
"arbitrary."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24963
msgid ""
"In the strings @t{<image file>}, @t{<txt file contents>} and @t{<alt text>}, "
"@samp{\"} is quoted as @samp{\\\"} and @samp{\\} is quoted as @samp{\\\\}.  "
"The txt and alt specifications are optional."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24968
msgid ""
"The @t{alt} value serves the same purpose as in HTML: A prose description of "
"the image.  In text-only displays or speech systems, for example, the "
"@t{alt} value may be used instead of displaying the (typically graphical) "
"@t{<image file>}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24971
msgid ""
"The @t{<txt file contents>}, if present, should be taken as an ASCII "
"representation of the image, for possible use on a text-only display."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24974
msgid ""
"The format does not prescribe the choice between displaying the @t{<image "
"file>}, the @t{<alt text>} or the @t{<txt file contents>}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24978
msgid ""
"The @t{dpi} and @t{depth} options are only output for the experimental "
"@samp{INFO_MATH_IMAGES} variable of @command{texi2any} (@pxref{Info and "
"Plaintext Customization Variables})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24983
msgid ""
"@t{dpi} gives the dots-per-inch (@dfn{DPI}) of the image.  Info-viewing "
"programs could use this to scale the image so it is an appropriate size for "
"surrounding text.  Here is a possible formula for the height of an image in "
"pixels:"
msgstr ""

#. type: displaymath
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24986
msgid "h_{@r{display}} = (h_{@r{file}} / dpi) * (72.27/10) * f"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24993
msgid ""
"Here, @math{h_{@r{display}}} is the height in pixels to display the image "
"at, @math{h_{@r{file}}} is the height in pixels of the unscaled image, "
"@math{dpi} is the DPI from the Info file, and @math{f} is the font size in "
"pizels of the currently used font."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:24999
msgid ""
"The 72.27 in this formula is the number of points in an inch (as used by "
"@TeX{}).  The 10 is the default 10 @dmn{point} font size of @TeX{}, in other "
"words, the size of 1 em.  The quotient (72.27/10) gives the number of ems "
"per inch and converts from the height of the image in inches to the height "
"of the image in ems."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25003
msgid ""
"@t{depth} gives the below-baseline depth of the image, in image pixels "
"(before scaling).  Viewing programs should use it for shifting the image "
"downwards to align it properly with surrounding text."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25005
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Printindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25006
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Printindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25008
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indices, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25012
msgid ""
"An index in Info format is a kind of menu, with an additional directive at "
"the beginning to mark it as an index menu."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25017
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <printindex> =\n"
"^@@^H[index^@@^H]\n"
"* Menu:\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25019
#, no-wrap
msgid "<index entry>*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25024
msgid ""
"The @t{<index entry>} items are similar to normal menu entries, but the free-"
"format description is replaced by the line number of where the entries "
"occurs in the text:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25028
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <index entry> =\n"
"* <entry text>: <node-spec>. <line-spec>\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25031
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <line-spec> =\n"
"<lparen>line <lineno><rparen>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25037
msgid ""
"The initial part, @samp{<entry text>: <entry node>.}, should be on a single "
"line.  The @t{<entry text>} is the index term."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25042
msgid ""
"Even though @t{<entry text>} is followed by a colon, it may itself contain "
"colons, so Info readers should try to include as much of the line as "
"possible in the @t{<entry text>}.  (However, @t{<entry text>} may not "
"contain the @code{DEL} characters that may occur in @t{<node-spec>}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25046
msgid ""
"@t{<lineno>} is an unsigned integer, given relative to the start of the "
"@t{<entry node>}.  An optional line break may occur before @samp{<line-"
"spec>}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25052
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"^@@^H[index^@@^H]\n"
"* Menu:\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25054
#, no-wrap
msgid "* thunder:           Weather Phenomena.             (line 5)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25058
msgid ""
"This means that an index entry for `thunder' appears at line 5 of the node "
"`Weather Phenomena'."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25060
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format Cross Reference"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25061
#, no-wrap
msgid "Info Format: Cross-reference"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25063
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cross-references, in Info format"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25066
msgid ""
"A general cross-reference in Info format has one of the following two forms:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25072
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <cross-reference> =\n"
"  * (N|n)ote <id>::\n"
"| * (N|n)ote <label>:<id>(.|,)\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25075
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    <id> = (<lparen><infofile><rparen>)?<node-spec>?\n"
"    <label> = <label text> | <del><label text><del>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25083
msgid ""
"No space should occur between the @samp{*} character and the following "
"@samp{N} or @samp{n}.  @samp{*Note} should be used at the start of a "
"sentence, otherwise @samp{*note} should be used.  (Some Info readers, such "
"as the one in Emacs, can display @samp{*Note} and @samp{*note} as @samp{See} "
"and @samp{see} respectively.) In both cases, @t{<label text>} is descriptive "
"text."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25089
msgid ""
"In both forms the @t{<id>} refers to a node or anchor, in the same way as a "
"reference in the node information line does (@pxref{Info Format Regular "
"Nodes}).  The optional parenthesized @samp{<infofile>} is the name of the "
"manual being referenced, and @t{<node-spec>} gives the node or anchor within "
"that manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25093
msgid ""
"The second form has a descriptive label.  A cross-reference in this form "
"should usually be terminated with a comma or period, to make it feasible to "
"find the end of the @t{<id>}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25101
msgid ""
"If @t{<label>} contains a colon character (@t{:}), it should be surrounded "
"with a pair of @t{<del>} characters.  Likewise, quoting characters may be "
"used for the node name if it contains problematic characters; then a "
"terminating comma or period is not needed.  As stated earlier, this quoting "
"mechanism is not supported in all Info-reading programs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25104
msgid ""
"The format does not prescribe how to find other manuals to resolve such "
"references."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25106
msgid "Here are some examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25113
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*note GNU Free Documentation License::\n"
"*note Tag table: Info Format Tag Table, for details.\n"
"*Note Overview: (make)Top.\n"
"*Note ^?:^?: (bash)Bourne Shell Builtins.\n"
"*Note alloca.h: (gnulib)^?alloca.h^?.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25117
msgid ""
"The first shows a reference to a node in the current manual using the short "
"form."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25122
msgid ""
"The second also refers to a node in the current manual, namely `Info Format "
"Tag Table'; the `Tag table' before the @samp{:} is only a label on this "
"particular reference, and the @samp{for details.} is text belonging to the "
"sentence, not part of the reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25125
msgid ""
"The third example refers to the node `Top' in another manual, namely "
"@samp{make}, with `Overview' being the label for this cross-reference."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25128
msgid ""
"The fourth example shows a colon character being quoted in a label, and the "
"fifth example shows a period being quoted in a node name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25130
msgid "@xref{Cross References}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25134
msgid "@nodedescription Copying this manual."
msgstr ""

#. type: unnumbered
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25139 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25140
#, no-wrap
msgid "Command Index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25145
msgid ""
"This is an alphabetical list of all the @@-commands and assorted Emacs Lisp "
"functions.  To make the list easier to use, the commands are listed without "
"their preceding @samp{@@}."
msgstr ""

#. type: unnumbered
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25149 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25150
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variables Index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25153
msgid ""
"This is an alphabetical list of all variables, mainly customization "
"variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: unnumbered
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25157 texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25159
#, no-wrap
msgid "General Index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: texinfo/upstream/texinfo.texi:25159
msgid "@nodedescription A menu covering many topics."
msgstr ""
